*G/TBT/M/75, para. 4.207.

The representative of the United States reiterated previously raised concerns, particularly regarding the potential detrimental effect of India&#39;s amendment on trade.* &nbsp;His delegation remained concerned with the burdensome nature of the amendment which harmed the ability of the US toy industry to access the Indian market. The US questioned the extent to which the amendment unfairly targeted imported toys and stated that India did not offer a credible explanation for departing from the &quot;gold standard&quot; of toy regulatory regimes that allowed toy manufacturers to test to either the ASTM, ISO, or EN toy safety standards in the country of manufacture by a laboratory accredited under the ILAC system. The US requested India to provide detailed information on the safety concerns of imported toys that led to the development of the new standard and further, information pertaining to in-country testing requirements. The US supported India&#39;s goal to replace the existing measure with a new regulation that would apply to all domestic and foreign toy producers, but requested India to notify this new regulation and allow interested parties to comment should it proceed with this replacement.

