COURTESY TRANSLATION

&nbsp;

China &ndash; Draft Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods 

&nbsp;

The delegation of Mexico refers to the Draft Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods published by the General Customs Administration (AGA) of China on November 26, 2019 (hereinafter, &ldquo;Draft Measures&rdquo;).

&nbsp;


	The Draft measures seek to replace Decree 145 on &quot;Administrative Measures for Registration of Foreign Import Food Producers&quot; issued by the General Administration of Quality Supervision, Inspection and Quarantine of China (AQSIQ).


&nbsp;


	These measures set out registration requirements for producers and importers of food and food products, including a recommendation issued by the competent authority in the country of origin, a risk assessment of the products to be imported and the possibility of in-situ visits or inspections at the exporting companies.


&nbsp;

The concerns of the Mexico are based on the following:

&nbsp;


	Definition of the scope:&nbsp; There is a lack of clarity regarding the scope of application of the Draft Measures. It does not establishes categories or a list of products subject to it.


&nbsp;


	Labeling Requirements: The Draft Measures also include labeling requirements for a &quot;minimum sales package&quot; without specifying the scope of the products covered or the meaning of &quot;minimum sales package&quot;.


&nbsp;


	Additional procedures for the competent authorities in the countries of origin: The Draft Measures requires competent authorities of the country of origin to apply an additional certification procedure aiming to assure the compliance with the Chinese regulations.


&nbsp;

This would represent an additional process for the Mexican authorities and for exporters, who would have to certify the products twice. The first procedure to comply with the export requirements contained in the national regulations and the second to comply with the export requirements to China. This procedure would imply onerous workloads for Mexican exporters and authorities, so it could contravene the proportionality principle of Article 2.2 of the WTO Agreement on Technical Barriers to Trade (TBT Agreement) by constituting an unjustified measure that would affect trade.


	Risk Assessment: The Draft Measures require a product-specific risk assessment for the registration of importers. However, it does not include any information on the scientific or technical basis used to determine the proposed evaluation. It is necessary to identify whether the requested risk assessment is based on an international standard.
	Transparency principle: The Draft Measure was not notified to the Members of the WTO TBT Committee, which prevents the effective application of Article 2.9 of the TBT Agreement, specifically on the opportunity for the TBT Committee Members to present comments to the measure and participate in the measures&rsquo; developing process.


Based on the abovementioned, Mexico kindly requests the following to the government of China:


	Clarify the scope of application of the Draft Measures, as well as the labelling requirements covered. If alcoholic beverages are covered by the regulation, Mexico would be interested in holding bilateral discussion with the Chinese authorities to work on a less restrictive procedure for Tequila imports by proposing the acceptance of Tequila&rsquo;s Certificate of Authenticity.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Explain how the proposed measures are effective and proportionate to fulfil China&rsquo;s legitimate objective on food safety.


&nbsp;


	Share the technical and scientific information, as well as the international standards on which these measures are based. Specifically, it is required to know the basis for requesting the proposed risk assessment.


&nbsp;


	Notification of the measure to the WTO TBT Committee so that Members can participate in the public consultation process. On the other hand, the delegation of Mexico would appreciate any information on the expected date for the entry into force, next steps and implementation process.


The delegation of Mexico thanks the delegation of China for its attention to this statement and the requests made therein.

&nbsp;

The EU has been following the development and consolidation of these measures carefully and commented on draft texts before and after they were notified to WTO under G/TBT/N/CHN/1522 and G/SPS/N/CHN/1191. The EU finds it regrettable that Decrees 248 and 249 were published by the General Administration of Customs of the People&#39;s Republic of China (GACC) without consideration of our comments or any reaction.

China and the EU trade high volumes of food products and beverages through tens of thousands enterprises, some big, many small. While the EU shares China&rsquo;s commitment to ensuring the safety and legality of trade, the administration of business registrations should support trade and avoid cumbersome procedures, where possible.

Clear guidance, standardised template forms and realistic transition periods are indispensable in order to minimise disruptions to economic relationships between enterprises on both sides.

Accordingly, the EU urges GACC to:


	develop detailed guidelines, implementing rules and template forms in cooperation with stakeholders and notify these elements through WTO channels for comment;
	clearly define the product categories by their HS numbers that must register under the &#39;registration with recommendation&#39; procedure under Article 7 of Decree 248;
	define the types of operations that must register. It is the EU&#39;s understanding that the exporter of consumer products must register, i.e. the entity that is liable for guaranteeing conformity of the product with food safety standards; it is also our understanding that products shipped in bulk from business to business &ndash; such as dairy powders &ndash; are not required to register;
	provide for realistic and practicable implementation and transition periods of at least 18 months after detailed guidance is available. Enterprises that are already registered and listed by GACC today should be exempt from the immediate implementation of the measures. For example, enterprises approved in 2018 should remain approved for five years;
	ensure that once registrations are approved and registration numbers are allocated, provisions related to labelling under Article 15 be implemented with adequate transition periods. In particular, products with a long shelf life, such as spirits, may be in retail stocks for many months and must be protected by transition periods of at least 36 months.

