COURTESY TRANSLATION

Kingdom of Saudi Arabia &ndash; Amendment to SASO 2885/2018 technical regulation on washing machines and energy efficiency

&nbsp;

The delegation of Mexico refers to the communication circulated on January 14, 2020 by the Saudi Standards, Metrology and Quality Organization (SASO), informing significant technical amendments to the SASO 2885/2018 technical regulation on energy efficiency for washing machines. Although the government of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia circulated this communication, the amendments have not been notified to the Members of the WTO TBT Committee.

&nbsp;

The concerns of Mexico are the following:


	Changes related to the temperature in the drum: The amendments refer to the temperature measurement requirement for washing machines that do not include a water heater feature.


&nbsp;

The requirement, in general terms, implies that top-loading washing machines that do not have a water heater feature have to be evaluated at higher temperatures (60&deg; C -40&deg; C). The previous temperature measurement requirement was between 60&deg; C to 15&deg; C.

&nbsp;


	By amending the temperature measurement requirement as proposed, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia would be limiting, indirectly, its imports of washing machines by only allowing front-loading washing machines to meet these new parameters. In the absence of the scientific and technical basis behind these amendments, this measure would constitute an unjustified barrier to trade based on the provisions established in article 2.2 of the WTO Agreement on Technical Barriers to Trade (TBT Agreement).


&nbsp;


	Co-existence of energy efficiency classes: In order to address the differences between existing systems, countries have allowed the co-existence of technologies in their technical regulations. However, with the proposed amendment, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia would be preventing the coexistence that was normally allowed. It is important to know if there is any specific concern of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia that justifies the prohibition of the coexistence of the different systems or, if the amendments are due to a concern that has arisen during the previous regulatory scheme.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;


	Use of International Standards: Based on article 2.4 of the TBT Agreement, it is necessary to know the international basis behind the proposed amendments, or if applicable, the reasons for any divergence made from it.


&nbsp;


	Transparency Principle: The content of the measure makes it compatible with the definition of &ldquo;technical regulation&rdquo; of the TBT Agreement, so it must comply with the transparency provisions stated in article 2.9 of this Agreement. However, there is no notification related to the last amendment made to the SASO 2885/2018 technical regulation.


&nbsp;


	Entry into force: The Mexican industry informed us that the proposed amendment became effective immediately the day after its publication. If so, and considering that the measure does not constitute an emergency technical regulation, the measure could contravene the provisions of article 2.12 of the TBT Agreement and article 5.2 of the Ministerial Declaration adopted by WTO Members on November 20, 2001 regarding the reasonable interval for the entry into force of technical regulations.


Based on the abovementioned, the delegation of Mexico requests the delegation of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia the following:


	Share the technical and scientific basis that justifies the amendments made on the technical regulation SASO 2885/2018 on washing machines and energy efficiency. In the absence of this information, it is requested to reconsider the amendments taking into account that this measure would constitute an unjustified barrier to trade for the products covered.
	Indicate how this measure is align with international standard.
	Notify the measure based on the transparency obligations contained in the TBT Agreement, as well as provide a reasonable period for its entry into force.


The delegation of Mexico thanks the delegation of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia for their attention to this statement and the requests made therein.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Reino de Arabia Saudita - Enmienda al Reglamento T&eacute;cnico SASO 2885/2018 relacionada con lavadoras y eficiencia energ&eacute;tica

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera lo expresado durante el procedimeinto escrito de este&nbsp;Comit&eacute; llevado a cabo en mayo de 2020, sobre la enmienda al reglamento t&eacute;cnico SASO 2885/2018 del Reino de Arabia Saudita. Agradecemos la apertura de la delegaci&oacute;n de Arabia Saudita para proveer informaci&oacute;n sobre esta medida. Sin embargo, las preocupaciones de M&eacute;xico persisten. Las modificaciones al reglamento t&eacute;cnico de SASO imposibilitar&iacute;an la entrada de lavadoras provenientes de M&eacute;xico, lo que podr&iacute;a contravenir el art&iacute;culo 2.2 del Acuerdo OTC de la OMC al representar una medida que restringe al comercio m&aacute;s de lo necesario a la luz del objetivo que persigue.

&nbsp;

A trav&eacute;s de los comentarios transmitidos anteriormente, M&eacute;xico ha cuestionado la proporcionalidad de la medida con relaci&oacute;n al objetivo perseguido. As&iacute; mismo, se ha solicitado que se comparta informaci&oacute;n que permita entender la problem&aacute;tica que propici&oacute; estos ajustes en la regulaci&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

Adicionalmente, la delegaci&oacute;n mexicana reitera la importancia de dar cumplimiento a los compromisos del Acuerdo OTC en materia de transparencia y sustento en normas internacionales.

&nbsp;

Por lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico solicita a la delegaci&oacute;n de Arabia Saudita lo siguiente:

&nbsp;


	Reconsiderar las modificaciones realizadas al reglamento t&eacute;cnico SASO 2885/2018 para evitar barreras injustificadas al comercio;
	Establecer un canal de comunicaci&oacute;n a trav&eacute;s del cual se puedan exponer las preocupaciones del Gobierno de M&eacute;xico;
	Tenemos conocimiento de que se ha otorgado un periodo de gracia que concluye a finales de noviembre de este a&ntilde;o, &iquest;Cu&aacute;les son las expectativas cuando este periodo de gracia culmine?
	Se solicita informaci&oacute;n sobre el estatus de la notificaci&oacute;n de las enmiendas al reglamento t&eacute;cnico a este Comit&eacute;, as&iacute; como permitir un plazo de consulta p&uacute;blica para la emisi&oacute;n de comentarios sobre la medida en cuesti&oacute;n.


&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece la delegaci&oacute;n del Reino de Arabia Saudita por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

	Canada welcomes this opportunity to discuss its concerns on transparency and clarity regarding G/TBT/N/BGD/3, Hazardous Waste (E-Waste) Management Rules, 2019, which Bangladesh notified to WTO Members on February 20, 2020.


&nbsp;


	In its initial comments submitted on April 16, 2020, as well as on two previous occasions in March 2020, Canada requested that Bangladesh extend the comment period of the notification to 60 days and share the full regulatory text of the Management Rules with Canada. Canada would like to take this opportunity to reiterate these requests and ask Bangladesh to share the full regulatory text other WTO Members and stakeholders to foster greater transparency and clarity on the Management Rules. &nbsp;


&nbsp;


	While Canada trusts that Bangladesh is currently giving due consideration to our comments, we nevertheless use this opportunity to highlight some of our questions and concerns on the Management Rules.


&nbsp;


	Canadian stakeholders have voiced various concerns regarding the lack of clarity of the proposed Management Rules and are requesting that Bangladesh provide additional supportive documentation on the new measure, which they consider as challenging to understand. Additional explanations would be beneficial on the scope of the measure, the selection criteria for the substances identified as &ldquo;hazardous&rdquo;, and the rationale behind the determination of the threshold limits for each of them. &nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada would further request that Bangladesh clarify specific aspects the Management Rules, notably regarding the method and rationale Bangladesh has followed to elaborate the grouping in Schedule 3. Some of these groups contain a broad range of substances, with each their own level of risks and hazard profiles. Some of these may not require to be grouped under the same threshold limit, or be included in Schedule 3 altogether. This includes, for example, the broad group of &ldquo;Mineral Wool&rdquo; or the joint entry for &ldquo;Nickel and Cadmium/Cadmium oxide/ Cadmium Sulphide&rdquo;. We also understand that some substances serve a specific purpose in the design of electronic products, like the flame retardant Tetrabromobisphenol-A, and would like know more on the rationale for their inclusion in Schedule 3. Canada would also appreciate additional information on how Bangladesh will be approaching enforcement of the Management Rules.


&nbsp;


	In light of the various request for greater engagement on this issue, Canada asks that Bangladesh delays the adoption and entry into force of the Management Rules, and takes the time necessary to provide greater transparency so that all affected parties may assess the potential impacts of the measure and provide substantial comments for Bangladesh&rsquo;s consideration in avoiding unnecessary impacts on trade.


&nbsp;


	Canada would also strongly encourage Bangladesh to give due consideration to any additional inputs or scientific evidence provided by stakeholder to ensure that the Management Rules are not more trade restrictive than necessary to meet the objectives of Bangladesh, specifically with regards to the categories and threshold limits of Schedule 3. This would help ensuring that the new measure does not generate unnecessary complexity and uncertainty in global trade.


&nbsp;


	Canada thanks Bangladesh for its future engagement on this STC, and look forward to received written answers on our letter of comments sent on April 16, 2020.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

On 20 February 2020, Bangladesh notified its Hazardous Waste (E-Waste) Management Rules, 2009 (G/TBT/N/BGD/3) to the WTO. Following notification, on 10 March 2020, Russia sent comments to the Bangladesh&rsquo;s TBT enquiry point.

We thank Bangladesh for the opportunity to comment on its draft Rules. Although Russia believes that our comments are being considered and will be replied soon, we would like to underline some of our concerns.

Specifically, Schedule 3 of the draft Rules establishes a list of hazardous substances. No explanation is provided as to what criteria were used to identify substances as hazardous. Schedule 3 also set threshold limit for each hazardous substance. Within 5 years from the enactment of the draft Rules, specified hazardous substances in electrical and electronic products shall be below threshold limits provided in the Schedule.

The Russian Federation notes that Schedule 3 lists nickel metal as hazardous and includes it in a group with cadmium and two cadmium compounds. This grouping appears to be incorrect due to significant differences between nickel and cadmium in terms of substance identity and hazard profile. The maximum nickel and cadmium content for products that fall within the scope of the draft Rules is set at an extremely low level, namely at 0,1%. For this reason, the Russian Federation requests that nickel should be considered separately and moreover should be entirely removed from the draft Rules.

Russia emphasizes that the draft Rules seems to effectively prevent the use of high-quality nickel-containing stainless steel, which enhance the longevity of electronic equipment. Also stainless steel plays an important role in medical equipment, as the material not only withstand corrosion, but also easy to clean and to disinfect. The measure at issue may affect the use of nickel coatings, which play an essential role in many electrical and electronic products.

The Russian Federation stresses that nickel is not classified by the International Agency for Research on Cancer and any major WTO member as carcinogen, mutagen and reprotoxine (CMR) of categories 1A or 1B substance and it has no other hazardous properties that would suggest singling it out in a short list of hazardous substances that should be avoided in electrical and electronic products. In case of adoption of the draft legislation, we note the contradiction between the measure itself, and its&rsquo; declared objective to tackle the problem of e-waste. Without the use of nickel-containing stainless steel, the life span of many of the products that fall within the scope of the Bangladesh&rsquo;s regulation would be shorter, thus aggravating e-waste problem.

Russia considers the measure to be more trade restrictive than necessary, as the inclusion of nickel in the list is not scientifically justified and has the potential to create unnecessary obstacles to international trade in a wide range of nickel-containing products. Besides, according to the notification (G/TBT/N/BGD/3), the period for comments in respect of the draft Rules was limited to March 2020, but the measure was notified in February 20, 2020. Russia kindly reminds Bangladesh that in accordance with the article 2.9 of the Agreement on TBT the Members should provide the period for comments on draft technical regulations not less than 60 days.

Bearing all these factors in mind, the Russian Federation suggests that Bangladesh:

1)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;make a revision of the document to remove nickel from the Schedule 3 as it is not classified as CMR 1A or 1B;

2)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;provide members of the WTO with the criteria used to include substances into the Schedule 3 in order to improve transparency and justification of the list;

3)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;use appropriate methodologies approved at the international level when forming the list of hazardous wastes, including a proper hazard and feasibility assessment;

4)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;conduct socio-economic assessment for the consequences of the draft measure for Bangladesh&rsquo;s and global economy;

5)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;take into consideration legitimate objectives when elaborating technical regulations, as well as conditions that require scientific justification under the Article 2.2 of the Agreement on TBT;

6)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;follow the transparency provisions under the Article 2.9 of the Agreement on TBT;

7)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;respond the Russian comments sent to the Bangladesh&rsquo;s TBT enquiry point in March 2020.

We would like to emphasize that Russia remains open for bilateral consultations with Bangladesh on this issue.

Viet Nam

Draft Circular

Regulations on list of products and goods liable to cause unsafety under the governance of the Ministry of Information and Communications (MIC)
G/TBT/N/VNM/161

New STC #2&nbsp;


Talking Points:


	The United States thanks Vietnam for its recent notification of the draft Circular &ldquo;Regulations on list of products and goods liable to cause unsafety under the governance of the Ministry of Information and Communications (MIC)&rdquo; as G/TBT/N/VNM/161. We understand this circular replaces Circular No. 05/2019/TT-BTTT.


&nbsp;


	The U.S. Information Technology Industry Council (ITIC) provided Viet Nam comments through MIC and the U.S. Inquiry Point on March 23, 2020. We received substantive response on March 17, 2020. ITIC again responded on April 27, 2020. We appreciate the robust discussion of this measure through the Inquiry Points.


&nbsp;


	Our understanding is the draft Circular will go into effect on July 1, 2020, less than two months after Vietnam plans to release the final measure on May 10, 2020. This short transition period does not allow a reasonable interval for stakeholders to come into compliance with the requirements and will likely cause significant delays to international trade of impacted products.


&nbsp;


	We ask Vietnam to implement at least a one-year transition period after the measure is finalized to allow stakeholders to adapt their products and methods of production to the new requirements.


&nbsp;


	We also understand that the draft Circular may invalidate existing Type Approval (TA) and Suppliers&rsquo; Declarations of Conformity (SDoC) certificates. Can Vietnam please confirm our understanding?


&nbsp;


	We would request that Vietnam continue to recognize the validity of existing TA and SDoC certificates until their expiration. Invalidation of existing certificates would be costly and overly burdensome for companies that have already spent time and money on the required existing certifications for Vietnam.


&nbsp;


	Our understanding from U.S. industry is that Vietnam does not currently have any established laboratories qualified to test a number of products listed in the draft Circular.&nbsp; This situation would make compliance with the regulations impossible within the current specified transition period and create a significant testing backlog. When does Vietnam expect a majority of its existing approved laboratories to be qualified to conduct this scope of testing?

India

Draft Chemicals (Management and Safety) Rules 

Not Notified

STC #3

&nbsp;

The United States has repeatedly asked India notify its draft chemicals management and safety rules to the WTO.&nbsp; The first request was in December 20, 2019, and we again requested notification on March 23, 2020. When will India notify this measure?

&nbsp;

On March 6, 2020, the U.S. Inquiry Point submitted comments from the U.S. industry concerning this draft measure. The Indian Inquiry Point has acknowledged these comments.

&nbsp;

We note industry&rsquo;s concerns that India has included in the Draft Rules a list of 750 chemicals, classified as &ldquo;Priority Substances,&rdquo; without any public consultation.

&nbsp;

U.S. industry is also concerned about the potential trade disruptions that may result from the criteria used by the Draft Rules&rsquo; risk management system.&nbsp; It bases the identification of Priority Substances on the mere presence of certain chemicals, rather than on assessments of actual risk of chemical exposure to humans or the environment. &nbsp;We urge India to consider risk-based approaches in identifying and prioritizing chemicals for regulatory action.

&nbsp;

We note that the broad criteria of the Draft Rules&rsquo; proposed risk management system could affect many products already regulated in India under other relevant regulations, such as cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, and agricultural pesticides. Has India considered exemptions for other affected products (e.g. polymers) controlled under other regulations?

&nbsp;

We request that India clarify stakeholder engagement as outlined in Chapters II and III of the Draft Rules. For example, how will the Chemistry Division work with manufacturers to designate a chemical as a &ldquo;Substance for Registration?&rdquo;

&nbsp;

We also ask that India provide further clarification as to how it defines intermediates.&nbsp; Do the same registration and testing requirements apply to intermediates that are restricted in Schedule VI?

&nbsp;

We observe that when additional testing is required to fulfill data requirements for substance registration, the Draft Rules mandate that such testing occur in India. We request India ensure that results of conformity assessment procedures conducted outside of India are also accepted to fulfill these data requirements.

&nbsp;

Industry notes that it needs a grace period of more than six months after final adoption of the Draft Rules in order to adapt products and methods of production to the new requirements. Additionally, due to the broad scope of the Draft Rules, we request that India consider launching its system in phases, working with industry stakeholders to determine a reasonable timeframe for phased implementation.

Statement by Canada

STC 36 &ndash; India &ndash;- Draft Chemicals (Management and Safety) Rules, 2020 (IMS ID 622)

&nbsp;


	Canada understands that the Draft Chemicals Rules are now in their fourth and &ldquo;final&rdquo; iteration, and have only been shared with a select group of stakeholders and business associations so far.


&nbsp;


	This situation creates a major asymmetry between those who have had access to the Draft Chemicals Rules and were able to influence their development, and those who will discover the extent of the Draft Chemicals Rules only once they are published.


&nbsp;


	Canada urges India to notify the draft rules as soon as possible in order to ensure that all interested parties are given sufficient time to review and provide comments, and ensure that sufficient time is allowed after the consultation period for Indian authorities to give due consideration and responses to those comments. Is India in a position to share with the Committee a timeline for the publication and consultation period of the Draft Chemicals Rules?


&nbsp;


	Due to the novelty and complexity of the Draft Chemicals Rules, and their potential to disrupt trade, Canada also supports a long transition period to give stakeholders the time they need to prepare. This will be essential, considering that some stakeholders have been given an unfair advantage by being consulted on four iterations of the Draft Chemicals Rules, while the intricacies of the new rules remain unknown to the rest of the world. Stakeholders will need time, resources and capacity to implement the new rules appropriately, and a phased implementation period could help ease the transition.


&nbsp;


	Canada urges India to make its regulatory process more open, transparent and collaborative, and to take the necessary time to work with all stakeholders and all WTO Members to assist them in understanding the full impact of the Draft Chemicals Rules.

The European Union would like to signal its concerns with regard to the proposed revision to the Guidance on Federal Conformity Assessment Activities (15 CFR Part 287) by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), notified on 4 March 2020. &nbsp;

Specifically, first, the language of the Guidance seems to suggest that agencies may deviate from the NIST guidance on Federal Conformity Assessment Activities in the exercise of their regulatory competences. Could the US clarify if this assumption is correct? If so, could the US further clarify what type of actions NIST may take to ensure coherence on conformity assessment at federal level in case an agency decides to deviate from the NIST guidance on Federal Conformity Assessment Activities?

Secondly, the Guidance removes &ldquo;the responsibility for NIST to collect and disseminate information on Federal, State and private sector conformity assessment activities&rdquo; 

The European Union considers that the use of multiple on-line sources might entail difficulties for domestic and overseas stakeholders in having access to and obtain a comprehensive overview of the different conformity assessment schemes that may exist at Federal, State and private levels in the US. The setting up of a single portal providing information on conformity assessment in the US would overcome these difficulties and better ensure transparency requirements.

Thirdly, the Guidance also removes &ldquo;the standards and conformity assessment related organizational names as examples (&sect;287.4). The inclusion or exclusion of names may be perceived as endorsement or criticism.&rdquo; This deletion concerns in particular the organizations named in &sect;287.4(d) of current 15 CFR Part 287 that include among others &ldquo;the International Organization for Standardization (ISO), the International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), the International Telecommunications Union (ITU) and the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), the World Health Organization (WHO), and the Codex Alimentarius Commission.&rdquo;

In parallel, the notified draft measure refers, among others, to the NIST Special Publication 2000-01 &ldquo;ABC&rsquo;s of Conformity Assessment&rdquo; which acknowledges the role and relevance of the conformity assessment standards developed by ISO and IEC and further states that &ldquo;The combination of geographical reach and a multi-stakeholder development approach creates wide support and use of the CASCO toolbox.&rdquo; (see page 6). The same applies to the NIST Special Publication 2000-02 &ldquo;Conformity Assessment Considerations for Federal Agencies&rdquo;, which repeatedly makes reference to the ISO/IEC conformity assessment standards and encourages agencies to make use of them.

As a consequence, the explanations included in the notified draft measure as regards this change seem to entail a contradiction as, on the one hand, the proposed revision removes names of relevant international standard-setting organizations as their inclusion may be perceived as endorsement and, on the other hand, the proposed revision refers in parallel to other relevant NIST publications which expressly acknowledge and endorse the relevance of these organizations in setting up relevant international conformity assessment standards. The European Union therefore considers that an exemplary reference to ISO and IEC should be kept as relevant international standard-setting organisations in the field of conformity assessment. The same approach should apply to the references to ITU, OECD, WHO, and Codex Alimentarius Commission that are proposed to be removed, given the relevance of these organizations in defining relevant international conformity assessment standards.

Fourthly, when it comes to the acceptance of conformity assessment results, the Guidance introduces further changes to &ldquo;reflect that U.S. access to international markets is achievable through many mechanisms. (&hellip;) The revised language recognizes that other mechanisms (not just recognition) can facilitate acceptance of standards and conformity assessment results to increase market access for U.S. products and services.&rdquo;

However, the notified draft measure does not provide any further explanations on the other mechanisms that could facilitate the acceptance of US standards and conformity assessment results. Can the US further elaborate on these additional missing examples?

Fifthly, in accordance with the Guidance, the new responsibilities of NIST include &ldquo;(f) To the extent that resources are available and upon request by a state government agency, work with that state agency to reduce duplication and complexity in state conformity assessment activities.&rdquo;

The European Union considers that the above language does not sufficiently encourage or promote the mutual recognition of conformity assessment results at State level. To the contrary, it seems to suggest that each state retains broad discretion in not using or recognizing conformity assessment results of Federal agencies or other states. This entails a serious risk of fragmentation and duplication of conformity assessment activities, leading to unnecessary costs and time delays in placing products on the US market. In this regard, could the US explain how it intends to ensure that the selection and use of conformity assessment procedures by a state that does not accept or recognise conformity assessment results from Federal agencies or other states does not constitute an unnecessary duplication in conformity assessment activities?

Preocupaci&oacute;n Comercial Espec&iacute;fica N.&ordm; 8. Uni&oacute;n Europea - No renovaci&oacute;n de la aprobaci&oacute;n de la sustancia activa mancozeb:

&nbsp;

El Ecuador desea manifestar su preocupaci&oacute;n respecto a la notificaci&oacute;n G/TBT/N/EU/712 sobre la no renovaci&oacute;n para el uso de la sustancia activa mancozeb, debido a que en Ecuador esta sustancia es crucial para el manejo de varios cultivos relevantes para las exportaciones del pa&iacute;s y que adem&aacute;s son importantes para los consumidores europeos,&nbsp; como el banano, cacao, br&oacute;coli, pitahaya, pi&ntilde;a y rosas.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Para el Ecuador es esencial que los estudios para la renovaci&oacute;n de sustancias activas se basen en evidencia cient&iacute;fica con resultados concluyentes y no bajo el principio de precauci&oacute;n. &nbsp;Nos referimos al art&iacute;culo 2.2 del Acuerdo OTC de la OMC que establece que &ldquo;los Miembros se asegurar&aacute;n de que no se elaboren, adopten o apliquen reglamentos t&eacute;cnicos que tengan por objeto o efecto crear obst&aacute;culos innecesarios al comercio internacional. A tal fin, los reglamentos t&eacute;cnicos no restringir&aacute;n el comercio m&aacute;s de lo necesario para alcanzar un objetivo leg&iacute;timo, teniendo en cuenta los riesgos que crear&iacute;a no alcanzarlo. (&hellip;) Al evaluar esos riesgos, los elementos que es pertinente tomar en consideraci&oacute;n son, entre otros: la informaci&oacute;n disponible cient&iacute;fica y t&eacute;cnica, la tecnolog&iacute;a de elaboraci&oacute;n conexa o los usos finales a que se destinen los productos&uml;.

&nbsp;

Por ello, Ecuador insta a la UE a tomar en cuenta la informaci&oacute;n cient&iacute;fica pertinente que existe en los organismos especializados internacionales que son reconocidos por la OMC, como es el Codex Alimentarius, que cuenta con informaci&oacute;n relevante sobre esta sustancia, considerando que, de acuerdo al resumen de an&aacute;lisis de riesgo del EFSA, la evaluaci&oacute;n sobre el riesgo al consumidor no ha concluido.

&nbsp;

Conforme al estudio presentado por la UE en el borrador de notificaci&oacute;n, Ecuador verific&oacute; que el an&aacute;lisis fue espec&iacute;ficamente realizado en tomate, papa, trigo y uva. Si bien actualmente esta notificaci&oacute;n &uacute;nicamente hace menci&oacute;n a la comercializaci&oacute;n de esa sustancia en la Uni&oacute;n Europea y en este momento no existe afectaci&oacute;n a los l&iacute;mites m&aacute;ximos de residuos (LMR) del mancozeb, el Ecuador desde ya insta a la UE a revisar su decisi&oacute;n de reducir todos los LMR de mancozeb al l&iacute;mite de detecci&oacute;n. &nbsp;El an&aacute;lisis para la determinaci&oacute;n del LMR de esta sustancia debe ser realizado de acuerdo a cada producto, considerando que la producci&oacute;n agr&iacute;cola var&iacute;a seg&uacute;n la regi&oacute;n y lo que funciona en Europa puede no ser apropiado en otros climas y regiones.

&nbsp;

Adem&aacute;s, para ciertos cultivos en los que se utiliza mancozeb y que actualmente se exportan,&nbsp; pueden no existir alternativas eficientes para controlar las plagas, lo que significar&iacute;a un duro golpe a las exportaciones agr&iacute;colas del pa&iacute;s, actualmente ya afectadas por la condici&oacute;n sanitaria a nivel mundial (COVID-19).

&nbsp;

Por este motivo, el Ecuador se une al llamamiento a la UE para que base sus medidas en estudios concluyentes, que no instaure medidas definitivas sobre la base del principio precautorio y que establezcan per&iacute;odos de transici&oacute;n de al menos 36 meses para el registro de sustancias alternativas, considerando la limitaci&oacute;n de herramientas disponibles para el control de plagas actualmente.

La delegacion de Paraguay quisiera a las delegaciones&nbsp;de Colombia, Brasil, Costa Rica, Estados Unidos y Ecuador&nbsp;por la inclusion de esta preocupaci&oacute;n en agenda. El Mancozeb es una sustancia activa con mas de 50 a&ntilde;os de uso y continua siendo considerada como una herramienta importante para el control de hongos fito patogenos a nivel mundial, sobretodo en programas anti resistencia.&nbsp;

El Mancozeb es un fungicida multisitio, protectante que se utiliza en m&aacute;s de 70 cultivos, como hortalizas, frutales, ornamentales, nueces, cereales, soja, ma&iacute;z, papas, bananas, c&iacute;tricos, uvas, c&eacute;sped. Esta sustancia menciona que controla m&aacute;s de 400 enfermedades solo o en mezclas con fungicidas sist&eacute;micos, de especies pertenecientes a los cuatro grandes grupos de hongos: PHYCOMICETOS &ndash; ASCOMYCETOS &ndash; DEUTEROMYCETOS &ndash; BASIDIOMYCETOS Phytophthora, Botrytis, Alternaria, Michosphaerella, Septoria, Peronospora, Phoma, Pseudoperonospora, Cercospora, Venturia, Plasmopara, Monilia, Antracnosis, Puccinias, Uromyces, Ascochitas, Helmintosporium, Sphaceloma, Uncinula, Diplocarpon. Convirti&eacute;ndose en un fungicida estrat&eacute;gico para el control de las 10 principales enfermedades a nivel mundial, con un uso en las mismas de m&aacute;s del 95%.

Los productores paraguayos utilizan actualmente el Mancozeb para el control de roya asi&aacute;tica que afecta a la soja para hacer frente a la resistencia que puedan presentar esta y que no puede ser enfrentada con otros fungicidas de circulaci&oacute;n actual en el mercado. Al ser un multisitio, el Mancozeb actua en varios &aacute;mbitos dentro del organismo patogeno que ya tiene resistencia formada a otros fungididas. Por ello, al remover este producto de la rotaci&oacute;n, se corre el riesgo no solo de aumentar la aplicaci&oacute;n de otros fungucidas en los ciclos productivos, sino tambi&eacute;n que disminuya la eficacia de estos, lo cual derivar&iacute;a en una afectaci&oacute;n del rendicimiento de la produccion de un cultivo fundamental para la econom&iacute;a paraguaya.

En la actualidad existen muy pocos productos multisitios como el mancozeb, uno de ellos es el chlorotalonil, que tambien es objeto de otra preocupacion comercial pues su utilizaci&oacute;n tambi&eacute;n esta siendo eliminada por la UE. El mancozeb y el cholorotalonil representan cerca del 90% del uso de multisitios en Paraguay.&nbsp;

Adem&aacute;s de los cultivos del soja, se ver&aacute;n afectados los cultivos de ma&iacute;z por esta no renovaci&oacute;n, pues el mancozeb es utilizado en ma&iacute;z para combatir una enfermedad causada por Phaeosphaeria maydis. Esta sustancia es en la actualidad la &uacute;nica alternativa de amplio uso y que tiene efectividad y buena relaci&oacute;n costo/beneficio para los productores.&nbsp;

Como ingrediente activo vigente, Mancozeb ha sido objeto de re-evaluaci&oacute;n por parte de las autoridades reguladoras en varias regiones durante la &uacute;ltima d&eacute;cada. Con base en una amplia base de datos, estudios y directrices reglamentarias en materia de salud y medio ambiente, Mancozeb ha completado con &eacute;xito la re-evaluaci&oacute;n regulatoria y los productos que lo contienen se han vuelto a registrar en muchos pa&iacute;ses, incluidos los de la Uni&oacute;n Europea (UE) en los &uacute;ltimos a&ntilde;os.

Cuando Mancozeb se utiliza de acuerdo con las buenas pr&aacute;cticas agr&iacute;colas (BPA) aprobadas, estos no plantean riesgos para la salud humana o el medio ambiente pues se trata de una sustancia con baja persistencia en el ambiente, baja toxicidad aguda en mam&iacute;feros y poca o ninguna fito-toxicidad.

Cualquier reducci&oacute;n de los l&iacute;mites m&aacute;ximos de residuos (LMR) para Mancozeb podr&aacute; representar un impacto significativo en el comercio mundial, afectando negativamente el acceso a los mercados de los productores de terceros pa&iacute;ses.&nbsp;

En el Paraguay, como pa&iacute;s en desarrollo sin litoral, el impacto podr&iacute;a ponderarse como alto, afectando a los agricultores y disminuyendo o cerrando la posibilidad de acceder al mercado europeo. Cabe destacar que el impacto negativo estar&iacute;a afectando directamente a los productores agr&iacute;colas, as&iacute; como indirectamente a los que intervienen en toda la cadena de producci&oacute;n y exportaci&oacute;n de productos agr&iacute;colas (transporte, log&iacute;stica, entre otros). Asimismo, al no contar en el mercado con un producto alternativo para controlar un fitopat&oacute;geno, la seguridad alimentaria podr&iacute;a verse afectada por la imposibilidad de continuar con su uso si se desea exportar a uno de los principales socios comerciales del Paraguay.

Por lo expuesto, instamos a la UE a:
1.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Establecer medidas respaldadas en evaluaciones de riesgo concluyentes realizadas con base a los criterios establecidos por las normas y recomendaciones internacionales, de conformidad con las disposiciones del Acuerdo MSF y OTC.
2.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Mantener los LMR de la UE existentes para el Mancozeb y no se establezcan nieveles m&aacute;s restrictivos, en particular en tiempos de post-Covid 19, en atenci&oacute;n a la problem&aacute;tica presentada en la comunicaci&oacute;n G/SPS/GEN/1778, G/TBT/GEN/296.
Recordamos que agricultura tiene una importancia fundamental para el Paraguay y el buen funcionamiento del sector agr&iacute;cola es esencial para garantizar la seguridad alimentaria, siendo los productos agr&iacute;colas una fuente principal de alimentos e ingresos nacionales que requieren de la disponibilidad de herramientas como lo es Mancozeb, para enfrentarse de manera efectiva el ataque de plagas

Indonesia thanks Colombia, Brazil, Costa Rica, The United States of America, and Ecuador for raising this issue and share the concern regarding EU regulation on Non-renewal of the approval of the active substance mancozeb.

&nbsp;

Indonesia understand that EU has considered the safety and health aspect of environmental whilst developing the Draft Commission Implementing Regulation concerning the non-renewal of the approval of the active substance mancozeb, in accordance with Regulation (EC) No 1107/2009 of the European Parliament and of the Council concerning the placing of plant protection products on the market, and amending the Annex to Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) No 540/2011.

&nbsp;

However, Indonesia is of the view that this measure is stringent than necessary and has the potential to disrupt trade, specifically for developing countries who export horticulture and other agriculture products to the EU. Indonesia regrets the application of this measure and, therefore, urge the EU to ensure that this measure is not stringent than necessary.

&nbsp;

In addition, Indonesia would also like to remind the EU to use the risk assessment approach that is internationally accepted based on appropriate data and scientific study. In developing this measure, EU only refer to risk assessment conducted by EFSA, which are clearly not in line with the recommendation of FAO Manual on the development and use of FAO and WHO Specifications for Plant Protection Products, FAO Plant Production and Protection Paper No. 173, Rome 2002. In this paper, FAO acknowledges the use of mancozeb as the active substance for plant protection product.

&nbsp;

Indonesia, thus, request the EU to review its policy regarding the non-renewal approval for mancozeb and to allow the use of mancozeb as active substances for plant protection product.

European Union - Non-renewal of the approval of the active substance mancozeb (IMS ID 627)

&nbsp;

Costa Rica desea expresar su apoyo a la preocupaci&oacute;n planteada por Estados Unidos, Paraguay, Brasil, Australia, Indonesia y Colombia en relaci&oacute;n con el proyecto de regulaci&oacute;n de implementaci&oacute;n, notificado por la Uni&oacute;n Europea, por medio del cual no se renovar&iacute;a la aprobaci&oacute;n para el uso de mancozeb.

&nbsp;

Costa Rica reconoce la potestad de los miembros de determinar el nivel adecuado de protecci&oacute;n sanitaria o fitosanitaria necesaria para proteger la salud y la vida de las personas, los animales y las plantas, y de establecer medidas en este sentido; recordamos a su vez que las mismas deben estar basadas en ciencia y no generar barreras innecesarias al comercio, m&aacute;s a&uacute;n en momentos en los que la pandemia todav&iacute;a incide sobre la capacidad de recuperaci&oacute;n econ&oacute;mica de los mercados internacionales.

&nbsp;

Para dimensionar lo esencial que resulta la sustancia mancozeb en la producci&oacute;n agr&iacute;cola en Costa Rica basta con se&ntilde;alar que actualmente se utiliza en m&aacute;s de 20 cultivos, destinados tanto a la exportaci&oacute;n como al consumo interno, por lo que es fundamental para el aseguramiento de la provisi&oacute;n de alimentos. El mancozeb adem&aacute;s se utiliza para combatir plagas de importancia econ&oacute;mica, particularmente en la producci&oacute;n de banano.

&nbsp;

Costa Rica es el segundo exportador de banano del mundo, y el primer pa&iacute;s en obtener la Indicaci&oacute;n Geogr&aacute;fica para este producto. El principal destino de las exportaciones es precisamente la Uni&oacute;n Europea, al que se env&iacute;a m&aacute;s del 50% de la fruta producida en el pa&iacute;s. Actualmente, en Costa Rica no existen productos de protecci&oacute;n fitosanitaria autorizados que se puedan considerar sustitutos o similares al mancozeb, lo que ilustra el impacto que generar&iacute;a la no autorizaci&oacute;n de uso de mancozeb y la posterior reducci&oacute;n de los l&iacute;mites m&aacute;ximos de residuos (LMR) para esta sustancia en banano.

&nbsp;

M&aacute;s all&aacute; de las m&uacute;ltiples reservas que Costa Rica tiene sobre el razonamiento regulatorio de la Uni&oacute;n Europea, el sector bananero costarricense no ha dejado de buscar productos alternativos al mancozeb. Sin embargo, de encontrarse alguna alternativa, esta requerir&aacute; tiempo para concluir las pruebas y los procesos de aprobaci&oacute;n correspondientes. En este sentido, Costa Rica reitera su solicitud a la UE de postergar el proceso de no renovaci&oacute;n y posterior reducci&oacute;n del LMR para mancozeb, de manera que se otorgue un tiempo prudencial a las autoridades sanitarias y fitosanitarias nacionales de responder a los innumerables retos asociados a la pandemia del COVID-19, y disponer de una sustancia alternativa para que los agricultores nacionales puedan mantener la producci&oacute;n de banano y exportarlo en los vol&uacute;menes requeridos por la demanda del mercado de la UE.

Colombia reitera su preocupaci&oacute;n por la medida notificada por la Uni&oacute;n Europea mediante documento G/TBT/N/EU/712 de abril de 2020, relacionada con la no renovaci&oacute;n de la aprobaci&oacute;n de la sustancia activa Mancozeb.

&nbsp;

La UE ha venido adoptando medidas que llevan a la no aprobaci&oacute;n de uso de productos para la protecci&oacute;n de plantas que afecta las exportaciones de Colombia. Las medidas de suspensi&oacute;n o no aprobaci&oacute;n de la comercializaci&oacute;n de numerosas sustancias activas y la posterior reducci&oacute;n de sus LMR al nivel m&iacute;nimo de detecci&oacute;n, se est&aacute;n tomando sin contar con evidencia cient&iacute;fica s&oacute;lida y sin demostrar que efectivamente son las medidas menos restrictivas al comercio para alcanzar un nivel de protecci&oacute;n adecuado.

&nbsp;

Sobre la importancia de esta sustancia de protecci&oacute;n fitosanitaria ya nos hemos referido en oportunidades anteriores. En esta oportunidad quisi&eacute;ramos solicitar la UE aclaraci&oacute;n sobre la relaci&oacute;n de la notificaci&oacute;n 712, sobre Manconzeb y la notificaci&oacute;n G/TBT/N/EU/797 &nbsp;- referida al Reglamento REACH en lo que se refiere a las sustancias carcin&oacute;genas, mut&aacute;genas o t&oacute;xicas para la reproducci&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

Igualmente queremos recordar que, si bien en este y diversos escenarios hemos solicitado a la UE informaci&oacute;n sobre los tiempos de adopci&oacute;n de la norma y sobre la aplicaci&oacute;n de l&iacute;mite m&aacute;ximos de residuos, la UE no ha atendido estas solicitudes.

&nbsp;

Recordamos que el art&iacute;culo 2.12 Acuerdo OTC, establece que los Miembros prever&aacute;n un plazo prudencial entre la publicaci&oacute;n de los reglamentos t&eacute;cnicos y su entrada en vigor, con el fin de dar tiempo a los productores de los Miembros exportadores, y en especial de los pa&iacute;ses en desarrollo, para adaptar sus productos o sus m&eacute;todos de producci&oacute;n a las prescripciones del Miembro importador.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;La informaci&oacute;n de la que se tiene conocimiento indica que la EFSA ha iniciado el procedimiento de revisi&oacute;n de los LMR del Mancozeb. Al respecto preguntamos a la Uni&oacute;n Europea de qu&eacute; manera los socios comerciales interesados podemos participar en este proceso, de qu&eacute; forma ser&aacute;n tenidos en cuenta nuestros comentarios&nbsp; y qu&eacute; plazos tienen previstos para una decisi&oacute;n por parte de EFSA.

&nbsp;

Siendo que el procedimiento que realiza EFSA es diferente al proceso de consulta p&uacute;blica internacional que se debe seguir bajo el Acuerdo OTC, instamos a que la UE notifique las normas correspondientes en una etapa convenientemente temprana y tome en consideraci&oacute;n los comentarios que presenten los miembros, en l&iacute;nea con el Art&iacute;culo 2.9.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Recordamos tambi&eacute;n que en el contexto de la pandemia por Covid-19, Colombia junto con un grupo amplio de pa&iacute;ses, solicit&oacute; a la UE suspender temporalmente los procesos de revisi&oacute;n de las aprobaciones de la comercializaci&oacute;n de sustancias de protecci&oacute;n fitosanitaria, as&iacute; como la entrada en vigor de las normas en esta &aacute;rea. Llamado que tampoco ha sido tomado en consideraci&oacute;n y sobre el cual reiteramos nuestra invitaci&oacute;n a la UE a que nos de una respuesta e iniciemos un di&aacute;logo que nos permita explorar alternativas. Finalmente, Invitamos a la UE a seguir las recomendaciones de las buenas pr&aacute;cticas regulatorias, bajo las cuales las normas deben basarse en informaci&oacute;n clara y objetiva, se promueve el di&aacute;logo abierto con las partes interesadas, la transparencia y la reducci&oacute;n de las distorsiones en el mercado.

El Paraguay se remite a sus declaraciones anteriores, tal como lo han mencionado otros miembros y reitera&nbsp;su preocupaci&oacute;n por la decisi&oacute;n de la UE de basar las medidas en un enfoque basado en peligros, sin un an&aacute;lisis de riesgos adecuado y sin cumplir con principios cient&iacute;ficos; que condujeron a la reducci&oacute;n de &nbsp;LMR as&iacute; como la no renovaci&oacute;n del mancozeb.&nbsp;&nbsp;La perdida del mancozeb como herramienta de proteccion de plantas generar&iacute;a un da&ntilde;o tremendo al sector exportador del Paraguay.&nbsp;

Como bien lo hab&iacute;amos manifestado, de las aproximadamente 350 sustancias que han sido o est&aacute;n siendo objeto de revisi&oacute;n&nbsp; en&nbsp; la&nbsp; actualidad, 116 son utilizadas como herramientas principales o accesorias en el combate de plagas en el Paraguay, un pa&iacute;s con condiciones clim&aacute;ticas, y por ende, presi&oacute;n de plagas, muy distintas a las de la Uni&oacute;n Europea.

Si bien compartimos los objetivos que la UE busca atender con estas pol&iacute;ticas, no compartimos el m&eacute;todo adoptado para la obtenci&oacute;n de estos objetivos, al no estar este basado en evidencia cient&iacute;fica. Instamos a la UE a estudiar alternativas menos restrictivas al comercio y basar sus medidas en evidencia cient&iacute;fica concluyente.

&nbsp;

Colombia reitera su preocupaci&oacute;n por la medida notificada por la Uni&oacute;n Europea mediante documento G/TBT/N/EU/712 de abril de 2020, relacionada con la no renovaci&oacute;n de la aprobaci&oacute;n de la sustancia activa Mancozeb.

