COURTESY TRANSLATION

&nbsp;

Draft Commission Regulation modifying Annex III of Regulation (EC) No. 1925/2006 of the European Parliament and of the Council, regarding botanical species containing hydroxyanthracene derivatives

&nbsp;

The delegation of Mexico presents the concern regarding the Draft Regulation of the Commission modifying Annex III of the Regulation (EC) No. 1925/2006 regarding botanical species containing hydroxyanthracene derivatives, notified to the Members of the WTO TBT Committee through the document G/TBT/N/EU/702. The Government of Mexico sent comments to this Draft Regulation on May 2, 2020 as part as the WTO public consultation process. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

The delegation of Mexico recognizes the interest of the European Union in establishing public health protective measures. However, it is consider that the Draft Regulation could be more trade restrictive than necessary to fulfil the legitimate objective pursued. Given that the Draft Regulation would impose an absolute ban for the use of aloe and its extracts in food and beverage formulas, its implementation would negatively affect the Mexican industry that produces and exports to the European Union a variety of human consumption products with aloin, a natural component intrinsically found in aloe vera.

&nbsp;

It is consider that the Draft Regulation could fail to comply with article 2.2 (proportionality principle), article 2.4 (use of international standards) and article 2.12 (establishment of a reasonable interval for the entry into force of the measure) of the WTO Agreement on Technical Barriers to Trade (TBT Agreement).&nbsp; These considerations are based on the following:


	The proposed amendments are based on a scientific opinion issued by the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) on November 22, 2017 on which the following comments are made:


&nbsp;

EFSA opinion does not differentiate between whole-leaf preparations and aloe vera extracts that may contain minimum levels of hydroxyanthracene derivatives (HAD). EFSA does not include in its risk assessment measures on the potential effects of other types of aloes that include minimal or no levels of HAD for use in food and food preparations. EFSA does not provide advice on a daily intake of aloe that does not give rise to concerns about harmful health effects, beyond improving the bowel function. The EFSA conclusions are based on studies carried out in rats and not in humans and even the conclusions provided by EFSA recognizes that uncertainty persists about the potential effect of aloe vera and its extracts on human health, so it would be inappropriate to adopt an absolute ban to aloe consumption.

&nbsp;


	Neither EFSA&#39;s opinion nor the documents shared through document G/TBT/N/EU/702 indicate whether any international standard was considered as reference for the proposed amendments. However, the CODEX General Standard for Food Additives, CODEX-STAN-1995 (2019), recognizes aloe vera as an additive whose use is allowed under specified conditions for food categories or certain food products.



	The Draft Regulation was not notified as an emergency technical regulation. Therefore, the provisions established on article 2.12 of the TBT Agreement and article 5.2 of the Ministerial Declaration adopted by the WTO Members on November 20, 2011[2], regarding a reasonable interval for the entry into force of technical regulations should not be omitted. &nbsp;


&nbsp;

Based on the abovementioned, Mexico would like to request the following to the European Union:

&nbsp;


	Reconsider the proposed amendment to Part A of Annex III of Regulation (EC) 1925/2006, based on the existing scientific evidence on the use of aloe vera and its extracts in consumer products. The existing scientific evidence do not deduce any threat to the health of the population that merits a ban on these substances.
	Clarify whether the Draft Regulation also applies to aloin intrinsically existing in food products.
	Align the Draft Regulation with the provisions of the CODEX-STAN-1995 (2019) standard, or share the justification by which the European Commission promotes the adoption of a divergent measure with respect to this standard.
	Establishment of a reasonable interval for the entry into force of the Draft Regulation.&nbsp;
	Review and consideration of the comments sent by the Government of Mexico on May 2, 2020 during the public consultation process.


&nbsp;

The delegation of Mexico thanks the delegation of the European Union for attending to this statement and the requests made therein.

&nbsp;

