Uni&oacute;n Europea. Proyecto de Reglamento de la Comisi&oacute;n por el que se modifica el Anexo III del Reglamento (CE) no 1925/2006 del Parlamento Europeo y del Consejo sobre especies bot&aacute;nicas que contienen derivados de hidroxiantraceno

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico hace referencia a la preocupaci&oacute;n sobre el Proyecto de Reglamento de la Comisi&oacute;n por el que se modifica el Anexo III del Reglamento (CE) No. 1925/2006 respecto a las especies bot&aacute;nicas que contienen derivados de hidroxiantraceno, la cual se notific&oacute; a los Miembros del Comit&eacute; OTC bajo la nomenclatura G/TBT/N/EU/702. La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece los comentarios recibidos por parte de la Uni&oacute;n Europa en respuesta a las observaciones emitidas en el proceso de consulta p&uacute;blica.

&nbsp;

Sin perjuicio de las respuestas recibidas, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera las preocupaciones que ha externado en este mismo foro con relaci&oacute;n a la proporcionalidad de la medida y sus posibles impactos en el comercio de productos que contienen o derivan del aloe o alo&iacute;na.

&nbsp;

El Proyecto de Reglamento propuesto por la UE podr&iacute;a resultar en una medida m&aacute;s restrictiva de lo necesario a la luz del objetivo leg&iacute;timo que pretende perseguir, tomando en cuenta que implicar&iacute;a una prohibici&oacute;n absoluta al uso de aloe y sus extractos en las f&oacute;rmulas de los alimentos y bebidas. La implementaci&oacute;n de esta medida impactar&iacute;a negativamente a la industria mexicana que produce y exporta a la Uni&oacute;n Europea una variedad de productos de consumo humano con alo&iacute;na, un componente natural intr&iacute;nsecamente encontrado en el aloe vera.

&nbsp;

Adem&aacute;s de reiterar nuestra preocupaci&oacute;n, solicitamos a la Uni&oacute;n Europea se reconsidere la necesidad de implementar una medida de este tipo, hasta que no se cuente con fundamento cient&iacute;fico suficiente que la sustente.

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece a la delegaci&oacute;n de la Uni&oacute;n Europea por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

	Canada would like to thank New Zealand for its bilateral engagement on this issue and for providing preliminary responses to Canada&rsquo;s comments submitted on February 7 and March 20, 2020 through New Zealand&rsquo;s enquiry point.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada acknowledges New Zealand&rsquo;s legislative requirement to establish a consumer information standard for the disclosure of a regulated food&rsquo;s country or place of origin.&nbsp; We also recognize New Zealand&rsquo;s objective to provide consumers with accurate country of origin information for certain single-ingredient, minimally processed foods. &nbsp;However, Canada is concerned with the proposed requirements for meat and cured pork that require labels to contain information on where the animal was raised.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Given the highly integrated nature of the North American supply chain for meat and livestock, producers and slaughterhouses would need to implement costly segregation and traceability measures throughout the production chain in order to comply with New Zealand&rsquo;s proposed labelling requirements.&nbsp; Canada&rsquo;s experience with similar measures imposed by another WTO Member in the recent past is well-known.&nbsp; In fact, Canada was successful in demonstrating that this measure resulted in the discriminatory treatment of trade from Canada and was in violation of international trade obligations.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada respectfully requests that New Zealand consider using relevant international standards, such as the Codex Standard for the Labelling of Prepackaged Foods (CODEX STAN 1-1985) for its proposed COOL requirements for meat products.&nbsp; This standard states that &ldquo;[w]hen a food undergoes processing in a second country which changes its nature, the country in which the processing is performed shall be considered to be the country of origin for the purposes of labelling&rdquo;.


&nbsp;


	Doing so would meet the objective of providing consumers with accurate and useful information pertaining to the food they purchase without requiring unnecessary restrictive traceability or segregation measures that impact trade.
	&nbsp;
	We remain open and willing to continue our engagement with New Zealand.


&nbsp;

	Canada welcomes this opportunity to discuss a series of 22 individual &ldquo;Quality Control Orders&rdquo; on chemicals and petrochemicals substances notified by India since November 2019. These prospective measures would make mandatory the use of Indian Standards on the notified substances.


&nbsp;


	Canada submitted its initial comments on these prospective measures on March 31, 2020, and would like to seize this opportunity to highlight some of its concerns with regards to the various notifications.


&nbsp;


	Firstly, Canada requests additional explanations from India on the rationale for enforcing mandatory Indian Standards on those chemicals and petrochemicals substances. Canada noted that each of the 22 notifications published by India did not contain any justifications for the new measures, except that &ldquo;the Central Government [&hellip;] is of the opinion that it is necessary or expedient so to do in the public interest&rdquo;.


&nbsp;


	Can India elaborate on the necessity for these mandatory Bureau of India Standards to safeguard public interest, and why international standards or other type of voluntary standards were deemed not sufficient to achieve the same objectives?


&nbsp;


	Secondly, Canada is concerned with India&rsquo;s approach to these notifications, and would request clarifications as to why India notified these new standards individually and without supporting documentation that would clarify the stated objective of the proposals, i.e., to ensure &ldquo;health, safety and environment for prevention of deceptive practices and national safety&rdquo;, and how these new regulatory requirements are not more trade restrictive than necessary to achieve this objective. What process, research, and assessment has India performed on theses substances to arrive at this conclusion? Can India share those documents with WTO Members? What are the risks to health, safety, environment, and &ndash; specifically &ndash; &ldquo;national safety&rdquo;?


&nbsp;


	Thirdly, Canadian stakeholders are concerned that these new measures could seriously undermine the ability of foreign chemical manufacturers to access the Indian market. The new permit requirements will add a significant level of bureaucracy for both industry and Indian regulators, and could reasonably result in significant delays in the import process. Moreover, Canada understands that the certification period will be limited to 1 year, which would accentuate the pressure on the administrative body to review, deliver and/or renew new permits on a constant basis. What steps has India taken to ensure the capacity of its administrative authorities to effectively implement the new measures and deliver the new permits in a timely manner? Has India considered extending the certification period beyond 1 year?


&nbsp;


	Fourthly, could India confirm if it will accept quality control assessments conducted by foreign firms and laboratories? If third party assessment will not be accepted, Canada would request that a rationale be provided on this decision.


&nbsp;


	Finally, Canada would request clarifications on how the new mandatory standards will be enforced for: 1) domestic manufacturers, and 2) foreign producers and importers. Specifically, Canada would seek clarifications on the steps taken by India to ensure that the new measures will not discriminate foreign products to the benefit of domestic manufacturers.


&nbsp;


	As all 22 notifications provided limited information on the process and rationale for the new measure, Canada looks forward to further engaging with India on this issue in order to ensure transparency, avoid unnecessary impacts to trade, and better understand the objectives sought by India. Canada would also request that the entry into force of the new mandatory standards be delayed until further clarifications have been provided to all interested parties. Canada also look forward to receiving written answers from India on the letter of comments it sent on March 31, 2020.


&nbsp;

Item 35&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;India &ndash; Quality Control Orders for Chemical and Petrochemical Substances (IMS ID 630)

The Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu would like to thank India for responding positively to our concerns at the previous meeting on the draft Orders regarding the mandatory requirements for a number of chemical and petrochemical substances, which basically involve the bearing of the Standard Mark under a license from the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS). Among the notified measures, we are particular concerned about the one for Terephthalic Acid (notified by G/TBT/N/IND/124 on 31 March 2020). We would like to seek elaboration and clarification from India on issues that are still of our concern, which mainly involve the obligations stated in Articles 5.1.1&nbsp;and 5.1.2&nbsp;of the TBT Agreement that conformity assessment procedures shall be non-discriminatory and shall not be more strict or be applied more strictly than is necessary.&nbsp;

1. As the new mandatory certification unavoidably increases trade cost and uncertainty, we are still concerned about the necessity of this draft order. India indicated in its notification (G/TBT/N/IND/124) and response that the objective of the new Order is to &ldquo;keeping in view the health, safety and environment for prevention of deceptive practices and national safety.&rdquo; We would like to ask India to elaborate on the reasons for changing the voluntary certification to mandatory, in particular information on the risk assessment that supported such a change.

2. It concerns us the likely discrimination against imported products and/or foreign manufacturers of this draft Order. According to Scheme-I of Schedule-II of India&rsquo;s BIS Conformity Assessment Regulations 2018, some of the requirements will significantly increases the burden and costs for foreign products vis-&agrave;-vis their domestic Indian counterparts.&nbsp;


	Firstly, the certification requires product testing and annual factory visit. The certification/license is valid only for 1 year for initial application, and 1-5 years for renewals. We are concerned over the very short validity of the certification that will make foreign manufactures more difficult if not impossible to apply for a renewal only 12 months after the issuance date. We seek India&rsquo;s clarification of the rationale behind this design and how this requirement can be applied to both domestic and foreign applicants on a non-discriminatory basis taking into account the practical difficulties faced exclusively by foreign manufacturers.
	Secondly, according to India&rsquo;s response, BIS does not recognize nor accept third-party testing reports from laboratories outside India. As this creates significantly higher burden for manufacturers located outside India, we seek India&rsquo;s reasons for this non-recognition/non-acceptance. To this end, we once again call for India to consider providing opportunities for testing laboratories and relevant inspection bodies from other WTO Members to participate in the intended conformity assessment process with the view of reducing the impact.&nbsp;
	Thirdly, we are highly concerned with the requirement for annual &ldquo;on-site&rdquo; factory visit/inspectioin, a costly requirement to fulfill especially for manufacturers located outside India. As the BIS Conformity Assessment Regulations 2018 stipulates that &ldquo;the need for carrying out the inspection shall be decided keeping in view the risk associated with&rdquo;, we seek further information from India on the risk assessment underpinning the new Order&rsquo;s decision that there is a need to carry out annual factory visit/inspection no matter it applies to initial application or renewal proceedings. In particular, we call for India to share with Members the risk factors and other reasons that prevent India from adopting less trade restrictive alternative approaches.


Another point we would like to bring to India&rsquo;s attention is the impact of Covid-19 on the implementation of the BIS Conformity Assessment Regulations 2018 especially for the requirement of factory visit or on-site inspection. As more countries are expected to impose travel bans or restrictions to control the spread of COVID-19, this pandemic will amplify the challenges for foreign producers to comply with the said Regulations. We would like to suggest that the Indian authority consider temporary measures to authorize qualified inspection bodies located in the exporting Members (e.g. inspection bodies accredited under ILAC MRA framework) to facilitate factory inspections in lieu of Indian delegation to mitigate the problem. The India inspecting agency can still participate remotely through video conference and/or supplementary document reviews.

In summary, we urge India to inform other Members how non-discrimination principle is observed and the necessity of this requirement in accordance of Article 5.1.1&nbsp;and 5.1.2&nbsp;of the TBT Agreement. In particular, we call for India to share with Members the risk factors and other reasons that prevent India from adopting less trade restrictive alternative approaches.

We would be grateful if the above-mentioned comments could be taken into account and replied to before adoption of the notified draft.

Statement by Canada

STC 27 &ndash; India &ndash;Quality Control Orders for Chemical and Petrochemical Substances (IMS ID 630)


	In previous Committee meetings, Canada raised concerns over the approach taken by India to make mandatory the use of Indian Standards on a series of chemical substances. Canada remains of the view that the notification process followed by India to inform interested parties of its &ldquo;Quality Control Orders&rdquo; (QCO) is problematic and could benefit from adjustments to improve the sharing of information with interested Parties.


&nbsp;


	For example, the 1-page document notified by India to explain its QCO could provide a link to additional background information on the science and deliberations having lead to the propose measure. The current scarcity of readily-available supporting documentation in the notifications makes it difficult to assess and comment on the measures.


&nbsp;


	At the February 2021 TBT Committee, India stated having carried a review of the existing standard and conducted consultations with various leading manufacturers to ensure to reflect new testing methods. Canada would suggest that such information &ndash; when available &ndash; be added to the TBT notifications as basic background information for interested Parties seeking to understand the objective and rationale behind the Indian measure.


&nbsp;


	To further facilitate stakeholders&rsquo; access to any related information on the BIS, Canada would also suggest that India includes an hyperlink to the latest official version of the standards in the notifications. India shared such a link with WTO Members at the last TBT Committee meeting.


&nbsp;


	If interested parties and stakeholders are not provided somewhat intuitive access to the relevant information that led to the regulatory proposal, they can hardly provide substantive comments and information that could inform India&rsquo;s decision-making process.


&nbsp;


	Canada recognizes and strongly supports Members&rsquo; right to regulate. However, Members must ensure that such actions follow internationally agreed-upon rules and processes to ensure a level playing field for all interested Parties. It is in this view that Canada is sharing suggestions on potential adjustments to the future Quality Control Orders notifications of India. We believe these improvements could have a genuine positive impact for stakeholders, and go a long way to support the transparency obligations of the TBT Agreement.

	Canada wishes to reiterate the importance Canada places on toy regulations that protect the health and safety of children, and supports WTO Members that put in place measures to achieve this objective.


&nbsp;


	While India did notify, on 7 February 2020, its new Toys (Quality Control) Order, 2020, Canada was very disappointed that India provided WTO Members with only 15 days, instead of the normal minimum 60 days, to review the new measure, consult with relevant stakeholders, and provide comments.


&nbsp;


	It remains unclear to Canada what rationale India used to allow only two weeks for Members and stakeholders to review the new Order, on the grounds that it was addressing concerns &ldquo;related to national security, health, safety, environment, and deceptive trade practices.&rdquo;


&nbsp;


	While Canada requested an extension of the comment period to India &ndash; which was not fulfilled, Canada still provided comments to India on 6 April 2020, and looks forward to receiving a response from India.


&nbsp;


	Canada kindly requests that India provide specific examples of the precise (i) legitimate objectives supporting and (ii) rationale for the following aspects of the measures:

		extensive in-country testing and marking that require separate applications for products in the same family, special packaging and/or labeling, and the segregation of products for the Indian market; and
		the use of technical regulations based on domestic standards, even though India&rsquo;s domestic standards are aligned with international toy safety standards.




&nbsp;


	Can India please specify how consistency with non-discriminatory treatment for imported toys relative to domestically produced toys is maintained under the new requirements, according to which foreign manufacturers must:

		nominate and retain an in-country Indian representative;
		obtain a performance bank guarantee; and
		provide an indemnity bond?




&nbsp;


	Canada looks forward to further engagement from India on this important issue.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

China appreciates that EU has notified the regulation and supports EU&#39;s efforts to provide customers with transparent and objective information on the energy efficiency of electronic displays. However, China notes that the implementation time of the regulation has brought unnecessary trade barriers to manufacturers.&nbsp;

According to the Regulation (EU) 2019/2013, it shall be implemented from 1 March 2021. However, point&nbsp;1(a) of Article 3 shall be implemented from 1 November 2020. Since the new label regulation EU 2019/2013 has not been comprehensively implemented, detailed test methods and cases have not been given, in the time period between November 1, 2020 and March 1, 2021, manufacturers can only use the test standards and methods specified in the former labeling regulation EU 1062/2010 for testing, while determine the new energy efficiency level according to the calculation formula specified in EU 2019/2013. The switching time for the old and new regulations is not reasonable, which will result in significant discrepancies between the energy efficiency level on the energy efficiency label attached by the manufacturer and the actual energy efficiency level of the product. It will mislead consumers.

Therefore, China suggests the implementation time of point 1(a) of Article 3 be consistent with the implementation time of the EU 2019/2013 regulation, the relevant test guidelines should be issued as soon as possible and the test standards as well as test cases should be given in due course.

Comunidad Econ&oacute;mica Euroasi&aacute;tica. Reglamento T&eacute;cnico EAEU 047/2018 sobre seguridad de las bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas.

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico se refiere al Reglamento T&eacute;cnico de la Comunidad Econ&oacute;mica Euroasi&aacute;tica EAEU 047/2018 sobre seguridad de las bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas, adoptado el 20 de noviembre de 2018 y que entrar&aacute; en vigor en 2021.

Durante la reuni&oacute;n de febrero de 2020 del Comit&eacute; OTC, M&eacute;xico sostuvo una reuni&oacute;n bilateral con la delegaci&oacute;n de la Federaci&oacute;n de Rusia a fin de compartir las preocupaciones sobre este reglamento t&eacute;cnico, espec&iacute;ficamente sobre su &aacute;mbito de aplicaci&oacute;n sobre bebidas como el Tequila. De igual forma, se han tenido intercambios de informaci&oacute;n con la Comisi&oacute;n Econ&oacute;mica Euroasi&aacute;tica. Sin embargo, no se ha podido confirmar si aplica o no al Tequila.

En caso de que el reglamento t&eacute;cnico&nbsp; aplique al Tequila, M&eacute;xico estar&iacute;a interesado en empezar un di&aacute;logo bilateral con las autoridades competentes en Rusia a fin de poner a su consideraci&oacute;n el an&aacute;lisis del reglamento t&eacute;cnico&nbsp; mexicano aplicable a ese producto: la Norma Oficial Mexicana NOM-006-SCFI-2012, Bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas - Tequila - Especificaciones&nbsp;para lo siguiente:

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Definici&oacute;n del Tequila: El reglamento t&eacute;cnico de la Comunidad Econ&oacute;mica Euroasi&aacute;tica solo considera dos categor&iacute;as de productos y en ninguna de esas categor&iacute;as se podr&iacute;a incluir el Tequila por sus caracter&iacute;sticas f&iacute;sico-qu&iacute;micas.&nbsp;

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Consideraci&oacute;n de las caracter&iacute;sticas f&iacute;sico-qu&iacute;micas: En el caso de que el reglamento t&eacute;cnico aplique a Tequila y considerando que Rusia reconoce al Tequila como Denominaci&oacute;n de Origen desde 2012, ser&iacute;a importante considerar las caracter&iacute;sticas f&iacute;sico-qu&iacute;micas del Tequila establecidas en la NOM-006-SCFI-2012, sobre todo en lo relativo a los niveles de metanol.&nbsp; Los niveles de metanol propuestos en el reglamento t&eacute;cnico de la Comunidad Econ&oacute;mica Euroasi&aacute;tica est&aacute;n por debajo de los niveles establecidos para el Tequila en la normatividad mexicana aplicable.&nbsp;

Durante la reuni&oacute;n de febrero de 2020 del Comit&eacute; OTC, la delegaci&oacute;n de la Federaci&oacute;n de Rusia comparti&oacute; que el reglamento t&eacute;cnico estaba por el momento planeado para entrar en vigor en 2021, pero esto podr&iacute;a cambiar y podr&iacute;a estar sujeto a comentarios y ajustes. Con base en lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico solicita respetuosamente a la delegaci&oacute;n de la Federaci&oacute;n de Rusia lo siguiente:

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Confirmaci&oacute;n oficial sobre el alcance de la medida. Solicitamos que se confirme formalmente si el reglamento t&eacute;cnico EAEU 047/2018 aplica o no al Tequila. En caso de no poder responder, solicitamos que se nos indique una persona con la cual establecer comunicaci&oacute;n dentro de su autoridad competente para que de respuesta a esta solicitud.

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Informaci&oacute;n de seguimiento. La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece que se nos proporcione informaci&oacute;n actualizada sobre la fecha de entrada en vigor del reglamento t&eacute;cnico EAEU 047/2018, as&iacute; como sobre los pr&oacute;ximos pasos y el proceso de implementaci&oacute;n.

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece la delegaci&oacute;n de la Federaci&oacute;n de Rusia por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

COURTESY TRANSLATION

&nbsp;

Eurasian Economic Community - Technical Regulation EAEU 047/2018 on safety of alcoholic beverages

The delegation of Mexico refers to the Eurasian Economic Community Technical Regulation EAEU 047/2018 on safety of alcoholic beverages, adopted on November 20, 2018 and which will enter into force in 2021.

During the February 2020 TBT Committee meeting, Mexico held a bilateral meeting with the delegation of the Russian Federation in order to share the concerns on this technical regulation, specifically regarding its scope of application on alcoholic beverages such as Tequila. Likewise, there have been some exchanges of information with the Eurasian Economic Commission. However, it has not been confirmed whether it applies to Tequila.&nbsp;

In case that the technical regulation applies to Tequila, Mexico would be interested in holding a bilateral discussion with the competent authorities in Russia in order to submit for its consideration the review of the Mexican regulation applicable to Tequila (NOM-006-SCFI-2012, Alcoholic Beverages &ndash; Tequila &ndash; Specifications)&nbsp;for the following:


	Definition of Tequila: The regulation proposed by the Eurasian Economic Community only considers two product categories. Tequila could not be included in any of those categories due to its physical-chemical characteristics.
	Consideration of the physical-chemical characteristics: If the technical regulation applies to Tequila and taking into account that Tequila has an Appellation of Origin recognized by Russia since 2012, it would be important to give consideration to Tequila&rsquo;s physical-chemicals characteristics established in the Mexican technical regulation NOM-006, especially on methanol levels. The methanol levels proposed by the Eurasian Economic Community technical regulation are below those established for Tequila in the applicable Mexican regulation.&nbsp;


During the February 2020 TBT Committee meeting, the delegation of the Russian Federation shared that the technical regulation had a planned entry into force in 2021, but this could be changed and it could be yet subject to comments and adjustments. Based on the abovementioned, Mexico would like to kindly request to the Government of Russia the following:

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Official confirmation on the scope of the measure. We request to provide official confirmation whether the technical regulation EAEU 047/2018 applies to Tequila. In case of not being able to respond to this request, we would appreciate to have a contact point within the competent authority to respond to the pertinent queries.&nbsp;

&middot;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Follow up information. The delegation of Mexico would appreciate any updated information on the date of entry into force of the technical regulation, as well as any information regarding next steps and implementation process.

&nbsp;The delegation of Mexico thanks the delegation of the Russian Federation for its attention to this statement and the requests made therein.

&nbsp;

Myanmar - Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre Importaci&oacute;n de Licor

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico hace referencia al Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre importaciones de licor comunicado por el Gobierno de Myanmar a la Alianza de Importadores de Vinos y Licores de Asia y el Pac&iacute;fico el 20 de junio de 2019.

El Gobierno de M&eacute;xico envi&oacute; la comunicaci&oacute;n oficial 523/01/058/20.IX.2019 con fecha 20 de septiembre de 2019&nbsp;al Departamento de Normas y Calidad Nacional de Myanmar&nbsp; y celebr&oacute; una reuni&oacute;n bilateral durante la reuni&oacute;n del Comit&eacute; OTC de noviembre de 2019.

La principal preocupaci&oacute;n del Gobierno de M&eacute;xico se relaciona con el requisito de 12 a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento contenido en el Proyecto de Procedimientos. Este requisito implica que no se permitir&aacute; la importaci&oacute;n de bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas con menos de 12 a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento en el territorio de Myanmar.

Este requisito representar&iacute;a una barrera para las importaciones de Tequila, ya que ninguna de las clases de Tequila que se producen bajo el reglamento t&eacute;cnico correspondiente (la Norma Oficial Mexicana NOM-006-SCFI-2012 Bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas-Tequila-Especificaciones) podr&iacute;a cumplir con tal requisito.

Tambi&eacute;n es importante mencionar que, si este requisito est&aacute; relacionado de alguna manera con la calidad de las bebidas objeto de la medida, en el caso del Tequila, la calidad no est&aacute; estrictamente relacionada con los a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento. La comunicaci&oacute;n oficial enviada por el Gobierno de M&eacute;xico en septiembre de 2019 contiene mayor detalle al respecto.

Adicionalmente, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico quisiera reiterar su preocupaci&oacute;n por el incumplimiento de Myanmar sobre los compromisos de transparencia contenidos ??en el art&iacute;culo 2.9 del Acuerdo sobre Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio de la OMC (Acuerdo OTC). El Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre Importaci&oacute;n de Licor establece especificaciones de car&aacute;cter obligatorio que deben cumplirse para que los productos se importen a ese pa&iacute;s, por lo que constituye un &ldquo;reglamento t&eacute;cnico&rdquo; en los t&eacute;rminos del Acuerdo OTC y debe notificarse a los Miembros del Comit&eacute; OTC en virtud de lo dispuesto en el art&iacute;culo 2.9 del Acuerdo OTC.

Con base en lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera amablemente las siguientes solicitudes a la delegaci&oacute;n de Myanmar:


	Se solicita respuesta a la comunicaci&oacute;n 523/01/058/20.IX.2019 o, en su defecto, informaci&oacute;n de seguimiento sobre la atenci&oacute;n que se est&aacute; dando a esa comunicaci&oacute;n.
	Se solicita una aclaraci&oacute;n sobre el &aacute;mbito de aplicaci&oacute;n de la medida, espec&iacute;ficamente si aplica a las importaciones de Tequila o si se limita a algunas categor&iacute;as de bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas o licores.
	Se solicita que se realice la notificaci&oacute;n del Proyecto de Procedimientos a los Miembros del Comit&eacute; OTC de la OMC, as&iacute; como que se proporcione informaci&oacute;n sobre la entrada en vigor, los pr&oacute;ximos pasos y su proceso de implementaci&oacute;n.


La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece la delegaci&oacute;n de Myanmar por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

&nbsp;

COURTESY TRANSLATION

&nbsp;

Myanmar &ndash; Draft Procedures on Importation of Liquor

&nbsp;

Mexico refers to the Draft Procedures on Importation of Liquor communicated by the Government of Myanmar to the Asia Pacific Importers of Wine and Spirits Alliance on June 20, 2019.

&nbsp;

The Government of Mexico sent the official communication 523/01/058/20.IX.2019 to Myanmar&rsquo;s National Standards and Quality Department on September 20, 2019&nbsp;and held a bilateral meeting during the November 2019 TBT Committee.

&nbsp;

The main concern of the Government of Mexico relates to the 12-year aging requirement contained in the Draft Procedures. This requirement implies that alcoholic beverages with less of 12 years of aging would not be allowed for importation in Myanmar. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

This requirement would represent a barrier for Tequila imports, since no class of Tequila produced under the applicable technical regulation (Mexican Technical Regulation NOM-006-SCFI-2012 Bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas-Tequila-Especificaciones) could comply with such requirement.

&nbsp;

It is also important to mention that if aging requirement is somehow related to the quality of the alcoholic beverages covered by the measure, in the specific case of Tequila, the quality is not strictly related to the years of aging. The official communication sent by the Government of Mexico on September 2019, contains detailed information on this matter.

&nbsp;

Additionally, the delegation of Mexico would like to reiterate its concern over the no-fulfillment of the transparency commitments stated in article 2.9 of the WTO Agreement on Technical Barriers to Trade (TBT Agreement). The Draft Procedures on Importation of Liquor establishes mandatory specifications that must be met in order for products to be imported into that country, and therefore constitutes a &ldquo;technical regulation&rdquo; under the terms of the TBT Agreement and must be notified to the Members of the TBT Committee as such.

&nbsp;

Based on the abovementioned, Mexico kindly reiterates the following requests to the Government of Myanmar:

&nbsp;


	Provide a response to communication 523/01/058/20.IX.2019 or otherwise, any follow-up information on the attention given to that communication.
	Provide clarification on the scope of the measure, specifically if it applies to Tequila imports or if it is limited to some categories of alcoholic beverages or liquors.
	To notify the Draft Procedures to the Members of the WTO TBT Committee, as well as information on the expected date of entry into force, next steps and implementations process.


The delegation of Mexico thanks the delegation of Myanmar for attending to this statement and the requests made therein.

&nbsp;

Myanmar - Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre Importaci&oacute;n de Licor

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico hace referencia al Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre importaci&oacute;n de licor comunicado por el Gobierno de Myanmar a la Alianza de Importadores de Vinos y Licores de Asia y el Pac&iacute;fico el 20 de junio de 2019. El Gobierno de M&eacute;xico envi&oacute; una comunicaci&oacute;n el 20 de septiembre de 2019 (n&uacute;mero 523/01/058/20.IX.2019), la cual conten&iacute;a las preocupaciones sobre esta medida y no ha sido respondida.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera su preocupaci&oacute;n sobre el requisito de 12 a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento contenido en el Proyecto de Procedimientos. Este requisito implicar&iacute;a que aquellos productos con menos de 12 a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento no puedan ser importados al territorio de Myanmar. En el caso de M&eacute;xico, el requisito de a&ntilde;ejamiento impedir&iacute;a la entrada del Tequila a ese mercado, ya que ninguna de las clases de Tequila que se producen bajo el reglamento t&eacute;cnico correspondiente (la Norma Oficial Mexicana NOM-006-SCFI-2012 Bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas-Tequila-Especificaciones) podr&iacute;a cumplir con tal requisito.

&nbsp;

Tambi&eacute;n es importante mencionar que, si este requisito se est&aacute; relacionando de alguna manera con la calidad de los productos, en el caso del Tequila la calidad no est&aacute; estrictamente relacionada con los a&ntilde;os de a&ntilde;ejamiento. La comunicaci&oacute;n oficial enviada por el Gobierno de M&eacute;xico en septiembre de 2019 contiene mayor detalle al respecto.

&nbsp;

Adicionalmente, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera su preocupaci&oacute;n por el incumplimiento de Myanmar sobre los compromisos de transparencia contenidos ??en el art&iacute;culo 2.9 del Acuerdo sobre Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio de la OMC (Acuerdo OTC). El Proyecto de Procedimientos sobre Importaci&oacute;n de Licor establece especificaciones de car&aacute;cter obligatorio que deben cumplirse para que los productos se importen a ese pa&iacute;s, por lo que constituye un &ldquo;reglamento t&eacute;cnico&rdquo; en los t&eacute;rminos de dicho Acuerdo.

&nbsp;

Con base en lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico reitera amablemente las siguientes solicitudes a la delegaci&oacute;n de Myanmar:

&nbsp;


	Se solicita respuesta a la comunicaci&oacute;n 523/01/058/20.IX.2019 o, en su defecto, informaci&oacute;n de seguimiento sobre la atenci&oacute;n que se est&aacute; dando a esa comunicaci&oacute;n.
	Se solicita una aclaraci&oacute;n sobre el &aacute;mbito de aplicaci&oacute;n de la medida, espec&iacute;ficamente si aplica a las importaciones de Tequila o si se limita a algunas categor&iacute;as de bebidas alcoh&oacute;licas o licores.
	Se solicita que se realice la notificaci&oacute;n del Proyecto de Procedimientos a los Miembros del Comit&eacute; OTC de la OMC, as&iacute; como que se proporcione informaci&oacute;n sobre la entrada en vigor, los pr&oacute;ximos pasos y su proceso de implementaci&oacute;n.


&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece la delegaci&oacute;n de Myanmar por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

&nbsp;

STC No.16&nbsp;&nbsp;

Chile &ndash; Technical specifications for the design of energy efficiency labels for washing machines&nbsp;

Korea would like to make some comments regarding this regulation as follows.&nbsp;

Regarding the regulation on washing machine energy-efficiency label in Chile, Korea received official comments from the Chile TBT Enquiry point in July and October 2020. However, Korea has not yet received a clear response from Chile regarding our requests, so Korea requests again the Chilean authorities to review the following concerns.

According to Number 9 of Table A (Resoluci&oacute;n Exenta N&deg;70 de fecha 30.12.2014 -Ministerio de Energ&iacute;a), the tolerance of energy consumption is &plusmn; 11 % and the tolerance of water consumption is &plusmn; 4 %.

If negative tolerance is applied, it might result failure in follow-up testing even though the product has better energy efficiency. Therefore, in Europe where majority of countries including Korea introduced and Peru, neighboring country of Chile, the tolerance of energy consumption and water consumption is less than +10 %.

Thus, Korea requests Chile for modifying the tolerance of energy consumption and water consumption on the energy efficiency label to less than +10 %.

The Republic of India is strongly concerned about EU&rsquo;s Directive (EU) 2018/851 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 30 May 2018 (Amendment to 2008 WFD) which adversely affects the Indian exporters to the EU. India would appreciate if the EU could respond to this concern in writing.

The Scheme&nbsp;

The revised EU Waste Framework Directive obliges suppliers to provide, starting from 5 January 2021, details of articles placed in the EU, to ECHA (European Chemical Authority). This Directive seeks information on substances of very high concern from the Candidate List that is maintained with REACH (Registration, Evaluation, Authorisation, and Restriction of Chemicals) which are present in the supplied articles. Furthermore, while this information can be supplied directly by the manufacturers based in Europe, the same is not possible for the manufacturers from outside of the EU, who have to perforce engage an intermediary (i.e. O.R.) to do provide the information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;Negative impact

This measure bears the following effects on the exporters of articles to the EU;


	It increases the administrative burden on the business operators and the industry.
	It imposes additional costs.
	Its negative impact on the MSMEs will be detrimental.


For non-EU members, it increases additional barriers.


	As per the Directive, the responsibility for fulfilling the requirements of ECHA in line with REACH&nbsp;lies with the importers established in the European Union. Since manufacturers or exporters cannot directly provide the information to ECHA, they will be forced to contract a representative established in the European Union to undertake the required registration process. This requirement is applicable to all manufacturers, exporters, and distributors, regardless of the quantity of article placed in the EU. &nbsp;This will add to the increase in costs and also act as a barrier to trade for non-EU countries, particularly because:

		Suppliers who place less than 1- tonne quantity of the article annually are also required to engage a representative, which they were previously not required to do if the article contained SVHCs,&nbsp;&nbsp;
		Distributors etc. also have to comply with this obligation, which creates duplicity of notification requirements, since the manufacturer may already have provided the relevant information to the ECHA.




&nbsp;WTO Agreement Violation&nbsp;

Under the Transparency and Notification obligations of the TBT Agreement, Members are required to notify all new measures. It is observed that no notification has been made by the EU or the EU Members to the WTO before they transpose and implement this legislation.

&nbsp;Action Sought from EU

Given the above, India would like to request the EU&nbsp;to;


	Notify this measure to WTO&nbsp;
	Defer implementation&nbsp;of this measure until the comments are received from the members.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Concern:&nbsp;New obligations (with cost implications) for Indian exporters to meet the extended database requirements for SCIP under Waste Framework Directive.

&nbsp;

The Republic of India has been raising this STC in the last two TBT meetings.&nbsp;

The &nbsp;EU has given a response in the last TBT meeting held in February 2021. We thank the EU for the detailed reply; however, the concerns raised by India are still not addressed.

&nbsp;

Hence, we reiterate the statement made in the February 2021 TBT meeting and request the EU to respond to the issues of concern not addressed so far and which are;

&nbsp;

Non-EU companies have no provision of an Only Representative (for its compliance); thus, they have to share the product details with the buyers &ndash; there is an issue of sharing intellectual property / Know-how sharing, which will act as a barrier to trade through implicitly. The non-EU companies treated unfairly.&nbsp; They have to depend on the EU buyers as they will have no choice to do the activities by themselves EU buyers may require unnecessary testing and declarations in situations where it may clearly be a non-SVHC product/article. EU-buyers may insist on certain specific testing facilities to be used and would have undue cost implications on the non-EU suppliers. Since major industries are impacted because of the Covid pandemic hence, the deadline must be extended for the industry to comply with these new requirements/ special provisions for compliance

Japan shares concerns about the Technical Regulation for Restriction of Hazardous Substances in Electrical and Electronic Equipment (EEE).

&nbsp;

Japan understands that the regulation was promulgated on July 9, 2021. At the past WTO/TBT committee meetings, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia stated that it would make the conformity assessment procedures reasonable in response to comments from many countries. However, the international standard for type approval ISO/IEC 17067, which many countries consider problematic, was adopted for granting certificates of conformity under the promulgated regulation.

&nbsp;

In addition, if the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia government will amend the conformity assessment procedure, there is the possibility that imports will be suspended because companies do not have enough preparation time by the enforcement date that is&nbsp; assumed to be January 2022. Also, in this situation where details of the conformity assessment procedures have not yet been presented, it is impossible to complete the certification procedures by the date of enforcement for manufacturers importing many kinds of products.

&nbsp;

Japan requests that a sufficient transitional period be given for obtaining certification before the date of enforcement and requests that this technical regulation be harmonized with international standards and practices, that the above mentioned concerns be dispelled, and that the implementation of these regulations be &nbsp;&nbsp;promptly clarified, so that they will not create unnecessary obstacle to international trade.

