3. India&#39;s STC statement against China for the TBT Committee Meeting of 24-26 Feb 2021

&nbsp;

The Chinese Ministry of Ecology and Environment (MEE) (erstwhile Ministry of Environment Protection or MEP) issued its Order 12 (MEE 12). &nbsp;This order came into force on January 01, 2021, and replaced the earlier order MEP Order 7. &nbsp;

The MEE Order 12 was first notified to the WTO on September 02, 2019, and was followed up with an implementation guideline that was also notified to the WTO on September 04, 2020.

China maintains a list of &quot;Inventory of Existing Chemical Substances Produced or Imported in China (IECSC).&quot; &nbsp;India is concerned about the limited number of items included in this list.&nbsp; This list does not contain many chemicals that China regularly imports. &nbsp;The Chemicals not listed under IECSC require registration. &nbsp;New substances become eligible for IECSC 5 years after first approval (as per MEE 12).

However, in practice, the process of adding new chemicals to the IECSC even after the completion of five years is prolonged. The regulatory and administrative system for chemicals in China thus adds unnecessary delays and costs to the exporters.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;Concerns:

When adding new substances meeting PB, PT, or BT criteria to IECSC, the MEE will also specify allowed uses in IECSC. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Should these substances be put to other uses, they will be subjected to new use management rules and require notification.&nbsp; Despite being on the list already, a substance has to undergo the same procedure as a new substance only because it is put to a different use.&nbsp; &nbsp;This is burdensome.

&nbsp;

Once the substance has completed the due procedure for being included in the IECSC list, the new use addition procedure may be simplified. This issue becomes all the more important because adding more chemicals to the list of IECSC is already prolonged. This is also evident from the fact that during the last ten years, only 573 new chemicals are added to the list of IECSC. (The IECSC was officially provided in 2013 by the Ministry of Ecology and Environment. It contained 45,612 chemicals. This was then subsequently updated with additions in 2016 (31 substances), 2018 (45 substances), January 2019 (28 substances), January 2020 (47 substances), May 2020 (156 substances), October 2020 (28 substances), and December 2020 (238 substances).

&nbsp;

In view of this, China is requested to consider simplifying the process for adding new uses of existing substances in the list of IECSC.

&nbsp;

b)&nbsp; Under MEE 12, notified new substance would be added to IECSC 5 years from its approval date. However, the process does not appear to be an automated one. Once a new substance is eligible to be included in the IECSC, the Chinese regulatory body, in consultation with appropriate technical committees, considers whether such substance should be included in the IECSC.

&nbsp;

Further, there is no clarification on how much time it may take for a product to be included in the list of IECSC. In view of this, China is requested to make the process of adding new substances to the list of IECSC systematic, time-bound, and transparent.

&nbsp;

c) Thirdly, there is no clarity about whether the new substances that have completed five years as per MEP 7 will be automatically included in the IECSC under MEE 12. If not, what further procedural obligations (and why) are expected before these substances are included in the list.&nbsp;

Article 2.2 of the TBT Agreement lays down certain objectives deemed &quot;legitimate&quot; for regulatory purposes. The TBT Agreement acknowledges members&#39; right to fulfill these objectives by regulating through technical regulations; however, the Agreement also emphasizes that such regulations shall not be implemented with a protectionist intention.&nbsp; Technical regulations are not be formulated and implemented to make more trade-restrictive than necessary to achieve a policy goal.

In the case of this Chinese measure, new substances become eligible for addition onto IECSC five years from the date of the first commencement of manufacturing or importation (as per MEP7). However, in practice, China is very slow in adding new chemicals to the IECSC even after the completion of five years. The regulatory and administrative system for chemicals in China adds unnecessary delays and costs to the exporter. Further, there is no clarity on these aspects under MEE 12 also.

In view of the above, China is requested to make the process of adding new substances to the list of IECSC systematic, time-bound, and transparent.

***

India is deeply concerned about the UAE&rsquo;s G- Mark requirement for all children&rsquo;s toys exported to the UAE.&nbsp; And it can be issued only by the agencies authorised by the GCC Standardisation Organisation.

This requirement of G-mark makes Indian products uncompetitive in the UAE as no agency is authorised to issue G-mark in India. To get G-mark certified products, Indian exporters have to send the entire consignment to the place where it can be G-mark certified.&nbsp; As per the extant regulation, the G-mark needs to be obtained for each and every toy. This process involves additional procedural requirements; it is also cost-intensive and makes the Indian product uncompetitive when placed in the UAE market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Further, during the conformity assessment, the G-mark Notified Bodies (NBs) frequently request physical samples of all products in a group, not only of the representative item. Despite the latest GSO guidance specifying test reports are required only for one representative item from a product group, a physical inspection of all items in a product group is burdensome, costly and inconsistent with Article 5.1.2 of the TBT Agreement.

As per Article 5.1.2 of the TBT Agreement, Members are obligated to ensure that CAPs are not prepared, adopted or applied with a view to or with the effect of creating unnecessary obstacles to international trade. CAPs shall not be stricter or applied more strictly than is necessary to give the importing Member adequate confidence that products conform with the applicable technical regulations or standards, taking account of the risks non-conformity would create.

Hence the UAE requested to consider that when the G mark is obtained for each and every toy, then the physical sampling should not be insisted by the Notified Bodies for all the products in the group. Such insistence is trade-restrictive and renders high costs and difficulties. Besides, it is also inconsistent with 5.1.2 of the TBT Agreement.&nbsp;

Chile agradece la oportunidad de referirse en este Comit&eacute; a la notificaci&oacute;n realizada por la Uni&oacute;n Europea al Comit&eacute; de Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio de la OMC &nbsp;bajo signatura G/TBT/N/EU/790, el 25 de marzo de 2021, relativa a la no renovaci&oacute;n de la aprobaci&oacute;n de la sustancia activa del plaguicida Phosmet bajo la Regulaci&oacute;n (UE) No. 1107/2009 y a las acciones que probablemente&nbsp; ser&aacute;n&nbsp; notificadas al Comit&eacute; de Medidas Sanitarias y Fitosanitarias de&nbsp; la OMC, relativas a la disminuci&oacute;n o eliminaci&oacute;n definitiva de los L&iacute;mites M&aacute;ximos de Residuos (LMRs) de esta sustancia en alimentos.

&nbsp;

La Asociaci&oacute;n de Exportadores de Frutas de Chile&nbsp; es una entidad gremial, privada y sin fines de lucro, que re&uacute;ne a m&aacute;s de 350 empresas productoras-exportadoras&nbsp; de&nbsp; fruta&nbsp; fresca en sus diferentes programas, canalizando alrededor del 90 % de las exportaciones de fruta fresca de Chile y representando un 63,1% de la superficie plantada con frutales a nivel nacional.

Para su actividad el producto Phosmet es una herramienta insustituible para la implementaci&oacute;n de &nbsp;programas y estrategias fitosanitarias, &nbsp;desde hace muchos a&ntilde;os, &nbsp;para &nbsp;el control de un amplio espectro de control de plagas de importancia productivas, tales como:&nbsp; Lobesia botrana, Pseudococcus spp., y Drosophila suzukii, entre otras.

&nbsp;

Adicionalmente, el Phosmet posee un conjunto de caracter&iacute;sticas t&eacute;cnicas, asociadas a un muy buen efecto de control de plagas y&nbsp; per&iacute;odo &nbsp;de protecci&oacute;n de los cultivos, convenientes per&iacute;odos de carencias o seguridad, amplias tolerancias, unido a ello a una muy buena relaci&oacute;n costo/beneficio.

&nbsp;

Una reducci&oacute;n de los L&iacute;mites M&aacute;ximos de Residuos actuales de Phosmet en diferentes frutas en los pa&iacute;ses de la Uni&oacute;n Europea, causar&iacute;a un serio problema a nuestros productores y empresas exportadoras, dada la inexistencia de alternativas que incluyan todas las fortalezas antes descritas para este insecticida.

&nbsp;

Hemos recibido informaci&oacute;n de la empresa Gowan Company, fabricante de este producto, qui&eacute;nes presentaron un conjunto de datos del registro en enero de 2016, en el marco del proceso de renovaci&oacute;n de sustancias activas (AIR) de la Uni&oacute;n Europea con la intenci&oacute;n de lograr una renovaci&oacute;n de la inclusi&oacute;n de este insecticida en el Anexo I de la Directiva 91/414/CEE.

&nbsp;

El Estado miembro informante, Espa&ntilde;a, present&oacute; un informe de evaluaci&oacute;n en diciembre de 2017 con una recomendaci&oacute;n para su renovaci&oacute;n en el Anexo I. &nbsp;En junio de 2019 y febrero de 2020, las reuniones de revisi&oacute;n por pares de expertos de la EFSA concluyeron que el Phosmet no cuenta con sustitutos eficientes, no es persistente en el medio ambiente y no tiene problemas de alteraci&oacute;n endocrina.

&nbsp;

Sin embargo, la EFSA decidi&oacute; reducir los criterios de valoraci&oacute;n de sus efectos en mam&iacute;feros y ecotoxicol&oacute;gicos entre 10 y 20 veces, con lo que la mol&eacute;cula no habr&iacute;a tenido una revisi&oacute;n adecuada de los aspectos de toxicidad y ecotoxicolog&iacute;a.

&nbsp;

Chile estima que la eliminaci&oacute;n de los LMRs y no su reducci&oacute;n razonable, ocasionar&aacute;n serios problemas a la fruticultura nacional e internacional, incentivando el uso de productos menos eficientes que repercutir&aacute;n en producciones menores y en consecuencia mayores aplicaciones de productos sustitutos, con el&nbsp; consiguiente mayor riesgo de sobrepasar los niveles permitidos, entre otras consideraciones.

En resumen, solicitamos a la Comisi&oacute;n Europea tener en consideraci&oacute;n estos antecedentes expuestos para una adecuada evaluaci&oacute;n del L&iacute;mites M&aacute;ximos de Residuos de Phosmet en los productos y subproductos que nuestro pa&iacute;s y otros exportan hacia dicha &aacute;rea econ&oacute;mica.

Gracias se&ntilde;or Presidente.

The Korean government appreciates this opportunity to provide our comments regarding Chile&rsquo;s &ldquo;Efficiency Analysis and/or Test Protocol for Electrical Products: Washer-Dryers (PE No. 1/06-1/2:2020)&rdquo;, notified to the WTO on 24 July 2020 as G/TBT/N/CHL/525, and in effect from January 2022 (PE No. 1/06/3:2020).

We sincerely respect the vigorous efforts of the Chilean government in protecting the environment and providing relevant information to consumers. Furthermore, Korean companies are fully committed to complying with the regulations.

However, we would like to present the following comments reflecting concerns raised by Korean companies regarding the energy efficiency regulations for &lsquo;washer-dryers.&rsquo;

If the energy consumption tolerance for &lsquo;washer-dryers&rsquo; follows the example of tolerance for &lsquo;washing machines&rsquo; and is established including minus tolerance (with Energy consumption tolerance of +/- 11%, and Water consumption tolerance of +/- 4%), we are concerned that washer-dryers with even better performance in energy efficiency may still fail to pass post-market surveillance test. To prevent this, most countries, including the EU, have set the tolerance of energy consumption and water consumption for washer-dryers to &lsquo;less than +10%.&rsquo;

Therefore, we respectfully request the Chilean government to consider establishing both the energy consumption tolerance and water consumption tolerance to &lsquo;less than +10%&rsquo; for energy efficiency labels on washer-dryers. &nbsp;

We would like to recall that, on 12 October 2020, the Korean government submitted similar Comments regarding the efficiency regulation for &lsquo;washing machines&rsquo; to the Chilean WTO TBT Enquiry Point, but there has been no reply from the competent authorities of Chile yet. So we hope to receive your response as soon as possible on this matter as well.
&nbsp;

STC #17 Egypt &ndash; New Halal Certification Requirements

&nbsp;


	Canada joins the United States, the European Union and other intervening Members to signal its concerns with Egypt&rsquo;s new halal certification requirements for all imported food and beverage products which came into effect as of October 1, 2021.
	&nbsp;



	While Canada supports Egypt&rsquo;s objective to ensure that Egyptian consumers are confident that they are buying and consuming Halal-certified products, Canada is deeply concerned that Egypt failed to notify the WTO TBT Committee of this new measure to provide Members and stakeholders the required period of at least 60 days to review the measure and provide comments.


&nbsp;


	In addition, as per Egypt&rsquo;s obligations under the TBT Agreement, a reasonable amount of time, understood as a period of not less than six (6) months, should be provided between the publication of a regulation and its entry into force to allow time for industry and Members to review and implement the new requirements. This has obviously not been the case with this measure.


&nbsp;


	Canada is also concerned that the lack of specifics and/or documentation regarding the implementation of the measure has created an unpredictable trading environment for our exporters. Based on the very limited information gathered to date, the halal certification requirements imposed by Egypt could be overly burdensome, unjustified, costly and more trade restrictive than necessary to achieve Egypt&rsquo;s objective.
	&nbsp;
	We strongly encourage Egypt to have open and transparent discussions with trading partners to share information, further clarify the requirements under this new measure and consider the impact it may have on trade prior to its implementation.


&nbsp;


	Specifically, we request that Egypt notify this measure to the WTO, pursuant to Egypt&rsquo;s transparency obligations, and provide trading partners and stakeholders with the opportunity to review and comment. Until then, we respectfully request that Egypt suspend the implementation of the measure

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico hace referencia a la Norma T&eacute;cnica 84 &ndash; Registro Sanitario de productos repelentes de uso externo en humanos y productos repelentes de uso en ambientes, la cual fue publicada, en su versi&oacute;n final, el 28 de agosto del a&ntilde;o en curso en el sitio web del Departamento de Regulaci&oacute;n y Control de Productos Farmac&eacute;uticos y Afines de la Direcci&oacute;n General de Regulaci&oacute;n, Vigilancia y Control de la Salud del Ministerio de Salud P&uacute;blica y Asistencia Social.

&nbsp;

Las preocupaciones sobre esta regulaci&oacute;n est&aacute;n principalmente relacionadas a los principios de proporcionalidad, uso de normas internacionales y transparencia previstos en el Acuerdo sobre Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio.

&nbsp;

La Norma T&eacute;cnica referida solicita estudios de toxicidad para la obtenci&oacute;n del registro sanitario de repelentes de uso externo en humanos, sin embargo, no aclara si ser&aacute; posible utilizar estudios ya existentes o estudios toxicol&oacute;gicos extrapolados (bridging data) sobre las formulaciones o si es necesario que se realicen estudios nuevos para cada formulaci&oacute;n aunque ya exista antecedente sobre la misma. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

La flexibilidad de permitir el uso de &nbsp;informaci&oacute;n ya existente, favorece &nbsp;la reducci&oacute;n del n&uacute;mero de estudios a realizarse y, por ende, de costos para los importadores en el proceso de solicitud del registro, limitando la necesidad de realizar estudios de toxicidad &uacute;nicamente cuando hubiera un nuevo ingrediente en la f&oacute;rmula.

&nbsp;

Asi mismo, con relaci&oacute;n a los estudios de toxicidad contenidos en el art&iacute;culo 5.1 de la Norma T&eacute;cnica, notamos que estos estudios requeridos por Guatemala para la obtenci&oacute;n del registro sanitario, podr&iacute;an incluir pruebas en animales, procesos que tienen una tendencia a ser prohibidos a nivel mundial. &nbsp;&nbsp;En este sentido, agradeceremos a la delegaci&oacute;n de Guatemala nos comparta informaci&oacute;n sobre la evidencia t&eacute;cnica y cient&iacute;fica en la cual basaron la versi&oacute;n final de la Norma T&eacute;cnica a fin de tener mayores insumos que justifiquen las bases de esta regulaci&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

Adicionalmente, contrario a las prescripciones de transparencia acordadas por este Comit&eacute;, la Norma T&eacute;cnica no fue notificada a los Miembros&nbsp; este Comit&eacute;, ni puesta a consulta p&uacute;blica internacional, lo que impidi&oacute; que el Gobierno de M&eacute;xico presentar&aacute; los comentarios y preocupaciones sobre este reglamento t&eacute;cnico por los canales oficiales.

&nbsp;

M&eacute;xico ha tenido &nbsp;un acercamiento con Guatemala en el que ha expuesto de manera detallada las preocupaciones a sus encargados de la agenda de Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio, &nbsp;por medio de correo electr&oacute;nico y en una reuni&oacute;n bilateral virtual, en julio y septiembre, respectivamente, del a&ntilde;o en curso, las cuales no fueron consideradas en la versi&oacute;n final de la Norma T&eacute;cnica en cuesti&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

Derivado de lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico solicita a la delegaci&oacute;n de Guatemala sus buenos oficios para pausar los procedimientos normativos relacionados a la Norma T&eacute;cnica 84 con la finalidad de permitir la consulta p&uacute;blica este reglamento t&eacute;cnico, as&iacute; como facilitar y promover un di&aacute;logo p&uacute;blico-privado donde pueda participar tanto la industria como el Gobierno de M&eacute;xico para exponer nuestras preocupaciones.

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece la oportunidad para presentar esta declaraci&oacute;n y la atenci&oacute;n a las solicitudes en ella expuestas.

Korea respects and strongly supports France&#39;s efforts to introduce &quot;Prohibition of Mineral Oils on Packaging&quot; as a part of &quot;Law No. 2020-105: Anti-Waste Law for a Circular Economy&quot; (the &quot;AGEC Law&quot;) to protect the environment and facilitate recycling procedures. Furthermore, Korean companies are fully committed to complying with the regulations of France.

Korean companies understand that the use of mineral oil in printing inks on packaging materials will be banned from January 2022, but there is not enough detailed information about the regulation, which makes it difficult for them to be in compliance. Therefore, we would like to present the following inquiries and requests.

First, we request that France specify the regulated substance list with the chemical substance name and CAS No., and specify the detailed range of packaging materials to be regulated, such as ink, box, plastic bag, etc., for clarification of the range of regulated substances.

Second, we request a grace period of one year after the announcement to enforce the regulation. This is because for finished product manufacturers, a regulatory response period of more than one year is required to investigate the use of regulated substances by ink manufacturers and printing companies, as well as to make improvement and ship the necessary substances.&nbsp;

For this reason, we request that a grace period of 12 months or longer be applied after France provides the essential information for regulatory compliance.
&nbsp;

Chile agradece la oportunidad de referirse en este Comit&eacute; al proyecto legislativo de la Secretar&iacute;a General de Pesca del Gobierno de Espa&ntilde;a, que pretende modificar el Anexo IV del Real Decreto 1521 del 1 de agosto de 1984 de la Secretar&iacute;a de Pesca de Espa&ntilde;a referido a las denominaciones comerciales nacionales admitidas en dicho pa&iacute;s y las denominaciones de alimentos conservados y preparados aplicables a productos de la pesca y la acuicultura.

&nbsp;

Seg&uacute;n la legislaci&oacute;n vigente espa&ntilde;ola, se denomina comercialmente &ldquo;Mejill&oacute;n&rdquo; a toda la familia de la especie Mytilus. En el proyecto de reforma, se propone la denominaci&oacute;n comercial &ldquo;Mejill&oacute;n&rdquo; a las especies Mytilus Edulis y Mytilus Galloprovincialis, denomin&aacute;ndose exclusivamente a la especie Mytilus Chilensis como &ldquo;Mejill&oacute;n Chileno&rdquo; o &ldquo;Chorito&rdquo;.

&nbsp;

Las evidencias cient&iacute;ficas muestran que las especies europeas (Mytilus galloprovincialis y Mytilus edulis) comparten el mismo g&eacute;nero con la especie end&eacute;mica Mytilus chilensis. La familia Mytilidae es un grupo diverso de bivalvos marinos con una amplia distribuci&oacute;n antitropical. En Chile existen al menos tres especies del g&eacute;nero Mytilus presentes en el sur del pa&iacute;s. El estudio &ldquo;Caracterizaci&oacute;n gen&eacute;tica y distribuci&oacute;n espacial del g&eacute;nero Mytilus en Chile realizado por la Universidad Austral de Chile, trabaj&oacute; en diferentes especies del g&eacute;nero Mytilus utilizando marcadores moleculares. De los resultados de este estudio se puede apreciar que las tres especies Mytilus edulis, Mytilus galloprovincialis y Mytilus chilensis, est&aacute;n muy ligadas taxon&oacute;micamente (estructura f&iacute;sica descriptiva), como filogen&eacute;ticamente (evolutiva &ndash;molecular), por lo tanto, en esta revisi&oacute;n se ha demostrado con antecedentes bibliogr&aacute;ficos robustos, as&iacute; como tambi&eacute;n las plataformas digitales taxon&oacute;micas especializadas en registros de especies marinas (World Register of Marine SpeciesWORMS), que las tres especies del g&eacute;nero Mytilus, comparten muchas caracter&iacute;sticas estructurales, sexuales, fisiol&oacute;gicas y moleculares que permiten ser clasificadas sin duda alguna bajo el mismo g&eacute;nero taxon&oacute;mico, por lo tanto, cualquier separaci&oacute;n del grupo por sobre el nivel que los agrupa (g&eacute;nero), puede considerarse discriminatorio al no estar ajustado a las evidencias cient&iacute;ficas expuestas.

&nbsp;

Chile ha consultado al Punto de Contacto Oficial del Acuerdo OTC/OMC de Espa&ntilde;a sobre la fecha de notificaci&oacute;n al Comit&eacute; de esta propuesta de cambio regulatorio, en septiembre de 2019, a efectos de poder efectuar los comentarios pertinentes, sin obtener respuesta. Tambi&eacute;n, por la v&iacute;a diplom&aacute;tica se insisti&oacute; durante el a&ntilde;o 2020 y 2021. La respuesta remitida fue que el proyecto de modificaci&oacute;n del real decreto se encontraba en discusi&oacute;n interna, por lo que podr&iacute;an producirse cambios al mismo, que deber&aacute;n ser en su caso remitidos a la Comisi&oacute;n Europea y que por ello a&uacute;n no han procedido a la remisi&oacute;n del proyecto normativo a la OMC.

&nbsp;

Para esta delegaci&oacute;n, es importante destacar que, en la actividad industrial y comercial en Chile, el cultivo del Mejill&oacute;n es la segunda actividad de la acuicultura despu&eacute;s del salm&oacute;n, con 377 mil toneladas en el mar, 11.000 hect&aacute;reas de mar sembradas, 550 PyMEs productoras, 17.000 empleos formales directos y m&aacute;s de 5.000 indirectos, con 20 plantas procesadoras que general un aporte al PIB de m&aacute;s de U$ 250 millones anuales, por tanto una modificaci&oacute;n a la normativa en comento, a la luz de las obligaciones de Espa&ntilde;a bajo el Acuerdo OTC/OMC debiera ser notificada al Comit&eacute;, permitir un plazo para emitir comentarios y aceptar la evidencia cient&iacute;fica proporcionada para no emitir regulaciones que generen un menoscabo a la importaci&oacute;n de este producto desde Chile, sin ninguna justificaci&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

Chile espera que esta preocupaci&oacute;n comercial espec&iacute;fica pueda ser debidamente atendida por el Ministerio de Agricultura, Pesca y Alimentaci&oacute;n de Espa&ntilde;a, para lo cual esta delegaci&oacute;n agradecer&aacute; una pronta respuesta a trav&eacute;s del Punto de Contacto Oficial OTC/OMC.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Gracias se&ntilde;or Presidente.

La Rep&uacute;blica Dominicana desea reiterar nuevamente nuestra preocupaci&oacute;n sobre esta regulaci&oacute;n de la UE la cual se ha mantenido en la agenda de este comit&eacute; desde hace m&aacute;s de dos a&ntilde;os.

Esta medida eval&uacute;a estas sustancias bas&aacute;ndose en un enfoque de identificaci&oacute;n del peligro, en lugar de una evaluaci&oacute;n de riesgo, tal y como lo establece el Acuerdo MSF de la OMC.

La Uni&oacute;n Europea no ha podido demostrar un riesgo real, o si estas medidas reducen de manera mensurable el nivel de riesgo. Nos preocupa el impacto sist&eacute;mico y comercial que tienen estas medidas sobre las exportaciones de nuestro pa&iacute;s.


Este tipo de medidas tienen un impacto socioecon&oacute;mico directo no solo en la Rep&uacute;blica Dominicana sino en toda la regi&oacute;n, m&aacute;s a&uacute;n durante estos momentos de incertidumbre mundial derivada de la crisis de la pandemia del COVID-19, que aparte de traer consecuencias econ&oacute;micas negativas, ha afectado el comercio internacional.&nbsp;

Exhortamos a la Uni&oacute;n Europea a reducir los efectos negativos sobre el comercio internacional, tomando en consideraci&oacute;n las preocupaciones y observaciones de los pa&iacute;ses miembros para evitar imponer medidas innecesariamente restrictivas del comercio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Particularmente las que afectan a los productos agropecuarios, para entonces minimizar las consecuencias socioecon&oacute;micas de los pa&iacute;ses exportadores de productos b&aacute;sicos y especial para los agricultores, que son las poblaciones m&aacute;s vulnerables de los pa&iacute;ses en desarrollo como lo es la Rep&uacute;blica Dominicana. Muchas gracias.

Gracias Presidente, en l&iacute;nea similar con la intervenci&oacute;n anterior, no ha habido avances en la resoluci&oacute;n de esta preocupaci&oacute;n.&nbsp;

Notamos que la UE se&ntilde;alo que las respuestas a las preguntas de G/SPS/GEN/1926 que esperamos sean circuladas a la mayor brevedad posible. Sin embargo, y hasta la fecha no contamos con una respuesta satisfactoria a muchas de nuestras preocupaciones e interrogantes, espec&iacute;ficamente en lo que respecta a la ponderaci&oacute;n de &ldquo;otros factores leg&iacute;timos&rdquo;, tales como la protecci&oacute;n del medio ambiente, en el proceso de tolerancia de las importaciones.&nbsp;

Tampoco ha respondido si se mantendr&aacute; el sistema de autorizaciones de emergencia para sustancias a las cuales se pretende negar una tolerancia a la importaci&oacute;n por considerarse que las preocupaciones ambientales a las que se busca responder con la no renovaci&oacute;n de la sustancia, son de car&aacute;cter global. En este sentido, nos permitimos se&ntilde;alar una vez m&aacute;s, que el thiametoxham, se encontrar&iacute;a dentro de estas sustancias pero que desde la limitaci&oacute;n, la misma se ha visto beneficiada con m&aacute;s de 135 autorizaciones de emergencia en Austria, B&eacute;lgica, Republica Checa, Croacia, Dinamarca, Eslovaquia, Estonia, Francia, Finlandia, Hungr&iacute;a, Letonia, Lituania, Pa&iacute;ses&nbsp; Bajos, Polonia y Rumania; para productos como la remolacha, achicoria, lechugas, girasol, zanahorias, ma&iacute;z, l&uacute;pulos, coles, colzas, entre otros productos.

Estas autorizaciones pueden ser renovadas constantemente, aunque cuando son otorgadas sean limitadas por un periodo de 120 dias.&nbsp;

No podemos dejar de notar con preocupacion la diferencia del trato, en el que algunos se benefician de excepciones laxas y otros deben pasar por procesos mucho mas exigentes y sin garantias de exitos.&nbsp;

M&eacute;xico hace referencia una vez m&aacute;s a la preocupaci&oacute;n planteada en relaci&oacute;n a las restricciones impuestas por Guatemala a las importaciones de ovoproductos con procesamiento t&eacute;rmico originarios de M&eacute;xico. Esta preocupaci&oacute;n se present&oacute; por primera vez en este foro en octubre de 2016, y ha sido reiterada en noviembre de 2018, marzo y julio de 2019, junio de 2020, y marzo y julio de 2021. 

&nbsp;

Dicha preocupaci&oacute;n deriva de la solicitud del Servicio Nacional de Sanidad, Inocuidad y Calidad Agroalimentaria (SENASICA) de M&eacute;xico, sobre el procedimiento y requisitos para la exportaci&oacute;n de ovoproductos l&iacute;quidos y deshidratados sometidos a proceso t&eacute;rmico, sobre lo cual, se ha enviado al Ministerio de Agricultura, Ganader&iacute;a y Alimentaci&oacute;n de Guatemala (MAGA), la informaci&oacute;n t&eacute;cnica que brinda las garant&iacute;as necesarias para asegurar que las exportaciones de dicho producto desde M&eacute;xico no implican un riesgo, sin embargo, las respuestas por parte de Guatemala han fluido de forma dilatoria y discontinua, dificultando as&iacute; los avances en las negociaciones.

&nbsp;

Adem&aacute;s de este foro, la preocupaci&oacute;n se ha externado en diversas reuniones, destac&aacute;ndose la reuni&oacute;n de abril de este a&ntilde;o, en el marco del Comit&eacute; MSF del Tratado de Libre Comercio entre M&eacute;xico y Centroam&eacute;rica, en la que Guatemala indic&oacute; la necesidad de evaluar a los servicios veterinarios a trav&eacute;s del cuestionario correspondiente.

&nbsp;

En octubre de 2021, las autoridades de Guatemala indicaron que no era posible continuar con el an&aacute;lisis debido a que la SAGARPA fue sustituida por la SADER; sin embargo, es preciso mencionar que la sustituci&oacute;n mencionada fue &uacute;nicamente del nombre de la Secretar&iacute;a, no vi&eacute;ndose afectadas las funciones, objetivos y lineamientos que rigen a la Secretar&iacute;a y a sus &oacute;rganos desconcentrados, situaci&oacute;n que fue notificada por los canales correspondientes a la Organizaci&oacute;n Mundial del Comercio y publicada en el Diario Oficial de la Federaci&oacute;n &nbsp;

&nbsp;

Considerando la respuesta del MAGA del pasado 04 de octubre de 2021, en la cual se indica que el cuestionario remitido por M&eacute;xico es considerado NO CONFORME, debido a la situaci&oacute;n descrita en el p&aacute;rrafo anterior, M&eacute;xico sigue considerando que la restricci&oacute;n impuesta por Guatemala es violatoria de los principios fundamentales sobre justificaci&oacute;n t&eacute;cnica y cient&iacute;fica, apego a las normas internacionales y evaluaci&oacute;n de riesgo adecuada a las circunstancias contempladas tanto en el Acuerdo MSF, como en el Tratado de Libre Comercio que M&eacute;xico y Centroam&eacute;rica tienen suscrito.

&nbsp;

A la luz de lo anterior, la delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico solicita amablemente a la delegaci&oacute;n de Guatemala lo siguiente:

&nbsp;


	Considerar como CONFORME, el cuestionario remitido por M&eacute;xico.
	Que las autoridades de Guatemala den prioridad al tema para dar resoluci&oacute;n a esta Preocupaci&oacute;n Comercial Espec&iacute;fica lo m&aacute;s pronto posible y continuar con el di&aacute;logo cordial entre autoridades.
	Permitir la importaci&oacute;n de los productos en cuesti&oacute;n.


&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece a la delegaci&oacute;n de Guatemala por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y las solicitudes en ella expuestas y reitera la importancia de resolver esta situaci&oacute;n de manera &aacute;gil y expedita, a trav&eacute;s del di&aacute;logo t&eacute;cnico entre ambos pa&iacute;ses. 

&nbsp;

Once again, the Philippines would like to reiterate our deep concern about Indonesia&rsquo;s measures and approach in applying these measures without regard to the objective of minimizing negative trade effects, as provided for in Article 5.4; the requirement under Article 5.6 not to introduce SPS measures that are more trade restrictive than required to achieve Indonesia&rsquo;s appropriate level of protection; transparency obligations under Article 7 and Annex B; and obligation to undertake procedures of checking SPS compliance without undue delay, as provided for in Article 8 and Annex C.

Despite complying with Indonesia&rsquo;s requirement of laboratory accreditation for MRL and heavy metals analysis in 2018 and the additional requirement of recognition of pest-free areas in 2019, our exporters continue to face difficulties in exporting shallots to Indonesia. Additional requirements are mandated from our exporters such as GAP certificate, license to operate issued by the Philippine competent authority, among others, prior to the issuance of permits to importers for shallots. These additional requirements were not officially communicated or made known to the Philippines through the usual process of notification under the purview of this Committee. This was further exacerbated by undue delays in the approval procedure. Import permits were only issued eight months since our exporters provided all the additional requirements. For the recent applications, Philippine exporters started providing their Indonesian trading partners with the necessary documents to support import permit applications in August 2020. However, they were faced by another documentary requirement, the certificate of good handling practices. We officially communicated with Indonesia as well as engaged the same bilaterally regarding this concern. As of this report date, there have been no permits issued to allow the Philippines to export shallots to the Indonesian market.

We would like to reiterate that Philippine exports of shallots to Indonesia have ceased, primarily due to the regulations enforced by Indonesia back in 2011. The Philippines and Indonesia had robust trade of shallots, without any food safety and phytosanitary issues, prior to the enactment of these regulations. We were made to understand that regulations have since been replaced; however, the changes have not addressed the difficulties faced by Philippine exporters of shallots to Indonesia.

The undue delays, lack of transparency, piecemeal and unpredictable approach, and discriminatory requirements without scientific justification were also faced by the Philippines as regards Indonesia&rsquo;s approval measures for the import of meat and meat products.

We urge Indonesia to be transparent and timely in its approval measures, and refrain from its piecemeal approach of introducing requirements that are cumbersome and costly; hence, making trade restrictive for our exporters.

We remain hopeful in our bilateral consultations with Indonesia in order to finally conclude and resolve successfully this long-standing concern.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Last update: 08.06.2020

77th WTO SPS Committee - Geneva, June 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

EU statement&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Subject: &nbsp;3. SPECIFIC TRADE CONCERNS - (b) Previously raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;INDONESIA&#39;S APPROVAL PROCEDURES FOR PLANT AND ANIMAL PRODUCTS (STC No 441)

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The European Union is concerned by the lack of transparency and undue delays of Indonesia&rsquo;s approval procedures.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As reported on previous occasions, the EU asked Indonesia to provide detailed information related to its market access approval procedures for agri-food products, including indicative and average timeframes for completing such procedures. Indonesia committed to reply but the EU has no so far received the requested clarifications.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The EU is in particular concerned by the lack of progress on its export applications on beef, dairy, poultry, pork and plant products, which in some instances were submitted more than 6 years ago.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Moreover the EU is experiencing specific difficulties with its export of dairy products, namely delay in approval procedures, postponement of audits and restrictions on the issuance of import license approvals for already approved Member States and establishments. All this constitutes unjustified barriers to trade.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; These developments are very worrying and of great concern to the EU and its Member States.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The EU believes that Indonesia is in breach of its obligations under the SPS Agreement, namely those of Article 8 and Annex C, 1.a) and 1.b) by the lack of transparency on market access approval procedures.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Against this background, the EU would like to ask Indonesia to correct the situation with no further delay. In particular, the EU calls Indonesia to respect its obligations by being transparent about its approval procedures and by finalizing pending market access applications from the EU Member States without undue delays. Moreover, the EU urges Indonesia to ensure that import licenses for dairy products that are already approved for export to Indonesia are swiftly issued.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The EU looks forward to receiving the requested information from Indonesia about its import procedures, the state of works on pending applications and the assurance on the respect of WTO SPS principles.

?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; For this, and as usual, the EU remains open to continue working with Indonesia to make concrete progress on market access applications for export of animal products to Indonesia and to have a more effective and regular dialogue at the appropriate level.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The United States would like to recall our previous statements at the July 2018, November 2018, and March 2019 Committee meetings that lay out our concerns regarding the implementation by the European Union (EU) of Article 118 of EU 2019/6.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We will not reiterate the details of these concerns now.&nbsp; These are documented in the Committee&rsquo;s summary records of previous meetings.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We appreciate our extensive bilateral engagement with the EU, as well as the EU&rsquo;s willingness to meet with concerned third countries in March 2019 and January 2020.
&nbsp;
We note that the implementation of this measure will require producers of animals and products of animal origin who intend to ship to the EU to comply with new EU production standards.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We understand that the European Commission developed a draft on the delegated act of the criteria to designate antimicrobials to be reserved for human treatment. &nbsp;Does the European Commission plan to consult with stakeholders and third countries on this draft?
&nbsp;
We would like to note our previous questions regarding the scope of animal products that the EU measure will impact, the antimicrobials to be included on the EU&rsquo;s list, and the enforcement mechanisms to be employed by the EU.
&nbsp;
Lastly, we understand that the delegated act on the criteria for antimicrobials is to be adopted no later than September 27, 2021, and the implementing acts on the list and rules for imports, no later than January 27, 2022.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We are concerned that the transition period between issuing the list of antimicrobials reserved for human use only and its application to imports will not allow sufficient time for producers and other operators to adapt. &nbsp;
&nbsp;
The United States has successfully implemented national programs to combat antimicrobial resistance while avoiding trade restrictions.&nbsp; We urge the EU to do so as well.
&nbsp;

The European Union&rsquo;s (EU) Veterinary Legislation That Would Restrict the Use of Antimicrobials by Producers in Third Countries (STC 446)

March 2021 WTO SPS Committee Meeting - United States Intervention

&nbsp;

The United States would like to call attention to our previous statements made at the July 2018, November 2018, March 2019, and June 2020 Committee meetings that lay out our concerns regarding the implementation by the EU of Article 118 of EU 2019/6.&nbsp;

We will not reiterate the details of our previous interventions, as they are documented in the Committee&rsquo;s summary reports.&nbsp;

We appreciate our bilateral engagement with the EU, as well as the EU&rsquo;s willingness to meet with concerned third countries, most recently in December 2020.

However, we are concerned that the European Commission has not yet published the delegated act of the criteria to designate antimicrobials to be reserved for human treatment for comment and review, which we understand will be adopted no later than September 27, 2021. The EU has also not yet published the implementing acts on the list and rules for imports that, again, we understand must be adopted no later than January 27, 2022.&nbsp; Recognizing these still remaining steps in the EC legislative process, we urge the EC to issue relevant documents in a timely manner allowing sufficient time for careful review and comments by stakeholders.

Further, the EU has yet to provide details regarding the scientific justification and risk assessments that will inform its list of antimicrobials reserved for human use. The EU has also not clarified how risk assessment will be used to inform its import policies, including whether data from third countries will be considered in assessments, nor has the EU explained how producers will be able to meet the requirements when the legislation is expected to go into force in less than one year.

Third countries cannot begin to make changes to their production practices until they know which antimicrobials may be reserved for human use or what type of control systems the European Commission may require for imports.

In addition to concerns around the extraterritorial implications of the EU&rsquo;s proposed measures, we are concerned that the EU will not allow a sufficient transition period between the finalization of its list of antimicrobials reserved for human use and the application of SPS measures to imported products.&nbsp; From our perspective, an appropriate transition period for justified SPS measures would be based on the lifespan of livestock under production and would also consider the shelf and storage life of products already in the supply chain.&nbsp; Cattle producers, for example, may need at least five years to adopt changes in the use of antimicrobial treatments.&nbsp;

Through its actions, we fear that the EU may end up limiting access by veterinarians to the medicines they need to address common livestock diseases associated with modern husbandry, while at the same time undermining the competence of national veterinary authorities, and we remind the EU that equivalence is a key provision of the SPS Agreement.

While we support innovation and the search for alternative approaches, data and information on safe and effective alternative approaches simply do not currently exist in some cases.&nbsp; Unnecessarily limiting access to safe veterinary medical products and practices will likely result in unintended negative animal health consequences and damage the livelihoods of livestock producers in the EU and beyond its borders. This, in turn, will affect Members&#39; ability to achieve goals related to food security and sustainable development.

Therefore, we ask the EU to limit phasing out the use of antimicrobials for growth promotion to those products that are medically important, rather than all antimicrobials.&nbsp; This would allow countries to focus efforts and resources on areas of public health concern while supporting safe and effective animal husbandry practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

The United States has avoided trade restrictions while successfully implementing national programs to address the development and spread of antimicrobial resistance. Beyond simply considering possible human health outcomes, we urge the EU to consider the needs of agricultural producers and both recognize and respect the level of protection provided by national regulatory systems as it works to implement its own system.

The European Union&rsquo;s (EU) Veterinary Legislation That Would Restrict the Use of Antimicrobials by Producers in Third Countries (STC 446)

July 2021 WTO SPS Committee Meeting&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;


	The United States would like to again call attention to our previous statements made at the July 2018, November 2018, March 2019, June 2020, and March 2021 meetings of this Committee and documented in the summary reports that lay out our concerns regarding the implementation of Article 118 of the EU&rsquo;s Regulation 2019/6.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Since the March 2021 Committee meeting, the European Commission (EC) notified two proposals associated with the prohibition of certain uses of antimicrobials. &nbsp;However, we continue to have concerns that the EC has not yet published the implementing acts on the list and rules for imports that we understand must be adopted no later than January 28, 2022.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	The EC notified G/SPS/N/EU/464 that adds to Article 118 of EU Regulation 2019/6 and amends Regulation 2017/625 regarding official controls on animals and products of animal origin exported from third countries into the EU. &nbsp;The legislation, however, does not explain how the EU plans to verify compliance of imported products with Article 118. As such, the implications of this legislation on trade to the EU remain unclear. &nbsp;In particular, there has been no clarification on how risk assessments will be used to inform import policies, nor has the EU appeared to consider the challenges foreign producers will face to comply with the legislation when it enters into force in a few months.


&nbsp;


	The EC also notified G/SPS/N/EU/478 of the delegated regulation to establish the criteria for the designation of antimicrobials to be reserved for the treatment of certain infections in humans and adopted the criteria on May 26, 2021. &nbsp;However, we remain concerned that the EU has not provided details on how the criteria will be used to inform its list of antimicrobials reserved for human use and how or whether the list will be updated. We note that this information is necessary to ensure a fair, transparent, and science-based risk assessment.


&nbsp;


	We understand that, through the criteria it developed, the EC is attempting to enhance its One Health approach to addressing AMR. &nbsp;As Members of this Committee are aware, a One Health approach recognizes the interconnectedness of people, animals, plants, and their shared environment. &nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Animal species, pathogens causing diseases, health management practices, antimicrobial access, availability of alternative treatments, and antimicrobial susceptibility profiles vary by country, and the United States is concerned that the EU is not adequately or appropriately considering the health of animals within this new policy, especially in cases where alternative treatments are not available in an exporting country.


&nbsp;


	Specifically, through its actions, we fear that the EU may unnecessarily limit access to the medicines needed to address common livestock diseases associated with modern husbandry. Risk management actions such as restricting use of antimicrobial drugs without scientific justification may result in unintended negative animal health consequences through disease spread and ultimately damage the livelihoods of livestock producers in the EU and beyond its borders. &nbsp;This, in turn, may affect countries&rsquo; abilities to achieve goals related to food security and sustainable development.


&nbsp;


	We are also concerned that the EU will not allow a sufficient transition period between the finalization of its list of antimicrobials reserved for human use and the application of SPS measures to imported products.&nbsp; We would note that, to limit unnecessary disruptions in production and trade, an appropriate transition period for justified SPS measures should be based on the lifespan of livestock under production and would also consider the shelf and storage life of products already in the supply chain.&nbsp; Cattle, poultry, and pigs, for example, have different life cycles and the transition periods for use of antimicrobial treatments would likely need to reflect those differences.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Further, we understand that the EU legislation includes restrictions around specific uses of antimicrobial drugs that are not medically important for humans, and we are concerned that these provisions will divert countries&rsquo; attention and resources away from more meaningful interventions with direct human health impacts.&nbsp; Therefore, we ask the EU to maintain the use of antimicrobials for growth promotion that are not medically important for humans. This would allow countries to focus efforts and resources on areas of public health concern while promoting safe and effective animal husbandry practices that support agriculture sustainability and food security.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	AMR is a health issue, and should not be used as an unjustified barrier to trade. &nbsp;For instance, the United States has avoided trade restrictions while successfully implementing national programs to address the development and spread of AMR. We urge the EU to avoid trade disruption, base its regulations on science and risk, and to consider the impact of its SPS measures on global animal health, food security, and agriculture sustainability. &nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Through this intervention, we reiterate our request that the EC issue relevant implementing regulations in a timely manner and allow sufficient time for careful review and comments by Members and foreign stakeholders such that the input may be considered. Further, as the EU moves forward with this process, we request that the EU both recognize and respect the level of protection provided by national regulatory systems and afford national competent authorities the flexibility they need to implement their own effective SPS systems.

The European Union&rsquo;s (EU) Veterinary Legislation That Would Restrict the Use of Antimicrobials by Producers in Third Countries (STC 446)

November 2021 WTO SPS Committee Meeting&nbsp;- Statement by the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 


	The United States would like to once again call attention to previous statements made at the SPS Committee, most recently during our July 2021 meetings and documented in the summary reports that lay out our concerns regarding the implementation of Article 118 of the EU&rsquo;s Regulation 2019/6.&nbsp;
	First, we would like to acknowledge the outcome of the September 15, 2021, session of the European Parliament, which allowed for the science-based criteria to move forward and be used to determine the EU&rsquo;s list of antimicrobials reserved for human use. This is a positive step, particularly given recent opposition to the criteria.
	As the EU starts to apply its criteria to develop its list of antimicrobials reserved for human use, we would like to request that the EU provide details on how the list will be maintained to ensure a fair, transparent, and science-based risk assessment. Regardless of when the measures are implemented, we would like to reiterate our concerns that, as currently envisioned, the legislation may unjustifiably limit access to the medicines needed to address common livestock diseases associated with modern husbandry, particularly in developing countries where alternative treatments are not available. &nbsp;
	Animal species, pathogens causing diseases, health management practices, antimicrobial access, availability of alternative treatments, and antimicrobial susceptibility profiles vary by country and regions within particular countries.&nbsp;
	The restricted use of antimicrobial drugs without scientific justification may result in unintended negative animal health consequences through disease spread and damage the livelihoods of livestock producers in the EU and beyond, ultimately affecting countries&rsquo; abilities to achieve goals related to food security and sustainable development. &nbsp;
	We understand that the EU legislation includes restrictions around specific uses of antimicrobial drugs that are not medically important for humans. &nbsp;These provisions may divert countries&rsquo; resources away from more meaningful interventions around antimicrobial resistance, or AMR, with direct human health impacts.&nbsp; Therefore, we again ask the EU to provide scientific justification for the restrictions on the use of antimicrobial drugs for growth promotion that are not medically important for humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Finally, as we have noted in our previous interventions, we reiterate the importance of appropriate transition periods for justified SPS measures, which should be based on the lifespan of livestock under production and should also consider the shelf and storage life of products already in the supply chain.
	Moving forward, we urge the EU review and revise its proposed timeline for implementation of these measures in order to avoid trade disruptions, to base its regulations on science and risk, and to consider the impact of its SPS measures on global animal health, food security, and agriculture sustainability. &nbsp;The United States also requests that the EU both recognize and respect the level of protection provided by national regulatory systems and afford national competent authorities the flexibility they need to implement their own effective SPS systems.

The United States thanks the sponsoring Members for continuing to support inclusion of this important issue on the Committee&rsquo;s agenda.
&nbsp;
We remain deeply concerned over the European Union&rsquo;s (EU) implementation of pesticide policies that lower maximum residue levels (MRLs) to trade restrictive levels without clear scientific justification or measurable benefit to human health. &nbsp;The EU&rsquo;s codification of a hazard-based approach to pesticide regulation and implementation of the so-called &ldquo;precautionary principle&rdquo; is adversely impacting global agricultural production and trade, particularly in developing countries.
&nbsp;
The EU has claimed that its pesticide and MRL measures are transparent, science-based, and non-discriminatory. &nbsp;Unfortunately, we do not find the EU&rsquo;s claims about its regulatory procedures or its &ldquo;high level of protection&rdquo; to comport with its actions.
&nbsp;
As the United States conveyed in our November 2019 statements to the Committee&mdash;circulated as G/SPS/GEN/1749 and G/SPS/GEN/1750&mdash;we continue to have serious concerns about the scientific underpinnings, objectivity, and consistency in application of EU measures.
&nbsp;
At the November 2019 meeting, the EU responded to our concerns only by saying that our statement was lengthy.&nbsp; We agree.&nbsp; Our itemized account of concerns regarding the scientific underpinnings, objectivity, and consistency of EU actions was lengthy.&nbsp; Again, as one of our largest trading partners, we invite the EU to respond in good faith to our concerns detailed in GEN/1749 and GEN/1750.
&nbsp;
Beyond the concerns previously detailed, we are troubled by additional actions taken by the EU.&nbsp; We would like to bring the attention of the Committee to recent actions taken by the EU that raise serious questions regarding its implementation of the SPS Agreement.
&nbsp;
First, we recall the precedent set in March 2019, when the European Parliament blocked the implementation of an import MRL for clothianidin on potatoes, despite determinations by the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) and European Commission that the proposed import MRL met all approval and safety criteria.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
The European Commission&rsquo;s subsequent withdrawal of the proposed import MRL, based on the Parliament&rsquo;s objections, appears to contradict the EU&rsquo;s repeated assurances to this Committee that EU MRLs will be established through an objective, risk-based process.
&nbsp;
In April 2020, the Committee on the Environment, Public Health and Food Safety of the European Parliament passed a resolution to block the implementation of MRLs for a series of products, despite determinations by EFSA and the European Commission that the proposed MRLs met all approval and safety criteria.
&nbsp;
In this case, we understand that a COVID19-related suspension prevented the European Parliament from acting on the resolution within the established legislative timelines.&nbsp; Will the European Commission resubmit a draft Commission regulation to set these MRLs at the same level recommended in its original draft resolution?&nbsp; If the European Commission is considering lowering these MRLs in a resubmission, we ask the EU to explain the basis for such changes.
&nbsp;
In addition to the Farm to Fork strategy, the Commission released in May 2020 the Pesticide REFIT evaluating Regulations 1107/2009 and 396/2005.&nbsp; Recalling that over 40 Members have in this Committee raised concerns about these Regulations in the last 5 years, we invite the EU to inform the Committee how it intends to implement several conclusions contained in the Pesticide REFIT in a manner consistent with its obligations under the SPS Agreement, including the obligation to base such measures on risk assessment.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
The Pesticide REFIT calls for improved implementation of the &ldquo;cut-off criteria&rdquo; contained in Regulation 1107/2009, including:&nbsp; (1) through increased certainty in the use of the cut-off criteria, and (2) by only continuing to conduct a full risk assessment if either the active substances do not meet the cut-off criteria or a least one of the derogation possibilities is invoked.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
In light of the many questions in our previous statements on:&nbsp; (1) how the EU is achieving consistency in the level of protection it considers appropriate with respect its actions on specific substances, (2) whether or not the EU applies a weight of evidence approach when considering, for example, genotoxicity data, and (3) the operation of the derogation provisions, we invite the EU to explain how it will attain increased certainty going forward in the use of cut-off criteria and when it will invoke derogations.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
If the EU will no longer conduct &ldquo;full risk assessments&rdquo; on substances meeting the &ldquo;cut-off criteria&rdquo;, we also ask the EU to explain the relationship of the assessments it will undertake to the elements established by Codex guidelines.
&nbsp;
We are also interested in the EU&rsquo;s plans announced in the Pesticide REFIT, to use all of its &ldquo;diplomacy, trade policy, and development support instruments&rdquo; to promote adoption of its hazard-based approach to pesticides in third countries, as well as to encourage support of its approach in the WTO SPS Committee and the Codex Alimentarius.&nbsp; The REFIT document makes clear that EU diplomatic, trade, and development policies are intended to &ldquo;ensure a level-playing field for EU operators.&rdquo;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Article 12 of the SPS Agreement directs the Committee to carry out the functions necessary to implement its provisions and to further its objectives.&nbsp; We welcome other Members&rsquo; views on the EU&rsquo;s intention to level the playing field for EU operators by, what would appear to be, restricting market access on the basis of use of crop protection tools that are deemed safe through scientific evaluation by competent regulatory authorities around the world, as well as to use this Committee to advance these objectives.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
With respect to the REFIT reference to Codex, we note that last year, the EU requested that the Joint FAO/WHO Meeting on Pesticide Residues (JMPR) reevaluate certain substances that are being banned in the EU. &nbsp;In September 2019, the JMPR evaluated the EU&rsquo;s concern forms for several such substances and disagreed with the EU&rsquo;s findings. &nbsp;On the basis of risk assessments, JMPR reaffirmed the safety of existing Codex MRLs for these substances. Around the world, national regulators, JMPR, and Codex are being compelled to divert limited resources to defend risk-based evaluations of substances already deemed safe for agricultural use and for consumers. &nbsp;Meanwhile, the EU&rsquo;s efforts are contributing to an erosion of public confidence in risk-based regulatory systems and casting unwarranted doubt in consumers&rsquo; minds about the safety of the global food supply.&nbsp; We invite the EU to explain how its efforts advance implementation of the SPS Agreement and further its objectives.
&nbsp;
Lastly, our growers and processors are increasingly concerned that the EU continues to implement transition measures that do not provide adequate time for legally produced commodities to clear the channels of trade, and that appear to establish differences in treatment between domestic and imported products.
&nbsp;
If the EU&rsquo;s short transitional measures for imported products are based on health concerns, as the EU has claimed, then why are MRL changes only notified to the SPS Committee after EU producers have benefitted from grace periods that ensure their own treated products can clear the channels of trade? &nbsp;Why has the EU not extended corresponding grace periods or transition measures to foreign producers?
&nbsp;
In the EU&rsquo;s response to U.S. comments on G/SPS/N/EU/248&mdash;one of the first notified EU MRL measures to introduce the transition measures in question&mdash;the EU explicitly acknowledged that non-EU countries would have a shorter time to comply with new MRLs compared to EU Member States.
&nbsp;
Although the EU indicated during our last meeting in November 2019 that it would begin notifying its pesticide non-renewal measures to the SPS Committee, it is not clear what steps the EU plans to take to ensure that its transition measures do not arbitrarily or unjustifiably discriminate between its own territory and that of other Members.
&nbsp;
As conveyed at the Council for Trade in Goods in November 2019 and June 2020, and as emphasized in the joint statement found in document G/C/W/767/Rev.1, farmers around the world rely on access to the full range of tools and technologies available for agricultural production, and these tools are essential to mitigate food security risks and to alleviate poverty.
&nbsp;
This access is facilitated by well-functioning, objective, and science-based regulatory systems that protect consumers and establish the basis for fair trade.
&nbsp;
This access is critical to protecting crops from pests and diseases, which are estimated to cause annual losses of 20 to 40 percent, and allows farmers to enhance yields and productivity while also limiting post-harvest losses and reducing unnecessary food waste, thus ensuring that the resources invested in producing food are being managed efficiently and sustainably.
&nbsp;
This access promotes an abundant supply of affordable, safe food to meet the growing demands of global consumers, many of whom rely directly or indirectly on agriculture to sustain their livelihoods.
&nbsp;
And yet, despite the concerns expressed by an unprecedented number of Members in the SPS and TBT Committees over the past 4 years, the EU continues to implement measures that jeopardize this access, without clear scientific justification or measurable health benefit, and without apparent regard for the potential to undermine rural livelihoods and sustainable development.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
For these reasons, we once again call on the EU to re-evaluate its approach to regulating pesticides; to confirm that import MRLs will be established on the basis of science and internationally-accepted approaches to risk analysis; and to cease implementation of those measures that unnecessarily restrict international trade.
&nbsp;

En los &uacute;ltimos a&ntilde;os Costa Rica ha notado con preocupaci&oacute;n la forma en la que la UE ha reducido los LMR para una serie de productos para la protecci&oacute;n de plantas que son fundamentales en la producci&oacute;n agr&iacute;cola y en el manejo de plagas de importancia cuarentenaria de los pa&iacute;ses con climas tropicales. Esas reducciones a menudo se alejan de las tolerancias establecidas por la comunidad internacional en el Codex Alimentarius, lo que es contrario al objetivo de facilitaci&oacute;n del comercio y armonizaci&oacute;n que rige las obligaciones del comercio internacional acordadas a nivel multilateral.

&nbsp;

A nivel espec&iacute;fico, en m&uacute;ltiples ocasiones hemos compartido con la Uni&oacute;n Europea nuestras preocupaciones sobre el impacto que tendr&iacute;a sobre nuestro sistema de producci&oacute;n la reducci&oacute;n de los LMRs a niveles m&iacute;nimos de detecci&oacute;n para varias de las sustancias enumeradas en esta preocupaci&oacute;n. Costa Rica ha enviado comentarios a trav&eacute;s de los procesos creados a tal efecto tanto a nivel bilateral y regional, como multilateral, y en el marco de un creciente n&uacute;mero de preocupaciones, levantadas por un igualmente creciente n&uacute;mero de Miembros; tanto en este Comit&eacute;, como en el Comit&eacute; sobre Obst&aacute;culos T&eacute;cnicos al Comercio, y m&aacute;s recientemente en el Consejo de Comercio de Mercanc&iacute;as. Sin embargo, a la fecha no hemos recibido respuesta satisfactoria por parte de la Uni&oacute;n Europea, que ha decidido continuar con su enfoque actual a pesar del impacto que esto tendr&iacute;a en la seguridad alimentaria y en las poblaciones m&aacute;s vulnerables de nuestro pa&iacute;s.

&nbsp;

Ya en anteriores reuniones abordamos el caso del clorotalonil, el imazalil y la buprofezina, a las que ahora se suma el mancozeb. Todas estas son sustancias que en Costa Rica se utilizan para el control de plagas cuarentenarias que afectan la producci&oacute;n y el transporte de banano. En todos estos casos hemos expresado en repetidas ocasiones nuestra preocupaci&oacute;n por la ausencia de evidencia cient&iacute;fica concluyente que justificara los cambios de LMRs, as&iacute; como la divergencia entre los hallazgos de la Uni&oacute;n Europea con respecto a las conclusiones y hallazgo de otras instituciones de importancia internacional. Tambi&eacute;n hemos planteado preguntas, cuya &uacute;ltima iteraci&oacute;n se encuentra en el documento G/SPS/GEN/1760.

&nbsp;

M&aacute;s all&aacute; del enfoque basado en peligro asumido por la UE, hemos recalcado la importancia de tomar tambi&eacute;n en consideraci&oacute;n el impacto que estas nuevas regulaciones tendr&iacute;an sobre los sistemas de producci&oacute;n de sus socios comerciales. El hecho es que en el mercado existe una disponibilidad limitada de mol&eacute;culas que permitan una adecuada rotaci&oacute;n para el control de plagas en condiciones de producci&oacute;n tropical, y las pocas que existen son fundamentales para reducir la posibilidad del desarrollo de resistencia cruzada. Es por ello que, la no renovaci&oacute;n y reducci&oacute;n de los l&iacute;mites m&aacute;ximos de residuos para buprofezina, clorotalonil e imazalil a niveles como los que est&aacute; estableciendo la Uni&oacute;n Europea, en la pr&aacute;ctica implica la eliminaci&oacute;n de estas herramientas de rotaci&oacute;n en los programas de control de plagas en banano que est&aacute;n siempre presentes en el tr&oacute;pico h&uacute;medo como la escama, la cochinilla o la temida sigatoka negra.

&nbsp;

Se&ntilde;or Presidente, nuestras preocupaciones y solicitudes han sido apoyadas por decenas de Miembros en los documentos G/C/W/767 y G/SPS/GEN/1778 (y sus revisiones), por lo que no las repetiremos hoy. En esta ocasi&oacute;n tomamos la palabra para instar a la Uni&oacute;n Europea a que reconsidere la direcci&oacute;n de su enfoque regulatorio y a que establezca un dialogo efectivo y amplio con los Miembros que se est&aacute;n viendo afectados, y a considerar medidas que limiten el impacto que tendr&iacute;an estas nuevas regulaciones en la seguridad alimentaria a nivel mundial. Este llamado es a&uacute;n m&aacute;s urgente ante las tr&aacute;gicas consecuencias que est&aacute; teniendo la pandemia de COVID-19 sobre las poblaciones m&aacute;s vulnerables de los pa&iacute;ses en desarrollo y menos adelantados.

This STC also includes the new ones from the&nbsp;June meeting:&nbsp;

474&nbsp;Modification of EU MRLs for plant protection products: Chlorpyrifos and Chlorpyrifos-methyl&nbsp;

(G/SPS/N/EU/360,&nbsp;G/SPS/GEN/1761,&nbsp;G/SPS/GEN/1807)

475&nbsp;Modification of EU MRLs for plant protection products: Mancozeb&nbsp;

(G/SPS/N/EU/384,&nbsp;G/SPS/N/EU/384/Add.1,&nbsp;G/TBT/N/EU/712,&nbsp;G/SPS/GEN/1796,&nbsp;G/SPS/GEN/1808)&nbsp;

EU MRLs and Pesticide Policies

November 2020 WTO SPS Committee Meeting - Intervention by the United States

&nbsp;


	The United States thanks Colombia, Costa Rica, Dominican Republic, Ecuador, and Paraguay for continuing to support inclusion of this important issue on the Committee&rsquo;s agenda.


&nbsp;


	We remain deeply concerned over the EU&rsquo;s implementation of unnecessarily restrictive pesticide policies. The EU&rsquo;s codification of a hazard-based approach to pesticide regulation and implementation of the so-called &ldquo;precautionary principle&rdquo; is adversely impacting global agricultural production and trade, particularly in developing countries.


&nbsp;


	At the June 2020 meeting, the EU provided a list of responses that once again do not address our concerns.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	The United States asked about the level of protection the EU seeks when it ignores the weight of scientific evidence in making its regulatory decisions. The EU responded that residues must not present &ldquo;an unacceptable risk to humans.&rdquo;&nbsp; However, many of the substances for which the EU has reduced MRLs to the limit of detection (LOD) have been reviewed by Codex and other trading partners, and found not to present an unacceptable risk when used according to good agricultural practices.&nbsp; And so, we continue to ask how these MRLs that have been adopted by Codex and are based on scientific evidence do not meet the EU&rsquo;s level of protection.


&nbsp;


	We specifically note in this regard, G/SPS/N/EU/394 notified on July15, 2020.&nbsp; Despite Codex proposing to increase its chlorothalonil MRL for cranberries, the EU has reduced the MRL to the LOD.&nbsp; In the same notification, and despite Codex having established MRLs, the EU also reduced MRLs to the LOD for fenamidone and propiconazole, foliar fungicides used on many crops including leafy green vegetables and brassicas, mushrooms, corn, wild rice, peanuts, almonds, sorghum, oats, pecans, apricots, peaches, nectarines, plums and prunes. Using a variety of fungicides helps reduce resistance to any specific fungicide, but the EU is limiting producer access to these tools.


&nbsp;


	The EU&rsquo;s efforts are eroding public confidence in Codex and risk-based regulatory systems and casting unwarranted doubt in consumers&rsquo; minds about the safety of the global food supply while doing nothing to advance public health.&nbsp; We request that the EU explain how these actions are consistent with obligations of the SPS Agreement.


&nbsp;


	The EU also claims that it makes its decisions on active substances &ldquo;after a rigorous risk assessment.&rdquo;&nbsp; However, the European Food Safety Agency (EFSA) reviews often point to inconclusive results or data gaps and EFSA then declines to complete the consumer risk assessments.&nbsp; Again we reference G/SPS/N/EU/394 as an example.&nbsp; The notification covered the modification of MRLs in ten active substances, eight of which had been previously approved for use in the EU but were not renewed upon review.&nbsp; None of these eight active substances, carbon tetrachloride, chlorothalonil, chlorpropham, dimethoate, ethoprophos, fenamidone, methiocarb, propiconazole nor pymetrozine, had completed consumer risk assessments as part of their EFSA reviews.&nbsp; This is only one of several SPS notifications that the EU has made in 2020


&nbsp;


	We also continue to be concerned with increasing politicization of MRLs, most notably in April when the Committee on Environment, Public Health and Food Safety (ENVI) voted in favor of an objection tabled by a Member of Parliament on maximum residue levels for mandestrobin, commonly used on strawberries, table grapes and wine.&nbsp; The MRLs that ENVI rejected met all EU approval and safety criteria, including a supportive EFSA risk assessment on data sufficiency and unlikely risk to consumer health.


&nbsp;


	The EU also continues to implement transition measures that do not provide adequate time for legally produced commodities to clear the channels of trade, and that appear to establish differences in treatment between domestic and imported products.


&nbsp;


	The EU&rsquo;s Farm to Fork strategy, Biodiversity Strategy, and the Pesticide REFIT evaluating Regulations 1107/2009 and 396/2005 raise concerns that the EU will only accelerate the use of the problematic practices we have highlighted.&nbsp; Further, these documents all include directives to force trading partners to adopt the EU&rsquo;s precautionary standards.


&nbsp;


	In July 2020, the U.S. International Trade Commission, an independent federal agency, published volume one of its report, &ldquo;The Global Economic Impact of Missing and Low Pesticide MRLs.&rdquo;&nbsp; The report finds, among many things, trade to be negatively impacted by unharmonized MRLs.


&nbsp;


	The international community should be working together to support science-based measures that promote a safe and sustainable food supply.&nbsp; We call on the EU to join with its trading partners in identifying such mutually beneficial solutions.

La siguiente intervenci&oacute;n se refiere a la Preocupaci&oacute;n comercial 448 y la Preocupaci&oacute;n comercial 475. 

&nbsp;

Ecuador desea reiterar su solicitud de suspensi&oacute;n de los procesos previos y la entrada en vigor de las reducciones de L&iacute;mites M&aacute;ximos de Residuos (LMR) para productos de protecci&oacute;n de plantas, en consideraci&oacute;n a los esfuerzos de los sectores productivos para la recuperaci&oacute;n econ&oacute;mica de la crisis causada por la pandemia de COVID-19. 

&nbsp;

Muchos de los productos que se encuentran en evaluaci&oacute;n para la reducci&oacute;n de los L&iacute;mites M&aacute;ximos de Residuos (LMR) son actualmente cruciales para el manejo y control de plagas. Debido al clima tropical en pa&iacute;ses como el Ecuador, el comportamiento de las plagas sigue patrones muy distintos a los imperantes en pa&iacute;ses de cuatro estaciones como el de los Miembros de la Uni&oacute;n Europea (UE), por lo cual el uso de ciertas sustancias activas y sus formulados son indispensables en la producci&oacute;n agr&iacute;cola. La sustituci&oacute;n o reducci&oacute;n de dichas sustancias requieren tiempo y recursos de implementaci&oacute;n a fin de que los productores se adapten a los cambio en sus procesos productivos.

&nbsp;

No permitir de manera dr&aacute;stica el uso de estas sustancias y la reducci&oacute;n de los LMR, afectar&iacute;a &nbsp;la econom&iacute;a de los peque&ntilde;os, medianos y grandes productores en el Ecuador, as&iacute; como para los consumidores en la UE, que cuentan los &nbsp;productos con precios competitivos y alta calidad que provienen de pa&iacute;ses como el Ecuador.

&nbsp;

A continuaci&oacute;n transmito varios puntos a considerar con respecto a las sustancias comprendidas en el enfoque europeo sobre LMR:

&nbsp;

1)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; El chlorotalonil es la herramienta principal para el control de la Sigatoka negra en banano debido a su eficacia, bajo costo, modo de acci&oacute;n multisitio y bajo riesgo de resistencia.&nbsp; Por estas razones, se encuentra presente en una amplia gama de productos con diversas formulaciones, proveedores y extensa distribuci&oacute;n a nivel nacional.

&nbsp;

Nuestra preocupaci&oacute;n reside en que no solo no se ha renovado la autorizaci&oacute;n para el uso de chlorotalonil, sino que tambi&eacute;n se procedi&oacute; a establecer los LMR m&iacute;nimos por default, es decir 0,01ppm, conforme se se&ntilde;ala en el Reglamento (UE) 2021/155 notificado con la signatura G/SPS/N/EU/394/Add.1. En este documento se establecen igualmente LMR m&iacute;nimos por default para las sustancias activas tetracloruro de carbono, clorprofam, dimetoato, etoprofos, fenamidona, metiocarb, ometoato, propiconazol y pimetrocina.

&nbsp;

Nos ha llamado mucho la atenci&oacute;n que el antes mencionado Reglamento considere excepciones para productos como la patata que, a diferencia del banano, puede consumirse con c&aacute;scara y cuyo margen de tolerancia se mantiene en 0,4 ppm. Vale la pena mencionar que el chlorotalonil, seg&uacute;n la agencia internacional para investigaci&oacute;n sobre c&aacute;ncer, es una sustancia de categor&iacute;a 2, junto las carnes rojas y las bebidas calientes por sobre los 65&deg;C. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

La modificaci&oacute;n del LMR a niveles m&iacute;nimos de detecci&oacute;n anal&iacute;tica, impactar&iacute;a negativamente en la producci&oacute;n bananera del Ecuador. Cualquier afecci&oacute;n en la din&aacute;mica productiva de esta fruta impacta directamente y en gran proporci&oacute;n al desarrollo social. Reducir el LMR de chlorothalonil podr&iacute;a causar una afectaci&oacute;n muy alta en los costos de producci&oacute;n de los peque&ntilde;os, medianos y grandes productores en el Ecuador. En este momento no existen productos fitosanitarios sustitutos, similares o que tengan un perfil ambiental o toxicol&oacute;gico igual, ya que las otras alternativas al uso del chlorotalonil como el mancozeb y metiram, tambi&eacute;n han sido objeto de cuestionamientos sobre riesgos a la salud humana y al ambiente por parte de la UE.

&nbsp;

2)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; La sustancia activa mancozeb, cuya autorizaci&oacute;n no ha sido renovada, es un fungicida utilizado en cultivos estrat&eacute;gicos ecuatorianos como son banano, cacao, br&oacute;coli, pi&ntilde;a, pitahaya, mango, uvilla. La prohibici&oacute;n y consiguiente reducci&oacute;n del LMR para mancozeb deja sin herramientas fitosanitarias a los programas de manejo y control de la Sigatoka Negra. &nbsp;Estas circunstancias acarrean consecuencias muy nocivas desde el punto de vista ambiental y de sostenibilidad econ&oacute;mica para el cultivo de banano. &nbsp;El Ecuador solicita a la Uni&oacute;n Europea reconsiderar la no renovaci&oacute;n de la mol&eacute;cula mancozeb y mantener sus LMR dentro de los par&aacute;metros establecidos por el Codex Alimentarius para evitar posibles afectaciones al comercio y a la sostenibilidad econ&oacute;mica.

&nbsp;

3)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Preocupa tambi&eacute;n que a trav&eacute;s de la notificaci&oacute;n G/SPS/N/EU/360 se modifican los anexos II y V del Reglamento (CE) No. 396/2005 del Parlamento Europeo y del Consejo en lo que respecta a los LMR de clorpyrifos y clorpyrifos-metilo en determinados productos. El chlorpyrifos y chlorpyrifos-metilo son sustancias utilizadas para cultivos son producidos por Ecuador e importados por la UE como por ejemplo, banano, br&oacute;coli, pi&ntilde;a, caf&eacute;, lim&oacute;n, tomate de &aacute;rbol, cacao, entre otros. En el caso espec&iacute;fico de banano y dem&aacute;s mus&aacute;ceas, los insecticidas clorpirifos y el clorpirifos-metilo ese impregnan en bolsas pl&aacute;sticas que son colocadas en la fase de formaci&oacute;n del racimo y retiradas durante la cosecha.

&nbsp;

Es preciso recordar el profundo impacto econ&oacute;mico y social que pueden tener las inflexibles modificaciones de procesos productivos del banano, en adici&oacute;n a las consideraciones t&eacute;cnicas que se han expuesto:

El sector bananero aporta con plazas de trabajo para 2,5 millones de personas. Cada a&ntilde;o, 300 millones de cajas de banano viajan desde Ecuador para el consumo en todo el mundo. Las exportaciones significan 2,100 millones de ingresos para el pa&iacute;s. Esto es un aporte de 2% al Producto Interno Bruto (PIB) y el 35% al PIB agropecuario. El manejo de agroqu&iacute;micos en el cultivo de banano es adecuadamente controlado por las fincas que cuentan con una cultura de prevenci&oacute;n en los trabajadores, metodolog&iacute;a de ense&ntilde;anza-aprendizaje que les permite y promueve la autonom&iacute;a y la conciencia a trav&eacute;s de pr&aacute;cticas de salud ocupacional y ambiental a nivel de planificaci&oacute;n y finca.

Ecuador solicita a la Comisi&oacute;n Europea que previo a una decisi&oacute;n definitiva para aplicar este tipo de medidas, se consideren los datos cient&iacute;ficos existentes en los organismos especializados reconocidos por la OMC como el Codex Alimentarius, cuyo objeto es proteger la salud del consumidor y evitar al mismo tiempo eventuales afectaciones al sector productor de alimentos. Este pedido se fundamenta en las disposiciones del Acuerdo MSF de la OMC, las cuales establecen que al determinar el nivel adecuado de protecci&oacute;n sanitaria o fitosanitaria los Miembros deber&aacute;n minimizar los efectos negativos en el comercio, que las medidas sanitarias y fitosanitarias deber&aacute;n estar justificadas cient&iacute;fica y t&eacute;cnicamente, basadas en la evaluaci&oacute;n del riesgo y que evitar&aacute;n constituirse en obst&aacute;culos injustificados al comercio.

Para concluir me gustar&iacute;a se&ntilde;alar que la pr&aacute;ctica internacional ha mostrado que los agricultores requieren de un mayor tiempo que el actualmente disponible para adaptarse a las prescripciones sobre LMR, toda vez que el desarrollo de un nuevo producto fitosanitario para el control de plagas toma un periodo de 36 meses en promedio.&nbsp; El Ecuador insiste en que &nbsp;la Uni&oacute;n Europea considere un plazo de al menos 36 meses, tiempo m&iacute;nimo requerido para realizar los ajustes necesarios en la producci&oacute;n y permitir&aacute; garantizar a los pa&iacute;ses en desarrollo el cumplimiento de las nuevas condiciones establecidas en las regulaciones europeas.

EU MRLs and Pesticide Policies

March 2021 WTO SPS Committee Meeting - United States&#39; Statement

The United States thanks Paraguay, Colombia,&nbsp;Ecuador,&nbsp;Costa Rica for continuing to support inclusion of this important issue on the SPS Committee&rsquo;s agenda.

The United States would like to emphasize our shared concern for human, plant, animal, and environmental health.&nbsp; However, we are disappointed that the EU continues to lower many MRLs to trade-restrictive levels without clear scientific justification or measurable benefit to human health, and we remain concerned that the EU&rsquo;s hazard-based approach to pesticide regulation and implementation of the &ldquo;precautionary principle&rdquo; will lead to trade barriers that threaten the security of global food systems.

For example, we note G/SPS/N/EU/394, which was notified to this Committee on July 15, 2020.&nbsp; Despite&nbsp;a Codex proposal to increase the chlorothalonil MRL for cranberries, the EU has not renewed the substance for use, and reduced the MRL to the limit of quantification or LOQ.&nbsp; In the same notification, and despite established Codex MRLs, the EU also reduced MRLs to the LOQ for&nbsp;fenamidone, a foliar fungicide used on many crops including leafy green vegetables and brassicas, and propiconazole, a systemic foliar fungicide used on mushrooms, corn, wild rice, peanuts, almonds, sorghum, oats, pecans, apricots, peaches, nectarines, plums and prunes.

The United States notes that pesticides can be an important component in integrated pest management (IPM) programs, and that unnecessarily removing active ingredients and modes of action can actually increase the development of resistance to pesticides, which adds an additional challenge for agricultural producers to face and overcome.

Along this vein, the United States would again like to raise our concern regarding the substance clethodim.&nbsp; In its justification for new MRLs, the EU indicated that it could not determine the genotoxic capacity of the clethodim metabolite-3 chloroallyl alcohol. Since the consumer risk assessment could not be completed, the MRLs were proposed at the LOQ .&nbsp; Although EFSA stated they could not rule out genotoxic potential, the United States would like to note that in the 2009 &ldquo;Conclusion on the peer review of the pesticide risk assessment of the active substance (EZ)-1,3-dichloropropene1&rdquo; it was noted that for the metabolite 3-chloroallyl alcohol there was no &ldquo;genotoxic potential&rdquo;[1].&nbsp;

In response to inconclusive genotoxicity potential for the metabolite, the applicant completed requested studies.&nbsp; From these data, the Rapporteur Member State (Sweden) indicated that the &ldquo;Maximum total dose (MTD) was not proved to be achieved.&rdquo;&nbsp;

While further data is collected, we would like to remind the EU that both the United States and Codex have existing MRLs for clethodim.&nbsp; Clethodim is a useful tool for both EU and U.S. farmers, and it is important for soybean, cranberry, rapeseed, peanut, corn, blueberry, and hop production.&nbsp; We further remind the EU that Article 5 of the WTO SPS Agreement requires members to consider available scientific information and the objective of minimizing trade affects.

We would also like to emphasize our concern that a 6-month transition period for clethodim MRLs is insufficient for producers to adapt production methods for newly produced goods to clear EU customs.&nbsp; The key products to which clethodim is applied tend to have longer shelf lives and may not clear trade channels within a 6-month transition period.

Finally, we recognize that EFSA is currently reviewing ten (10) Member States&rsquo; emergency authorizations for neonicotinoids, which are currently banned within the EU, including clothianidin, imidacloprid, and thiamethoxam. It would appear as though producers in the EU view these products as integral components of their IPM programs and, accordingly, we request that the EU consider affording producers in third countries equal access to these pesticides.

Overall, the United States continues to be concerned with the EU&rsquo;s MRL risk assessment process.&nbsp; The EU previously noted in its response to G/SPS/GEN/1847 in reference to the determination by European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) if an MRL is safe for consumers, &ldquo;This requires that a minimum of supporting data is provided in line with the EU data requirements allowing EFSA to reach a conclusion on the safety of the MRL. If such supporting evidence is lacking or insufficient, EFSA cannot conclude that the MRL is safe.&rdquo;

This reasoning suggests that recent MRL decisions, including those for fenamidone, propiconazole, chlorothalonil, and clethodim are based on incomplete risk assessments and are thus provisional. We ask the EU to clarify&nbsp; the provisional nature of these recent MRL decisions, and confirm that scientific data, including from Codex, will be collected and analyzed to maintain these measures, consistent with the SPS Agreement.

We note that this is a consistent issue in ongoing pesticide reviews.&nbsp; While the EU has not yet lowered MRLs for extoxazole, G/SPS/N/EU/408 indicates that the substance will not be renewed based on what appears to be an incomplete risk assessment.&nbsp; EFSA did not finalize its consumer dietary risk assessment for processed commodities and the fate of persistent soil metabolites in rotational crops, nor did it complete the isomerization for dietary risk assessment.&nbsp; While the EU states it will take additional information into account after the expiration of the grace period for uses on edible crops and prior to lowering MRLs, a &ldquo;stop the clock&rdquo; approach, that allows EFSA to gather the needed data and keep a temporary MRL in place until the process is completed, would facilitate continued safe trade.&nbsp; &nbsp;

Finally, the United States would again like to raise the manner in which the EU enforces newly reduced MRLs. &nbsp;The EU enforces new reduced MRLs at the point of production for domestic goods, and at point of importation for imported goods.&nbsp; This causes trade inefficiencies and disruptions for products destined for the EU market depending on when a new reduced MRL is enforced and results in both an inconsistent application of the SPS measure and an unfair advantage for EU producers.&nbsp;

Moving forward, we are increasingly concerned with the politicization of MRLs, particularly as evidenced in the EUs Farm to Fork Strategy, Biodiversity Strategy, and the Pesticide REFIT.&nbsp; We believe that science, not politics, should inform development of SPS measures and we look forward to productive engagement around the issues raised above.

On a positive note, we would like to thank the European Commission for the invitation to attend the Third Country Information Session on January 20, 2021 on the consideration of environmental factors in the MRL setting process.&nbsp; We look forward to continued engagement in this area and encourage the EU to continue facilitating dialogues with third countries in this context.

&nbsp;

[1]Conclusion on the peer review of the pesticide risk assessment of the active substance (EZ)-1,3-dichloropropene1.&nbsp; Conclusion on pesticide review.&nbsp; https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.2903/j.efsa.2009.1341

Gracias Se&ntilde;or Presidente, mi delegaci&oacute;n quisiera agradecer a la UE y a sus delegados en Ginebra por su permanente inter&eacute;s y disposici&oacute;n a discutir este tema.&nbsp;Agradecemos tambi&eacute;n el documento revisado explicativo sobre el procedimiento de determinaci&oacute;n de LMR en la Uni&oacute;n Europea. Adicionalmente, Colombia agradece a la UE por su disposici&oacute;n a responder las preguntas en el marco de nuestras preocupaciones comerciales de LMR bajo la signatura GEN1926.

Colombia ha manifestado su preocupaci&oacute;n respecto de la base cient&iacute;fica de las determinaci&oacute;n de los LMR, en particular a la hora de elegir las sustancias que se revisan, a la hora de permitir la intervenci&oacute;n de interesados, a la hora de establecer criterios como la forma de consumo de un alimento, a la hora de desconocer las condiciones geogr&aacute;ficas y clim&aacute;ticas distintas de los pa&iacute;ses, en especial en &aacute;reas tropicales, y, sobre todo, en la aplicaci&oacute;n precautoria de nuevos LMR ante la ausencia de informaci&oacute;n suficiente para la determinaci&oacute;n del riesgo. En tal sentido, hoy reiteramos todos esos argumentos y nuestras intervenciones anteriores.&nbsp;

Como una manera de avanzar y de hacer evolucionar el discurso, hoy centraremos nuestra intervenci&oacute;n en el mundo de las derogaciones, excepciones o flexibilidades existentes en la UE frente a los problemas anteriores. En efecto, adem&aacute;s de los problemas del procedimiento de adopci&oacute;n de LMRs, nos encontramos que los mecanismos de excepci&oacute;n, que permiten seguir usando determinados productos y sustancias para los productores, desnivelan m&aacute;s la cancha.&nbsp;

Hay una excepci&oacute;n de enormes proporciones, muy f&aacute;cil de usar, reservada para los productores internos, de nombre &ldquo;Autorizaci&oacute;n de emergencia&rdquo;, y una excepci&oacute;n cada vez mas limitada, a disposici&oacute;n de los pa&iacute;ses que exportan hacia la UE, llamada de &ldquo;Tolerancias de importaci&oacute;n&rdquo;; generando una diferencia&nbsp;entre&nbsp;los dos mecanismos, pese a tratarse de situaciones que hacen frente b&aacute;sicamente a las mismas realidades o problemas.

En efecto, las razones por las cuales se otorgan excepciones a los productores internos en la UE son exactamente las mismas que hemos presentado en todos los Foros -desde nuestros pa&iacute;ses-: la existencia de una plaga incontrolable, la inexistencia de alternativas eficaces, la posibilidad de establecer medidas de mitigaci&oacute;n. Y, sin embargo, la excepci&oacute;n interna se otorga siempre y la excepci&oacute;n for&aacute;nea casi nunca, y cada vez menos.

El procedimiento para el otorgamiento de una autorizaci&oacute;n de emergencia en la UE es sencillo. Se trata de un examen solicitado por los usuarios del producto de protecci&oacute;n de las plantas, y no por el laboratorio productor.&nbsp; En algunos casos las autorizaciones se han renovado varias veces, incluso ilimitadamente. Adem&aacute;s, el r&eacute;gimen de control de dichos productos parece difuso con posterioridad a la derogaci&oacute;n otorgada.&nbsp;

El procedimiento, en cambio, para una tolerancia a la importaci&oacute;n es dif&iacute;cil y, con el paso del tiempo, se hace cada vez mas estricto.&nbsp; A pesar de basarse en los mismos argumentos y condiciones f&aacute;cticas, el r&eacute;gimen de qui&eacute;n puede aplicar, debe mostrar y debe probar, es radicalmente distinto. Lo mismo ocurre con su temporalidad y con el r&eacute;gimen de control que se le aplica.&nbsp;En la Uni&oacute;n Europea, los productores europeos se han visto favorecidos por 1934 autorizaciones de emergencia en cuatro a&ntilde;os, desde que la regulaci&oacute;n fue expedida. En cambio, solo 61 tolerancias a la importaci&oacute;n han sido concedidas a productores extranjeros desde 2017, en el mismo periodo de tiempo (solo 16 para productores de pa&iacute;ses en desarrollo).

Ante esta situaci&oacute;n, Colombia reitera que las normas de la OMC no permiten un posible trato menos favorable a las importaciones de otros miembros de la OMC en contraste de los productos internos. Los productores extranjeros no pueden ser expuestos a una condici&oacute;n de desventaja frente a competidores. Por lo que un mecanismo de derogaci&oacute;n deber&iacute;a ser equivalente y esa es una de nuestras solicitudes.

Para finalizar, quisi&eacute;ramos llamar la atenci&oacute;n sobre la adaptaci&oacute;n de los pa&iacute;ses productores frente a medidas de inocuidad que vinculan la reducci&oacute;n de los LMR de sustancias activas, dificultando de esta manera el control de plagas y enfermedades -de afectaci&oacute;n econ&oacute;mica-. Reemplazar una mol&eacute;cula es una actividad que demanda tiempo, investigaci&oacute;n y recursos financieros importantes para los pa&iacute;ses&nbsp; en desarrollo, y la mayor&iacute;a de ellos, no cuentan con la capacidad para atender estas situaciones. Ejemplo de ello: el glifosato cuesta 4&nbsp;euros/hect&aacute;rea, mientras las nuevas mol&eacute;culas que podr&iacute;an reemplazarla cuestan m&aacute;s de 50euros/hect&aacute;rea. Estos sobrecostos son completamente inviables en nuestros pa&iacute;ses. Y todo esto en un contexto intuitivo de diferencias geogr&aacute;ficas donde la cercan&iacute;a a los tr&oacute;picos hace que exista una prevalencia mayor de plagas que en zonas templadas.

Insistimos una vez m&aacute;s entonces en la necesidad del establecimiento de un mecanismo estructurado y amplio de di&aacute;logo plurilateral, en paralelo a las discusiones bilaterales, para buscar soluciones constructivas y sustanciales a esta cancha desnivelada.

Gracias se&ntilde;or Presidente, mi delegaci&oacute;n agradece a la delegaci&oacute;n de Colombia por la introducci&oacute;n de la preocpacion que hemos presentado cojuntamete a la UE, reiteramos ademas que hemos presetado preguntas por escrito en el documento G/SPS/GEN/1926 y esperamos poder contar con las respuestas por escrito a la mayor brevedad posible. Tomamos nota tambi&eacute;n del documento G/SPS/GEN/1494/Rev.2. Asimismo, agradecemos a la UE por las recientes reuniones bilaterales que hemos mantenido y que esperamos puedan arrojar soluciones concretas a estas preocupaciones que contin&uacute;an sin resolverse a pesar de estos esfuerzos, que notamos son aun incipientes.

Las soluciones a estos problemas ser&aacute;n encontradas &uacute;nicamente de manera conjunta, en un esp&iacute;ritu de dialogo colaborativo, que involucre a todas las partes interesadas. Para ello ser&aacute; necesaria la demostraci&oacute;n de flexibilidad, y una genuina predisposici&oacute;n a escuchar y entender las preocupaciones presentadas, y un trabajo desarrollado en modo de b&uacute;squeda de soluciones concretas.

La repetici&oacute;n de ciertas posiciones no ser&aacute; conducente a este trabajo de b&uacute;squeda de soluciones. A modo de ejemplo, notamos que la UE reitera en numerosas ocasiones que su nivel de alineaci&oacute;n con el Codex es elevado. En este sentido, notamos que el nivel de alienaci&oacute;n no es lo relevante en esta discusi&oacute;n. Sino los motivos por los cuales la UE se desv&iacute;a de estos estandares y&nbsp;la evidencia cient&iacute;fica que justifica esta desviaci&oacute;n.

Notamos adem&aacute;s con preocupaci&oacute;n, al igual que Colombia, la proliferaci&oacute;n de las autorizaciones de emergencia de los Estados Miembros de la UE para las mismas sustancias que son objeto de esta y otras preocupaciones comerciales. Las muchas de ellas presentan argumentos similares a los de las numerosas delegaciones que han expresado su preocupaci&oacute;n por las pol&iacute;ticas de la UE respecto a los productos de protecci&oacute;n de plantas o simplemente se encuentran en blanco. Sin embargo, son otorgadas de igual modo. Mientras que las tolerancias de las importaciones deben pasar por un largo proceso y sin garant&iacute;a alguna de su aprobaci&oacute;n.&nbsp;

Esperamos poder continuar abordando este tema y otros relativos a nuestras preocupaciones de manera constructiva, en un modo orientado a soluciones en nuestras discusiones.&nbsp;

Muchas gracias

Gracias Se&ntilde;or Presidente,&nbsp;

Mi delegaci&oacute;n quisiera agradecer a la UE y a sus delegados en Ginebra por su inter&eacute;s y disposici&oacute;n a discutir este tema. &nbsp;Lamentamos que desde la introducci&oacute;n de este &iacute;tem en la agenda por primera vez y hasta la fecha, no se haya podido avanzar en la sustancia de manera significativa como para levantar este &iacute;tem de la agenda. Lamentamos tambi&eacute;n que no hemos recibido respuesta a nuestro ultimo set de preguntas, formulado entre varios pa&iacute;ses, hace ya seis meses.&nbsp;

&nbsp;Hoy vamos a resumir nuestra posici&oacute;n y ser mas expl&iacute;citos en el aspecto de implementaci&oacute;n de la pol&iacute;tica europea, y no tanto en su naturaleza y decisi&oacute;n politica. En efecto, hay que resaltar que la pol&iacute;tica de LMR de la Uni&oacute;n Europea, al margen de sus principios orientadores, de sus justificaciones y objetivos de pol&iacute;tica publica, es simplemente discriminatoria en su implementaci&oacute;n.&nbsp;

De hecho, mas all&aacute; de si se comparten o no sus presuntos objetivos, la forma en la que la pol&iacute;tica opera en la practica es fuertemente diferenciada entre productores locales y extranjeros.&nbsp;

HAREMOS UNA ENUMERACION:

PRIMERO, Colombia ha dicho que el proceso de determinaci&oacute;n de un LMR en la UE resulta discriminatorio a la hora de elegir las sustancias que se revisan. En efecto, a la hora de escoger qu&eacute; sustancias evaluar cada a&ntilde;o, hay un ejercicio natural de priorizaci&oacute;n y selecci&oacute;n que realiza la Union Euriopea. Y por supuesto se escoge evaluar algunas sustancias por encima de otras, teniendo en mente el impacto en sus propias constituencies, no en las extranjeras. As&iacute;, ese ejercicio de selecci&oacute;n bien puede terminar por dejar de lado algunas sustancias que afectarian de manera onerosa a sus propios productores agr&iacute;colas, o bien podr&iacute;an escoger mas f&aacute;cilmente sustancias que ya perdieron la proteccion de su propiedad intelectual, de modo que este caso se protegen los laboratorios productores de dichas sustancias cerrando la puerta de potenciales competidores gen&eacute;ricos en el extranjero. En ese primer proceso de selecci&oacute;n, realizado a puerta cerrada y con espacios para la arbitrariedad, hay un espacio enorme de protecci&oacute;n comercial interna.&nbsp;

SEGUNDO, tambi&eacute;n se discrimina al establecer LMR distintos para la misma sustancia, pero utilizada en un producto eminentemente europeo versus uno extranjero.&nbsp; Solo el caso ilustrativo de la cascara de banano tratada con imazalil, considerada como peligrosa, mientras que la cascara de la naranja, tratada con imazalil, considerada como no peligrosa. Y no hay que ser un experto para notar que la cascara de naranja se suele comer, mientras que la de banano no. En la selecci&oacute;n de productos para revisi&oacute;n, tambi&eacute;n hay un trato inequitativo que deber&iacute;a ser igualitario.

TERCERO, a la hora de permitir la participaci&oacute;n de interesados, el procedimiento europeo admite multiples instancias de intervencion de sus paises miembros y de sus stakeholders, mientras que los demas nos vemos abocados a intervenir solo cuando la decisi&oacute;n llega a la OMC, es decir cuando un consenso intraeuropeo ya ha sido negociado y alcanzado. De ahi que a pesar de las notificaciones y comentarios, nunca haya cambiado ni una sola medida relativa a los LMR. Los comentarios de los productores europeos y de los pa&iacute;ses europeos ocurren en un momento del proceso donde tienen efectividad, mientras que los comentarios de productores o pa&iacute;ses extranjeros ocurren cuando la decisi&oacute;n ya esta tomada. No hay igualdad de trato.

CUARTO, la implementacion de la politica europea tiene poderosas derogaciones, waivers y carve outs&nbsp; que reconocen las condiciones geogr&aacute;ficas y clim&aacute;ticas distintas de los pa&iacute;ses, asi como la prevalencia localizada de pestes. Para esas situaciones existe un procedimiento simple de waiver, de carve out, que permite a los agricultroes europeos seguir utilizando la sustancia, y venderla en todo el mercado europeo y exportarla sin controles adecuados. Ninguna de esas circunstancias geograficas y climaticas es adecuadamente reconocida como valida para productos extranjeros. En especial en &aacute;reas tropicales, las pestes son prevalentes y distintas. No hay que ser tampoco un experto para notar que las temperaturas mas altas tienen efectos sobre&nbsp;una prevalencia mayor de pestes que en zonas templadas. Ahora bien,&nbsp;estos waivers sencillos y practicos, con escaso control, llamados autorizaciones de emergencia, no existen para productos extranjeros. Para los extranjeros, existe un esquema dispensioso, a menudo imposible de cumplir, y que es cada vez mas estricto. Un mecanismo de derogaci&oacute;n deber&iacute;a ser equivalente, igual de estricto o de suave, y esa es una de nuestras razonables solicitudes.

La politica de LMR entonces, con sus loables objetivos verdes de proteccion de la salud humana y de conservacion de la biodiversidad, que Colombia comparte plenamente, se estrella contra un escenario de implementaci&oacute;n fuertemente desnivelado, donde unos deben cumplir con unas reglas muy onerosas, sin posibilidad de discutirlas, mientras que otros tienen la posibilidad de influir, decidir e incluso de exceptuar medidas conversadas por ellos mismos.&nbsp; Esto no es un tema de salud o medio ambiente, sino de simple proteccionismo en la implementaci&oacute;n de una pol&iacute;tica.

[A este panorama algo sombrio, debe agregarse finalmente&nbsp; la gigantesca capacidad de subsidiar que tiene la UE para lograr la adaptacion a cualquier estandar por parte de sus productores, que otros no tenemos. A un problema de estandares desnivelados, se agrega otro de chequeras desniveladas].&nbsp;

&nbsp;Quiero ser muy claro en esto: todo lo anterior ocurre sin cuestionar las bases de la pol&iacute;tica misma.&nbsp;Colombia tambi&eacute;n ha cuestionado la base cient&iacute;fica de dichas determinaciones y, sobre todo, la aplicaci&oacute;n precautoria de nuevos LMR ante la ausencia de informaci&oacute;n negativa sobre sus efectos. Pero hoy nos estamos centrando en la implementacion desnivelada y discriminatoria de dichos estandares.

Por eso Insistimos una vez m&aacute;s entonces en la necesidad del establecimiento de un mecanismo estructurado y amplio de dialogo plurilateral, en paralelo a las discusiones bilaterales, para buscar soluciones constructivas y sustanciales a esta cancha desnivelada. Colombia propone los siguientes caminos, que consideramos razonables y aceptables para la Union Europea, que no le exigen cambiar su pol&iacute;tica ni sus reglamentos, sin perjuicio de que nos reservamos los derechos sobre este segundo aspecto. Dichas muestras de esp&iacute;ritu constructivo podr&iacute;an verse reflejadas en la adopci&oacute;n de las siguientes medidas, entre otras:

a) Se cree un mecanismo espejo de autorizaci&oacute;n de importaciones, estrictamente equivalente a la existencia de autorizaciones de emergencia dentro de la UE que permiten seguir utilizando productos con sustancias no autorizadas. O se permita el acceso a las autorizaciones de emergencia en los mismos t&eacute;rminos que se le permite a los productores europeos.

c) Se permitan periodos de transici&oacute;n m&aacute;s largos, especialmente en los casos en los que no existen alternativas viables para las sustancias no renovadas en el mercado.

d) Se haga una revisi&oacute;n conjunta del procedimiento de autorizaci&oacute;n de una sustancia para su comercializaci&oacute;n, para asegurar la participaci&oacute;n efectiva de los pa&iacute;ses afectados, en t&eacute;rminos de buenas pr&aacute;cticas regulatorias, mucho antes de que las notificaciones lleguen a la OMC.

&nbsp;a) Se mantengan los LMR del Codex en los casos en que la evidencia cient&iacute;fica no sea concluyente, mientras se completa la informaci&oacute;n cient&iacute;fica. En los casos en los que no haya LMR del Codex, la UE podr&iacute;a imponer un LMR fijo por defecto distinto al del 0,01%, que cumpla con su objetivo pero no sea mas restrictivo del comercio de lo necesario.

Estas son solo unas ideas que creemos constructivas y plausibles para todos. Lograr un arreglo requiere de voluntad pol&iacute;tica y mucho di[alogo, al cual estamos plenamente dispuestos.

Muchas gracias se&ntilde;or presidente.

&nbsp;

Mi delegaci&oacute;n quisiera reiterar su preocupaci&oacute;n por la no renovaci&oacute;n y modificaci&oacute;n de LMRs de la sustancia MANCOZEB por parte de la UE. Como se ha manifestado en ocasiones anteriores, el MANCOZEB es un fungicida de uso com&uacute;n en Paraguay, utilizado para combatir m&aacute;s de 400 enfermedades, en m&aacute;s de 70 cultivos, incluyendo varios rubros de exportaci&oacute;n a la UE como la soja, el ma&iacute;z. Su uso especifico para combatir la Phakopsora pachyrhizi, Roya asi&aacute;tica y la Phaeosphaeria maydis, de la mancha marr&oacute;n respectivamente.

Su eliminaci&oacute;n en la rotaci&oacute;n de sustancias durante el ciclo productivo, as&iacute; como el clorotalonil, generaran grandes problemas para los productores paraguayos que deben enfrentar presiones de plagas y enfermedades muy distintas a los productores europeos, por las condiciones clim&aacute;ticas y geogr&aacute;ficas.

Instamos una vez m&aacute;s a la UE a reconsiderar su enfoque regulatorio y a asegurar tolerancias a las importaciones, basadas en evidencia cient&iacute;fica y procedimientos del Codex Alimentarius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) New Requirements for Animal Feed, Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 (dated 27 January 2020) (STC 479)

November 2020 WTO SPS Committee - Statement by the United States


	The United States remains concerned with India&rsquo;s new directive on animal feed, issued on January 27, 2020.&nbsp; We anticipate that the directive will have a significant impact on trade in feed ingredients, and possibly on meat and dairy products from livestock fed in other countries and imported by India.
	The directive contains two parts. Part one of the directive make mandatory the formerly voluntary guidance for animal feed, to implement the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) specifications for Compounded Feeds for Cattle (IS 2052:2009).&nbsp; Part one has not been notified to the WTO SPS Committee and, to date, WTO Members have not had an opportunity to comment on this regulation.&nbsp; Part two of the directive was notified to the SPS Committee as G/SPS/N/IND/249 on February 26, 2020.&nbsp;
	The United States appreciates that India provided a six-month implementation period for members to comply with the directive. &nbsp;However, given the current COVID-19 pandemic and its impact on the preparedness of all stakeholders, in addition to the fact that India has not yet notified part one of the directive to the SPS Committee, we ask that India suspend implementation until India notifies these mandatory requirements and responds to questions and concerns raised by members including those of the United States.
	As we noted during the June 2020 SPS Committee Meeting, part one of the directive appears to make mandatory a guidance document from 2009 titled, &ldquo;Indian Standard, Compounded Feeds for Cattle - Specification.&rdquo;&nbsp; According to this document, India only allows import of those feed types listed in the document.
	This list omits several feed ingredients commonly used around the world, such as distillers&rsquo; dried grains with solubles (DDGS), alfalfa, timothy hay, and several types of vitamin and mineral feed supplements.&nbsp;
	It appears that this list has not been updated since 2009to include new or different feed types. &nbsp;We again request that India please clarify the criteria used to compile the list and the process by which other types of feed can be added to the list.
	Part two of the directive also includes language that amends requirements for meat and meat products as detailed in the 2011 FSSAI Food Product Standards and Food Additives regulations.&nbsp; This text appears to link feed and the acceptability of meat and meat products from cattle fed with certain types of feed.&nbsp; The text states that milk- and meat-producing animals, except poultry and fish, shall not be fed with feed containing bovine- or swine-origin materials except for milk/milk products.&nbsp;
	The United States raised concerns with these regulations during previous SPS and TBT Committee meetings, and we have not received a response or further explanation on this issue from India. We again request that India please provide further information to the United States on these important matters.

INDIA -&nbsp; Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) New Requirements for Animal Feed, Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 (dated 27 January 2020)

March 2021 WTO SPS Committee&nbsp; -&nbsp; United States&#39; Statement

The United States remains concerned with India&rsquo;s new directive on animal feed, issued on January 27, 2020.&nbsp; We anticipate that the directive will have a significant impact on trade in feed ingredients, and possibly on meat and dairy products from livestock fed in other countries and imported by India.

The directive contains two parts. Part one of the directive makes mandatory the formerly voluntary guidance for animal feed, to implement the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) specifications for Compounded Feeds for Cattle. Part one has not been notified to the WTO SPS Committee and, to date, WTO Members have not had an opportunity to comment on this regulation.&nbsp;

As we noted during the June and November 2020 SPS Committee Meetings, according to the directive, India only allows import of those feed types listed in the directive, and this list omits several feed ingredients commonly used around the world, such as distillers&rsquo; dried grains with solubles (DDGS), alfalfa, timothy hay, and several types of vitamin and mineral feed supplements.&nbsp;

India noted in the November SPS Committee meeting that it was willing to work with the United States to clarify the process by which feed ingredients may be added to the official list, and we thank the Government of India for this offer of cooperation.&nbsp;

While we have received an informal response from India regarding this list, we wish to reiterate that guidance on this issue must be in writing and outline predictable, non-discriminatory, and scientifically sound methodology for adding new feed ingredients.&nbsp; Thus far we have received no such guidance.&nbsp; We also request for India to submit a notification on part one to the SPS Committee.

Part two of the directive was notified to the SPS Committee as G/SPS/N/IND/249 on February 26, 2020.&nbsp; Part two of the directive includes language that amends requirements for meat and meat products as detailed in the 2011 FSSAI Food Product Standards and Food Additives regulations.&nbsp;

This text appears to link feed and the acceptability of meat and meat products from cattle fed with certain types of feed.&nbsp; It is our understanding that meat-producing animals must be fed only the 49 feed ingredients approved in the BIS standard. We ask that India please confirm this understanding, and provide the scientific basis for such a requirement.

Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) New Requirements for Animal Feed, Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 (dated 27 January 2020)

July 2021 WTO SPS Committee

&nbsp;


	The United States again raises concerns with India&rsquo;s directive on animal feed, which was issued on January 27, 2020.


&nbsp;


	These concerns are documented in G/SPS/GEN/1804, G/SPS/GEN/1866 and G/SPS/GEN/1898 and in the Committee&rsquo;s summary reports of the previous meetings.


&nbsp;


	As we noted during the June and November 2020 and March 2021 SPS Committee meetings, according to the directive, India only allows import of the 49 feed types listed in the directive and this list omits several commonly used feed ingredients, such as distillers&rsquo; dried grains with solubles, alfalfa, timothy hay, and several types of vitamin and mineral feed supplements.


&nbsp;


	India noted in the November 2020 and March 2021 SPS Committee meetings that it was willing to work with the United States to clarify the process by which feed ingredients may be added to the official list, and we thank the Government of India for its willingness to cooperate and look forward to bilateral engagement.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	While we appreciate India&rsquo;s general response regarding this list, we continue to request the written process outlining a transparent and science-based methodology for adding new feed ingredients to the list.&nbsp; Further, we again request the Government of India to delay implementation of the measure until the specific written process is notified and Member comments on the proposed measure have been taken into consideration.

China Draft Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods (STC 485)

November 2020 WTO SPS Committee Meeting - Statement from the United States

&nbsp;


	The United States remains concerned with China&rsquo;s draft &ldquo;Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods&rdquo; (Administrative Measures) and urges China to reconsider implementing this restrictive, unnecessary and burdensome regulation.


&nbsp;


	The draft measures appear as though they may apply to all food products, including both low-risk products and products that are already accompanied by health and safety certificates issued by foreign government competent authorities.


&nbsp;


	The draft measures also appear as though they would require foreign competent authorities to confirm that manufacturers are in continuous compliance with China&rsquo;s laws, regulations, and standards. We request that China provide its technical justification for these requirements and explain how they are related to China&rsquo;s level of protection.


&nbsp;


	We anticipate that the draft measures, if implemented, would likely create major trade disruptions for many countries that export food and agricultural products to China, including developing countries whose competent authorities may have limited capacity to meet China&rsquo;s proposed requirements.


&nbsp;


	In previous SPS and TBT Committee meetings, China indicated that it was still drafting these measures, and we request that China provide its timeline for completing and notifying the draft measures to the WTO.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	We also reiterate our request that China reconsider the necessity of these measures and that it work with WTO Members in a transparent, inclusive, science-based manner when developing measures that affect international trade.

The EU commented on draft texts before and after they were notified to WTO under G/TBT/N/CHN/1522 and G/SPS/N/CHN/1191. It is regrettable that Decrees 248 and 249 were published by the General Administration of Customs of the People&#39;s Republic of China (GACC) without any reaction or consideration to our comments.

China and the EU trade high volumes of food products and beverages through tens of thousands enterprises, some big, many small. Given the dimension of the task, for an effective registration of businesses, clear guidance, standardised template forms and realistic transition periods are indispensable in order to minimise disruptions to economic relationships between enterprises on both sides.

Accordingly, the EU urges GACC to:


	develop detailed guidelines, implementing rules and template forms in cooperation with stakeholders and notify these elements through WTO channels for comment;
	clearly define the product categories by their HS numbers that must register under the &#39;registration with recommendation&#39; procedure under Article 7 of Decree 248;
	define the types of operations that must register. It is the EU&#39;s understanding that the exporter of consumer products must register; it is unclear whether exporters of products shipped in bulk from business to business &ndash; such as dairy powders &ndash; are also required to register;
	provide for realistic and practicable implementation and transition periods of at least 18 months after detailed guidance is available. Enterprises that are already registered and listed by GACC today should be exempt from the immediate implementation of the measures. For example, enterprises approved in 2018 should remain approved for five years;
	Once registrations are approved and registration numbers are allocated, provisions related to labelling under Article 15 must be implemented with adequate transition periods. In particular products with a long shelf life, such as spirits, may be in retail stocks for many months and must be protected by transition periods of at least 36 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

China&#39;s Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported&nbsp;Foods and Administrative Measures on Import and Export Food Safety&nbsp;(G/TBT/N/CHN/1522) (G/SPS/CHN/1191) (STC 485)

July 2021 WTO SPS Committee

&nbsp;


	The United States understands that China recently published the final versions of the draft measures notified respectively to the TBT and SPS Committees as Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods &ndash; TBT/CHN/1522 (Administrative Registration Measures) &ndash; also published as Decree 248; and Administrative Measures on Import and Export Food Safety &ndash; SPS/CHN/1191 (Administrative Import Measures) &ndash; also published as Decree 249.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	We note that both measures work together to describe food facility registration requirements. &nbsp;Decree 249 appears to be the over-arching regulation for imported and exported food products, and Decree 248, the implementing measure for food facility registration. &nbsp;Thus, we are addressing both Decrees together in this intervention.


&nbsp;


	First, we would like to thank China for the opportunity that was provided to submit comments to these measures, and we appreciate the responses we have received from China.&nbsp; However, we are concerned that these Decrees would mandate additional certification, audit, and inspection documents and procedures beyond what is already required for higher-risk products, which will likely create major trade disruptions for many countries that export food and agricultural products to China.


&nbsp;


	To that end, we request that China identify the specific food safety risks it is attempting to address through these measures and explain why these risks are not mitigated by China&rsquo;s existing regulations. &nbsp;If further SPS measures are scientifically justified, China could then consider a risk-based systems approach to determine which procedures may be required for individual trading partners and product groups to meet China&rsquo;s appropriate level of protection


&nbsp;


	Regardless of the approach taken, the proposed implementation date of January 1, 2022 is not feasible or realistic, especially when considering the complexity of the proposed measures and their likely significant effect on international agricultural trade. &nbsp;As such, we request that China postpone implementation of these measures and continue engaging with trading partners to address China&rsquo;s concerns and identify a mutually satisfactory path forward.


&nbsp;

	Thank you, Chair.


&nbsp;


	Canada has been following with interest the development of both Decrees and provided comments on draft texts notified to the WTO under G/TBT/N/CHN/1522 and G/SPS/N/CHN/1191.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	&nbsp;
	This information is necessary to ensure that industry stakeholders and foreign competent authorities understand and are able to comply with the requirements.


&nbsp;


	These measures are broad and over-arching in scope, and will have a significant impact on exports to China.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Over the years, Canada and China have implemented many bilateral arrangements that have successfully reinforced and provided food safety assurances and protections for consumers in our respective nations.


&nbsp;


	As such, Canada remains concerned that the administrative measures being implemented by China are overly burdensome and unjustified as they go beyond the extent necessary to protect against food safety risks.


&nbsp;


	Canada is also disappointed that the Decrees were adopted and published by General Administration of Customs of the People&#39;s Republic of China (GACC) immediately following the comment deadline without sufficiently taking into account comments from Members.


&nbsp;


	Canada is troubled by these measures as it remains unclear how they will contribute to achieving China&rsquo;s appropriate level of protection. If implemented in their current form, these measures will likely create serious barriers to trade including significant financial impacts on both industry and foreign competent authorities and will constrain trade.


&nbsp;


	Canada has requested numerous bilateral discussions with China to further understand how these requirements will be applied.


&nbsp;


	Canada is deeply concerned that these measures will create confusion for competent authorities and industry due to the lack of details and transparency from China regarding the implementation of these new requirements.


&nbsp;


	Canada urges China to delay the implementation of Decrees 248 and 249 for 18 months to allow both governments and industry to fully understand and comply with the new requirements contained in the Decrees.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada calls on China to provide further clarity and hold discussions with Members in a timely manner so there is sufficient time for trading partners, including both competent authorities and industry, to comply with China&rsquo;s new measures.


&nbsp;


	Specifically regarding these Decrees, Canada remains concerned that these measures provide additional oversight and requirements that appear to be more trade restrictive than necessary and do not appear to be based on an assessment of risks taking into account the risk assessment techniques developed by a relevant international organization.


&nbsp;


	In particular, Canada requests further clarity from China regarding the scope of the products impacted by the Decrees as the measures appear to apply to all food and food products including those that have inherently low food safety risk due to low water activity or processing methods.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canadian exports of shelf stable products and products with low safety risk, such as canola oil, malted grains and liquor, will be significantly impacted by these measures, which do not appear to be based on an assessment of risk that takes into account the characteristics of these products.


&nbsp;


	Additionally, Canada requests an explanation for the need of all imported food and food products to be registered with China.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	The Canadian Food Inspection Agency verifies that Canadian establishments comply with Canadian legislation as well as China&rsquo;s import requirements.


&nbsp;


	The additional licensing, inspections and approval procedures being requested by China are unjustified, overly burdensome and go beyond what is required to ensure the safety of exported food.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Furthermore, Canada requests that China explain how these measures relate to Decree 177 on the import/export of grain. The need to be registered on separate lists seems to be more trade-restrictive than necessary.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	In conclusion, Canada urges China to provide Members with additional information and clarification on the implementation of Decree 248 and 249 and delay implementation of these requirements to provide sufficient time to address this uncertainly and allow trading partners to comply with the Decrees.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Thank you, Chair.

Australia recognises the right of&nbsp;Members&nbsp;to take measures necessary to protect public health, including food safety and hygiene&nbsp;measures.&nbsp;However,&nbsp;Australia&nbsp;remains concerned&nbsp;that&nbsp;China&rsquo;s&nbsp;Regulation on Registration and Administration of Overseas Manufacturers of Imported Food&nbsp;(G/TBT/N/CHN/1522), promulgated as Decree 248 on 12 April 2021&nbsp;will&nbsp;unnecessarily&nbsp;disrupt and&nbsp;restrict trade.&nbsp;Australia is also concerned that WTO Members and businesses do not have sufficient time or&nbsp;information&nbsp;to prepare for an anticipated entry into force on 1 January 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Further, timeframes requested&nbsp;by China&nbsp;to respond&nbsp;to requirements&nbsp;are&nbsp;unreasonably short. For example, the&nbsp;most&nbsp;recent&nbsp;request received&nbsp;from&nbsp;China&nbsp;to provide lists of recommended food facilities for registration gave many trading partners less than a month&rsquo;s notice for to respond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia again requests that&nbsp;China&nbsp;notify the&nbsp;implementation guidance for&nbsp;the regulation&nbsp;under the SPS Agreement,&nbsp;outlining any&nbsp;SPS measures&nbsp;contained in the regulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia is concerned that aspects of the proposed Regulation are more trade restrictive than necessary to fulfill China&rsquo;s food safety&nbsp;objectives&nbsp;and are inconsistent with China&rsquo;s SPS Agreement obligations. Moreover, such a blanket approach to applying measures, considering food safety risk,&nbsp;does not follow Codex standards and guidelines.&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;also&nbsp;requests&nbsp;that&nbsp;China&nbsp;provide details of the risk analysis, scientific data and technical information used to develop this regulation, and&nbsp;explain&nbsp;how&nbsp;the measures&nbsp;are&nbsp;proportionate and necessary for managing the actual risk of&nbsp;different categories of&nbsp;imported foods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australian food exporters are ready and willing to comply with any reasonable food safety requirements, but businesses and governments need clarity and time to plan and make changes to comply with new measures.&nbsp;Australia also believes the proposed Regulation would increase regulatory and administrative burden&nbsp;on&nbsp;competent authorities, lead to administrative delays and unnecessarily restrict trade.&nbsp;There are already significant delays associated with listing of food facilities,&nbsp;and Australia is concerned about the potential for increased delays under this new regulation&nbsp;which may be exacerbated by the Regulation. However, further trade disruptions could be avoided depending upon how these requirements are implemented.&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;appreciated the recent&nbsp;implementation guidance provided by China, however there are a number of outstating issues raised by Australia that have not been addressed by China.&nbsp;There is an urgent need for clarity on:&nbsp;&nbsp;

Relevant&nbsp;HS Codes&nbsp;for specific&nbsp;foods covered&nbsp;by Article 7&nbsp;and Article 9;&nbsp;&nbsp;

Timeframes and process&nbsp;on&nbsp;the approval of applications&nbsp;for registration&nbsp;received after 1 November 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

the&nbsp;acceptance of competent authority&nbsp;issued registration numbers&nbsp;and/or&nbsp;issuance of registration numbers,&nbsp;and&nbsp;&nbsp;

transitional&nbsp;arrangements&nbsp;for&nbsp;labelling of&nbsp;products&nbsp;manufactured&nbsp;prior to&nbsp;any revised&nbsp;implementation date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Understanding that this regulation&nbsp;will&nbsp;come into effect on&nbsp;1 January 2022,&nbsp;Australia&nbsp;requests&nbsp;that&nbsp;China delay&nbsp;implementation&nbsp;to&nbsp;allow time for&nbsp;businesses to prepare and&nbsp;administrative processes to occur.&nbsp;Consistent with SPS Committee Transparency guidelines,&nbsp;Australia requests&nbsp;that Members have&nbsp;a transition period. Australia requests&nbsp;at least&nbsp;18 months&nbsp;(until&nbsp;at least&nbsp;1 July 2023&nbsp;date)&nbsp;for this&nbsp;transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

It is important&nbsp;to give&nbsp;industries&nbsp;time to amend&nbsp;food labels to meet&nbsp;the&nbsp;new labelling requirements, noting that many of the products due to be exported in early 2022 have already been produced.&nbsp;Australia will also be making a statement in next week&rsquo;s TBT Committee meeting regarding China&rsquo;s imported food measures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;would welcome&nbsp;the&nbsp;opportunity to engage with&nbsp;China to&nbsp;discuss how&nbsp;we can meet&nbsp;China&rsquo;s&nbsp;objectives,&nbsp;while&nbsp;ensuring that trade is not unnecessarily disrupted.&nbsp;

	Thank you, Chair.



	Canada joins other Members in once again raising significant concerns and challenges with China&#39;s administrative measures for the registration of overseas manufacturers of imported food. Canada is concerned that the Decrees will negatively impact trade.



	These measures are broad and over-arching in scope, and will have a significant impact on exports to China.&nbsp;



	Over the years, Canada and China have implemented many bilateral arrangements that have successfully reinforced and provided food safety assurances and protections for consumers in our respective nations.



	As such, Canada remains concerned that the administrative measures being implemented by China are overly burdensome and unjustified as they do not appear to be based on an assessment of risks using the risk assessment techniques developed by international organizations and go beyond the extent necessary to protect against food safety risks.



	It remains unclear how the Decrees will contribute to achieving China&rsquo;s appropriate level of protection and, if implemented in their current form, the Decrees will likely create serious barriers to trade including significant financial impacts on both industry and foreign competent authorities.



	Canada is disappointed that the Decrees were adopted and published by the General Administration of Customs of the People&#39;s Republic of China (Customs China) immediately following the deadline for comments by WTO members without sufficiently taking into account comments and concerns from Members.



	Although China responded to Canada&rsquo;s comments on the draft measures in May 2021, the information provided does not provide sufficient clarity for Canada to fully understand how to comply with these measures.



	This information is necessary to ensure that industry stakeholders and foreign competent authorities understand and are able to comply with the requirements.



	Canada remains deeply concerned that these measures will create confusion for competent authorities and industry due to the lack of details and transparency from China regarding the implementation of these new requirements.



	Canada urges China to delay the implementation of Decrees 248 and 249 for 18 months to allow both foreign governments and industry to fully understand and comply with the new requirements contained in the Decrees.&nbsp;



	During the proposed delay, Canada calls on China to provide further clarity and hold discussions with Members in a timely manner so there is sufficient time for trading partners, including both competent authorities and industry, to comply with China&rsquo;s new measures.


&nbsp;


	Canada welcomed the additional information and guidance provided by China in early October related to Custom China&rsquo;s request to have some Canadian companies register by October 31, 2021.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Although Canada did comply with China&rsquo;s October 31, 2021 deadline, the purpose of the request remains unclear, creating additional uncertainty within Canadian industry.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	In conclusion, Canada urges China to provide Members with additional information and clarification on the implementation of Decrees 248 and 249 and delay implementation of these requirements to provide sufficient time to address this uncertainly and allow trading partners to comply with the Decrees.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Thank you, Chair.

CHINA - Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods and Administrative Measures on Import and Export Food Safety&nbsp; 

(G/TBT/N/CHN/1522) (G/SPS/CHN/1191) -&nbsp; SPS STC 485

November 2021 WTO SPS Committee - Statement by United States


	The United States remains concerned about the China&rsquo;s Decrees 248 and 249, previously notified respectively to the TBT and SPS Committees as Administrative Measures for Registration of Overseas Producers of Imported Foods &ndash; TBT/CHN/1522 (Administrative Registration Measures) and Administrative Measures on Import and Export Food Safety &ndash; SPS/CHN/1191 (Administrative Import Measures).&nbsp;



	These decrees mandate additional certification, audit, and inspection documents and procedures beyond what is already determined necessary for higher-risk products. &nbsp;



	This will likely create major trade disruptions for many countries that export food and agricultural products to China, and negatively affect Chinese consumers&rsquo; access to a broad range of imported food products.



	We also remain concerned that China has yet to respond to previous requests for scientific justification and clarification to help inform Members how to implement these measures.



	To that end, the implementation date of January 1, 2022 is not feasible and we request a postponement of at least 18 months before China implements these Decrees.&nbsp; During this time we request that China communicates with foreign food manufacturers and relevant foreign government regulators about the scope and application of the new Decrees.&nbsp; We are also seeking a commitment that trade will not be interrupted during this time.

Also the EU must express concern about the implementation of Decree 248 of the Chinese Customs Administration.

The EU does not in the least question the wish of China to ensure that imported food products come from legitimate sources. We share and support this objective. But the EU is gravely concerned about the manner how China is implementing the planned registration of exporting businesses.

China and the EU trade high volumes of food products and beverages through tens of thousands enterprises, some big, many small. For an effective registration of businesses, clear guidance, standardised template forms and realistic transition periods are indispensable in order to minimise disruptions to economic relationships between enterprises on both sides.

However, instead of offering information events, webinars or at least detailed implementing rules and a web site with frequently asked questions, China sent a letter to EU Member States and other trade partners with the request to identify and register exporting businesses for 14 unclearly defined product categories within five weeks.

This deadline is impossible to meet given that &nbsp;it covers tens of thousands of businesses. As products due to be imported into China early next year have already been produced, if existing timeframes are maintained, Decrees 248 and 249 risk disrupting global food supply chains and delaying food supply into China.

The EU urges China to


	postpone the implementation of the Decree 248 until detailed guidelines, template forms and functioning web sites are available;
	clearly define the product categories by their HS numbers that must register under the &#39;registration with recommendation&#39; procedure under Article 8 of Decree 248; and to clarify the specific actions requested of foreign competent authorities, in particular in the &ldquo;Declaration of Conformity.&rdquo;
	provide for realistic and practicable transition periods of at least 18 months for the self-registration of businesses under Article 9 of Decree 248. Trade with businesses not yet registered should continue without disruption;
	Once registrations are approved and registration numbers are allocated, provisions related to labelling under Article 15 must be implemented with adequate transition periods. In particular products with a long shelf life, such as spirits, may be in retail stocks for many months and must be protected by transition periods of at least 36 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Organise information sessions both for trading partners and busines so that we can have a better understanding of the new registation requirements.

China&rsquo;s COVID-19-Related Trade Actions against Food and Agricultural Products (STC 487)

March 2021 WTO SPS Committee - United States Intervention&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The United States understands the concerns of consumers around the world who want food producers, processors, and regulators to act to reduce the spread of COVID-19.&nbsp; We all must work together to clearly and effectively communicate to consumers the risk of COVID-19 transmission associated with food and agricultural products. &nbsp;

We are concerned by several measures China continues to implement, including requirements for exporter statements, suspension of imports from facilities where local media report worker cases of COVID-19, testing requirements for imported foods, and port-of-entry rejections of imported products when positive nucleic acid test results are reported.&nbsp;&nbsp;

The United States and several other Members shared their concerns about the China&rsquo;s measures during the November 2020 SPS Committee meeting and requested China to withdraw these trade disruptive and non-science based restrictions. Through notification G/SPS/N/CHN/1173, China indicated that it is implementing its measures on an emergency basis. To maintain this measure, China must seek to obtain enough evidence to do so.&nbsp;

China has not provided any science-based justification or testing results to support the need for, or efficacy of, these measures. After more than a year since the COVID-19 outbreak was declared an international public health emergency, the U.S. Department of Agriculture, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration and the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention continue to underscore that there is no credible evidence of food or food packaging associated with, or as a source of, viral transmission of severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), the virus causing COVID-19.

It is particularly important to note that COVID-19 is a respiratory illness that is spread from person to person, unlike foodborne or gastrointestinal viruses that often make people ill through contaminated food. While there are relatively few reports of the virus being detected on food and packaging, most studies focus primarily on the detection of the virus&rsquo; genetic fingerprint rather than evidence of transmission of virus resulting in human infection.

Our understanding, that the risk is exceedingly low for transmission of SARS-CoV-2 to humans via food and food packaging, is based upon the best available information from scientific bodies across the globe, including a continued international consensus.

For example, a recent opinion from the International Commission on Microbiological Specifications for Foods (ICMSF), stated: &ldquo;Despite the billions of meals and food packages handled since the beginning of the COVID-19 pandemic, to date there has not been any evidence that food, food packaging or food handling is a source or important transmission route for SARS-CoV-2 resulting in COVID-19.&rdquo;

Members are confronted by an increasing number of COVID-19-related import restrictions imposed by China on a variety of food and agricultural commodities.&nbsp; &nbsp;Unjustified trade restrictions adopted during the COVID-19 pandemic threaten the integrity of global food supply chains at a time when many WTO members are already struggling to maintain food security.

As Members of the WTO, we encourage China to withdraw these measures and work with its global partners to support the guidance of international organizations by building the body of scientific evidence on COVID-19. &nbsp;Collaborative engagement is essential as we collectively combat the pandemic, avoid unnecessary barriers to trade on food and agricultural products, and maintain food security for all.&nbsp;

	Thank you Chair.


&nbsp;


	As we stated at our last meeting, WTO Members are all trying to meet the challenges that COVID-19 poses to the health of our citizens and our economies, to avoid unnecessary barriers to trade and to contribute to food security.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	It remains crucial, now more so than ever, that we work together to meet these challenges and share the latest science on COVID-19 and its transmission.


&nbsp;


	It is also paramount that we base the measures we take to control COVID-19 on sound scientific principles and an assessment of risks, and that we do not sustain those measures without sufficient scientific evidence.


&nbsp;


	In this context, Canada continues to seek information from China regarding the scientific basis for its recent measures relating to COVID-19 that are affecting trade in food and agricultural products as notified in G/SPS/N/CHN/1173, dated September 21, 2020.


&nbsp;


	Canada continues to base its SPS measures relating to COVID-19 on the April 7, 2020, World Health Organization and FAO document entitled COVID-19 and Food Safety: Guidance for Food Businesses.


&nbsp;


	Canada notes that the current scientific evidence available on COVID-19 indicates that food, food packaging or food handling is not a transmission route. COVID-19 is a respiratory illness.


&nbsp;


	This was borne out by the September 3, 2020, International Commission on Microbiological Specifications for Foods or ICMSF opinion on COVID-19 and its relationship to food safety.


&nbsp;


	Accordingly, if China has scientific evidence that suggests there is transmission of COVID-19 through, food, food packaging or food handling, Canada again requests that this evidence, including testing methodology and data, be shared with WTO Members.


&nbsp;


	Canada took very careful note of China&rsquo;s response at the November 2020 SPS Committee, and was particularly pleased by China&rsquo;s concluding remarks that cooperation needs to be strengthened among all Members.


&nbsp;


	Canada notes that our technical experts continue to be in contact with Chinese counterparts on this matter.


&nbsp;


	Canada again encourages China to maintain this technical dialogue and to work with Canada to reinstate suspended meat establishments at the earliest opportunity.


&nbsp;


	In this regard, Canada welcomes the opportunity for continued collaboration with China &ndash; both bilaterally and multilaterally &ndash; towards our common objective of avoiding unnecessary barriers to trade and contributing to food security.


&nbsp;


	Thank you Chair.

