CHINA- COVID-19 NUCLEIC MATERIAL ON SEAFOOD PACKAGING &nbsp;

&nbsp;

CONCERN:

&nbsp;

China has been suspending the export approval of Indian units citing detection of Covid-19 nucleic acid from the packaging of the seafood consignments shipped by Indian units. China has not yet shared any test reports or the test procedures adopted for testing the presence of Covid-19 nucleic material. This measure of China is affecting our exports of seafood. Until now, there is no definitive evidence that Covid-19 is transmitted through food and food packaging and no confirmed cases of anyone contracting Covid-19 from food packaging.

&nbsp;

The above measure of China is prima facie inconsistent with Articles 2.2 and 5.1 of the SPS Agreement. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

As of date, there is no evidence of Covid-19 being transmitted via food or food packaging. Given this, India requests China to share the test reports for the consignment on which Covid-19 nucleic material is being found and to establish how the measure at issue is necessary to protect human, animal or plant life or health and based on scientific evidence.

&nbsp;

Additionally, as per the Covid-19 and food safety guidance for food businesses published by WHO on 7th April 2020, which is being referred by the General Administration of Customs of the People&rsquo;s Republic of China (GACC) in its communications, &nbsp;&ndash; &ldquo;It is highly unlikely that people can contract from food packaging. Covid-19 is a respiratory illness, and the primary transmission route is through person-person contact and through direct contact with respiratory droplets generated when an infected person coughs or sneezes. There is no evidence to date viruses that cause respiratory illness being transmitted via food or food packaging. Coronavirus cannot multiply through food and food packaging; they need an animal or human host to multiply.&rdquo; &nbsp;Given this, India seeks from China scientific evidence and studies to demonstrate the appropriateness of its measures, i.e. suspending the export approval of Indian units citing detection of Covid-19 nucleic material from the packaging of the seafood consignments.

&nbsp;

China is also requested to demonstrate how these measures are consistent with Articles 2.2 and 5.1 of the SPS Agreement.

&nbsp;

Australia wishes to reiterate&nbsp;our&nbsp;requests&nbsp;for&nbsp;China&nbsp;to&nbsp;provide the Committee with an update on its steps to obtain additional information and review the measures&nbsp;for food businesses related to COVID-19 such as those&nbsp;outlined in GACC Announcement No. 103 of 2020, as required under the SPS Agreement.&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;recognises the right&nbsp;of&nbsp;Members&nbsp;to take provisional measures necessary to protect public health, including&nbsp;to prevent the introduction of pathogens injurious to human health.&nbsp;Members&nbsp;are&nbsp;also&nbsp;obliged to&nbsp;base&nbsp;provisional&nbsp;measures on&nbsp;available pertinent information, to&nbsp;seek&nbsp;the additional information necessary for a more objective assessment of risk and&nbsp;to&nbsp;review the&nbsp;measures&nbsp;within a reasonable period of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia requests that China update the Committee on its steps to obtain additional information and review&nbsp;the emergency COVID-19&nbsp;measures notified in&nbsp;SPS notification G/SPS/N/CHN/1173 and&nbsp;outlined in GACC Announcement No. 103 of 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia would appreciate if&nbsp;China&nbsp;would&nbsp;advise the Committee how&nbsp;it&nbsp;will take into account&nbsp;the revised &lsquo;COVID-19 and Food Safety: guidance for food businesses&rsquo;&nbsp;FAO/WHO guidance,&nbsp;which states&nbsp;that it &ldquo;is highly unlikely that people can contract COVID-19 from food or food packaging&rdquo; and that &ldquo;there is no evidence to date of viruses that cause respiratory illnesses being transmitted via food or food packaging. Coronaviruses cannot multiply in food; they need an animal or human host to multiply.&rdquo;&nbsp;Australia&rsquo;s firm belief is&nbsp;that China&rsquo;s&nbsp;current approach&nbsp;of continuing to enforce these suspensions is&nbsp;contrary to&nbsp;the FAO advice and other international guidance.&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;also&nbsp;requests that China submit an SPS notification for its&nbsp;additional&nbsp;measures&nbsp;to prevent&nbsp;COVID-19&nbsp;that have not yet been notified to the WTO,&nbsp;including&nbsp;requirements for:&nbsp;



	widespread testing&nbsp;and disinfection&nbsp;of imported products and packaging;&nbsp;


	mandatory commercial declarations&nbsp;and&nbsp;variations to commercial contracts;&nbsp;&nbsp;


	requirements to&nbsp;maintain or regain market access; and&nbsp;





	We note&nbsp;extended&nbsp;suspensions&nbsp;of&nbsp;overseas food manufacturers&nbsp;following the detection of SARS-CoV-2 in onsite workers,&nbsp;with no clear&nbsp;process for lifting such suspensions&nbsp;in a timely manner.&nbsp;



Specifically, Australia formally requests that China provides an update on the stage it is at in its consideration of, and indicative timeframe for, lifting the suspension on Australian export establishments where SARS-CoV-2 was detected in onsite workers in July and September 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

These establishments acted in good faith and pursuant to the GACC&rsquo;s commitment to trading partners that such establishments would have a clear pathway to lifting the suspension and reinstating trade following the resolution of COVID-19-related incidents.&nbsp;&nbsp;Australia has provided comprehensive submissions to China in&nbsp;support of lifting&nbsp;suspensions&nbsp;on Australian export establishments&nbsp;suspended&nbsp;since July&nbsp;and September 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;Australia requests that China&nbsp;provide details of its&nbsp;consideration&nbsp;process and&nbsp;an&nbsp;indicative timeframe for lifting these suspensions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia is concerned that retaining such measures without scientifically based justification will be unnecessarily trade restrictive, particularly in the&nbsp;transition to a new COVID-normal.&nbsp;Once again, Australia would be pleased to work with China and other members to ensure any measures to prevent the spread of COVID-19 are science and risk-based, take account of the latest available pertinent information and minimise any unnecessary impact on trade.

China&rsquo;s COVID-19-Related Trade Actions against Food and Agricultural Products 

(SPS STC 487)

November 2021 WTO SPS Committee -&nbsp; Statement by the United States

&nbsp;


	The United States would like to reiterate the concerns we have shared over the past year regarding several measures China continues to implement, including testing requirements on imported foods and port-of-entry rejections of imported products when positive nucleic acid test results are reported, and suspension of imports from specific facilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	In August 2021, the FAO released updated guidance for preventing transmission of COVID-19 within food businesses. That document states that, &ldquo;current data indicates that neither food nor food packaging is a pathway for the spread of viruses causing respiratory illnesses, including SARS-CoV-2. In other words, SARS-CoV-2 is not a direct food safety concern.&rdquo;


&nbsp;


	Through notification G/SPS/N/CHN/1173, China indicated that it is implementing these measures on an emergency basis. &nbsp;To date and in response to numerous requests by WTO Members, China has not provided any science-based justification or testing results to support the need for, or efficacy of, these measures, and we would remind China that in order to maintain provisional measures, China must seek to obtain enough evidence to justify them.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	As the world continues to slowly reopen and rebuild, the unjustified trade restrictions adopted by China during the COVID-19 pandemic continue to threaten global food supply chains, slow global recovery efforts, and further challenge global food security.
	&nbsp;
	As Members of the WTO, we again urge China to withdraw these measures and work with its global partners to support the guidance of international organizations by building the body of scientific evidence on COVID-19. &nbsp;Collaborative engagement is essential as we collectively seek to combat the pandemic, avoid unnecessary barriers to trade on food and agricultural products, and maintain food security for all.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Lastly, the United States notes that two U.S. poultry production facilities remain ineligible to export to China over a year after China suspended exports allegedly based on COVID-19-related concerns. These facilities have provided substantial information to China to support reinstatement, but China has not provided a clear process for trade to resume.&nbsp; We encourage China to make public this process and promptly reinstate these facilities as eligible to export to China.


&nbsp;



		Thank you, Chair.




&nbsp;




		As we stated at the June and November 2020 WTO SPS Committee meetings, pulses have long been an important element of Canada&rsquo;s bilateral trade relationship with India.




&nbsp;




		Canada therefore remains concerned about the impact of India&rsquo;s trade restrictive measures on pulses. These include India&rsquo;s mandatory fumigation requirements, as well as new measures imposed in October 2019 related to weed seeds.




&nbsp;&nbsp;




		Starting first with the fumigation issue: as previously stated, Canada has been actively engaged with India since 2016 to explore alternatives to India&rsquo;s mandatory import fumigation requirements.




&nbsp;




		Canada has raised this matter on several occasions with India, at every level, including bilaterally on the margins of previous meetings of this Committee and in formal interventions.




&nbsp;




		We note India&rsquo;s commitment at the last SPS Committee meeting to continue engaging with Canada with a view to finding a mutually acceptable solution at the earliest opportunity. We are disappointment by the lack of engagement since and note that India has not yet responded to Canadian overtures made in November 2020.&nbsp;




&nbsp;




		Canada is prepared to work with India to resolve this matter, which is seriously affecting the trade in pulses, and encourages India&rsquo;s continued engagement and timely resolution of the issue.&nbsp;




&nbsp;&nbsp;




		Turing to the weed seeds issue: in October 2019, India published amendments to its List of Quarantine Weed Seeds, adding 26 new weed seed species.




&nbsp;




		Canada is concerned with the impact of India&rsquo;s expanded regulation of weed seeds on trade in pulses, and has conveyed to India our view that these actions are inconsistent with the principles of transparent and predicable international rules-based trade.




&nbsp;




		Canada questions India&rsquo;s technical justification for the continued regulation of these weed species, as it appears that India did not follow IPPC guidance and put its measures in place before completing all necessary IPPC steps.




&nbsp;




		Canada is troubled by the lack of transparency, predictability and scientific basis for these measures, as well as non-conformity with relevant international standards.




&nbsp;




		These concerns remain despite the two sides having agreed to continue technical discussions, most recently through India&rsquo;s commitment at the November SPS Committee meeting.




&nbsp;




		Canada urges India&rsquo;s further bilateral technical engagement with the aim of an early resolution to these outstanding issues.




&nbsp;




		Thank you, Chair.




&nbsp;

India &ndash; &ldquo;Non-GM/GM free&rdquo; certificate requirement for imported food

July 2021 WTO SPS Committee Meeting


	The United States again raises its concerns with India&rsquo;s measure mandating &ldquo;non-GM (genetically modified) origin and GM free certificates&rdquo; for certain agricultural imports into India, notified on September 2, 2020, as G/TBT/N/IND/168.


&nbsp;


	The United States raised this issue at the November 2020 and March 2021 SPS Committee meetings and our concerns are documented in G/SPS/GEN/1865 and G/SPS/GEN/1901, and in the Committee&rsquo;s summary reports of the previous meetings.


&nbsp;


	Per previous interventions on this issue, we again request that India provide the scientific justification and the risk assessment for the measure, or otherwise indicate the relevant international standards or guidelines on which this measure is based.&nbsp; In the absence of this information, the United States again urges India to withdraw its measure.


&nbsp;


	The measure has disrupted U.S. apple exports.&nbsp; Trade stoppages risk future product shortages and could lead to price increases for Indian consumers and further disruptions to Indian supply chains.


&nbsp;


	We request that India consider alternative approaches that can be implemented in a non-discriminatory manner, and in a way that is not more trade-restrictive than necessary.


&nbsp;


	In January 2021, the U.S. proposed technical cooperation with India to develop alternatives to the non-GM origin and GM-free certificate. &nbsp;We look forward to the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India&rsquo;s (FSSAI) response to this proposed technical cooperation opportunity as a means by which we can develop a mutually agreeable approach to this issue.

While Australia respects the right of Members to take measures necessary to ensure&nbsp;food safety,&nbsp;Australia&nbsp;continues to have&nbsp;concerns about the EU&rsquo;s new rules for shelf-stable composite products under EU Regulations 2019/625 and 2020/2235.&nbsp;&nbsp;These new rules are not commensurate with risk as they&nbsp;unnecessarily restrict trade&nbsp;by&nbsp;applying stringent&nbsp;measures&nbsp;to low food safety risk foods. Australia has already seen that these new rules on composite products have restricted&nbsp;or ceased&nbsp;trade in shelf-stable composite products&nbsp;such as&nbsp;biscuits containing low percentage of egg ingredients,&nbsp;and there has been a detrimental impact on&nbsp;Australian&nbsp;businesses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia maintains that the sourcing of animal-origin ingredients from EU-listed establishments for all composite products, irrespective of the percentage of animal ingredient in the product, is not justified as the&nbsp;consumer exposure to contaminants would be negligible and&nbsp;this measure&nbsp;will not lead to any improvements in safety for EU consumers.&nbsp;

Should all countries adopt this same approach in requiring all exporting countries to replicate systems that are to be approved by the importing country, trade will effectively slow, increasing the costs of food production and potentially affecting food security.&nbsp;

The private attestation requirement&nbsp;places&nbsp;an onerous obligation on businesses which adds transaction costs for no additional food safety benefit. We consider the private attestation is unnecessary and&nbsp;should be dropped from the rules with no impact on food safety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Further, as set out in Articles 4 and 5 of the SPS Agreement, Australia&nbsp;again&nbsp;requests that the EU recognise the equivalence of third countries&rsquo; systems,&nbsp;where those countries already implement a sufficient level of sanitary regulation for low-risk shelf-stable composite products, and that the sanitary measures are commensurate with the level of risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

In summary, Australia&nbsp;again&nbsp;requests the EU&rsquo;s reconsideration of&nbsp;how it implements the&nbsp;measures in&nbsp;this regulation, including the product coverage and thresholds. Australia requests that animal ingredients that make up a&nbsp;low percentage&nbsp;of a composite product not be subject to the regulation.&nbsp;We also request the EU to&nbsp;formally&nbsp;recognise Australia&rsquo;s overall high-quality food safety system as providing an equivalent level of sanitary protection to the level of protection provided within the EU itself.&nbsp;Can the EU please advise on the process and information it requires to consider&nbsp;alternative measures to achieve an equivalent&nbsp;outcome?&nbsp;

In 2013, Mexico suspended the imports of shrimp products from China on the ground of preventing the introduction of the Acute Hepatopancreatic Necrosis disease?AHPND?. The Chinese side expressed STC at the 79th&nbsp;meeting. Mexico responded that it is also willing to consider the measures recommended by OIE, to reach a trade facilitation plan to eliminate risks, and in May 2021 provided us&nbsp;with sanitary requirements for imported frozen shrimp from China. China shows&nbsp;gratitude for Mexico&rsquo;s active cooperation.

Chinese experts&nbsp;conducted research on the sanitary requirements for imported Chinese frozen shrimp. Articles 3, 4 and 5 set strict restrictions on the destination, use, port of entry, sanitary certificates of imported Chinese shrimp, while Article 2 requires compliance with Article 9.1.12 of the OIE Aquatic Animal Health Code. However, it should be pointed out that there are obviously contradictions in Mexico&rsquo;s sanitary requirements, because Article 9.1.12 of the OIE Aquatic Animal Health Code states that,&nbsp;&ldquo;no matter whether there is AHPND, as long as the frozen shrimp is decapsulated, and prepared and packaged complied with Article 5.4.2, import country should not add any conditions related to AHPND.&rdquo;&nbsp;But Mexico&rsquo;s sanitary requirements set restrictions on imported frozen shrimp according to this Article.&nbsp;China believes Articles 3, 4, and 5 of Mexico&rsquo;s sanitary requirements obviously set an excessively high level of protection for the prevention and control of this disease.

The OIE Aquatic Animal Health Code has specified that AHPND virus will not be spread by frozen shrimp products. China considers Mexico&rsquo;s suspension of imports of shrimp products from China on the grounds of preventing the introduction of AHPND to be inconsistent with the SPS Agreement and the GATT 1994.

China and Mexico are important trading partners of aquatic products. China hopes that Mexico will strictly comply with relevant regulations and standards based on scientific and objective principles, carefully study and scientifically formulate the import requirements for Chinese frozen shrimp, and resume imports frozen shrimp products from China as soon as possible.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico presenta nuevamente esta preocupaci&oacute;n debido a la demora en las gestiones que se llevan a cabo ante la Autoridad Paname&ntilde;a de Seguridad de Alimentos (AUPSA), con relaci&oacute;n a la renovaci&oacute;n de autorizaciones a establecimientos mexicanos exportadores de productos y subproductos de la especie bovina.

&nbsp;

El extra&ntilde;amiento que nuestra delegaci&oacute;n presenta, obedece a demoras en la atenci&oacute;n de comunicaciones por parte de la autoridad de Panam&aacute;, lo cual ha impactado en el vencimiento de autorizaciones de establecimientos que hab&iacute;an estado exportando sin problema alguno.

&nbsp;

M&eacute;xico solicit&oacute; el procedimiento a seguir para la renovaci&oacute;n de las autorizaciones, con tiempo suficiente previo a su vencimiento e incluso despu&eacute;s del vencimiento se solicit&oacute; la atenci&oacute;n de renovaci&oacute;n a establecimientos cuya autorizaci&oacute;n ya hab&iacute;a vencido.

&nbsp;

Posteriormente, en febrero de 2021, se llev&oacute; a cabo la Subcomisi&oacute;n de Asuntos Econ&oacute;micos, Comerciales y Financieros, en el marco de la VI Reuni&oacute;n de la Comisi&oacute;n Binacional M&eacute;xico- Panam&aacute;, en la que se solicit&oacute; expresamente a Panam&aacute; dar respuesta a las gestiones pendientes para la renovaci&oacute;n de autorizaciones de establecimientos Tipo Inspecci&oacute;n Federal (TIF) y tambi&eacute;n se solicit&oacute; reciprocidad para la emisi&oacute;n de autorizaciones. No obstante, la autoridad de Panam&aacute; indic&oacute; que, para renovar las autorizaciones, requiere desarrollar un procedimiento para la elegibilidad de los pa&iacute;ses que desean exportar dichos productos a Panam&aacute;, adem&aacute;s de la evaluaci&oacute;n de los servicios veterinarios de M&eacute;xico, siendo que ya exist&iacute;a un comercio abierto y no se present&oacute; alg&uacute;n cambio en el estatus sanitario de M&eacute;xico.

&nbsp;

En la perspectiva de M&eacute;xico, las autoridades paname&ntilde;as han mostrado demoras indebidas en las gestiones, ya que, aun considerando las condiciones generadas por la actual pandemia, M&eacute;xico ha otorgado pr&oacute;rrogas a establecimientos paname&ntilde;os que exportan a M&eacute;xico.

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico desea hacer referencia a los derechos y obligaciones de los Miembros ante el Acuerdo de Medidas Sanitarias y Fitosanitarias de esta Organizaci&oacute;n, particularmente a los principios de basar las medidas en sustento cient&iacute;fico y en una evaluaci&oacute;n adecuada a las circunstancias de los riesgos existentes para la vida y la salud de las personas y de los animales, as&iacute; como al Anexo C de dicho Acuerdo, en lo que se refiere a la obligaci&oacute;n de que los procedimientos para verificar y asegurar el cumplimiento de MSF se inicien y ultimen sin demoras indebidas.

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de M&eacute;xico agradece a la delegaci&oacute;n de Panam&aacute; por la atenci&oacute;n a esta declaraci&oacute;n y reitera su disposici&oacute;n de mantener una comunicaci&oacute;n abierta entre autoridades, esperando que en la pr&oacute;xima reuni&oacute;n del Comit&eacute; MSF del Tratado de Libre Comercio entre M&eacute;xico y Panam&aacute; a celebrarse el pr&oacute;ximo 21 de julio, se pueda llegar a una soluci&oacute;n mutuamente favorable, considerando los derechos y obligaciones internacionales en la materia.

	Australia recognises the right of governments to take appropriate sanitary and phytosanitary measures consistent with their WTO obligations under the SPS Agreement. However, Australia remains concerned with the long delays and lack of transparency in China&rsquo;s approval and administrative update process, as well as a lack of risk and/or evidence basis in China&rsquo;s approach which prevent trade from Australia.
	&nbsp;
	Australia has longstanding requests for Chinese government approval of food export establishments and/or products which have not progressed. This has led to Australian products receiving less favourable treatment than China&rsquo;s domestic goods and establishments, and those of other trading partners.
	&nbsp;
	Australia requests that China extend the same opportunities to all WTO Members, consistent with China&rsquo;s WTO obligations under Article 2.3 of the SPS Agreement. We also request that China ensure its SPS measures are science-based, proportionate to the risk and are not more trade restrictive than necessary
	&nbsp;
	Australia is still waiting for China to honour its commitments in approving establishment registrations and updating administrative listings changes. For example:
	&nbsp;

		Some Australian meat establishments have been waiting for over a decade for listing approval. Despite repeated requests, GACC continues to not engage with Australia on these listing approvals.
		Following a successful audit by Chinese regulators, in March 2017, China agreed to list 15 additional Australian red meat establishments and grant expanded access for chilled meat and tripe. China have not actioned this commitment.
		Administrative updates to the approved Australian meat establishment list are also outstanding. These have included requests to correct minor typological and administrative errors in which Australia has provided supporting evidence to show an error has been made.&nbsp; Without correction of even minor errors, many of these establishment are unable to trade.&nbsp; &nbsp;




&nbsp;


	Australia has also faced significant delays in China accepting and publishing our product listing requests, and requests for renewal of registrations prior to expiry. For example, Chinese authorities have not provided clarity regarding the process, timelines for approval or additional information requirements for a number of products:
	&nbsp;

		Australia has faced delays of up to two years for Australian infant formula brand registrations despite Australian industry providing comprehensive submissions as requested by China. Chinese regulators have not provided transparent processes or timelines for approval.
		Australia has faced delays for over four years for new approvals of Australian seafood species for export to China, despite providing comprehensive submissions and Australia&rsquo;s repeated follow up with Chinese regulators. China has not provided clarity on the process, timeframes for approval or additional information requirements. &nbsp;
		Australia has repeatedly requested that China update lists for Australian horticulture orchards, packhouses and treatment facilities over the last 9 months for export to China under agreed arrangements. Despite having provided China all the required information, this straightforward administrative process has faced protracted delays without any justification or engagement from China, and
		Despite advising China over one year ago of 25 export hay establishments due for registration renewal by February 2021, Australia has not yet received a response.
		&nbsp;


	While Australia remains committed to ensuring Australian exports comply with China&rsquo;s importing country requirements, we would also note that sanctions applied for a detected non-compliance should be proportionate to the food safety or biosecurity risk posed by that non-compliance. Further, where non-compliances have been addressed by Australia, China should engage in good faith to ensure the necessary steps are taken to have these suspensions lifted in a transparent, timely and non-discriminatory way.&nbsp; For example:
	&nbsp;

		A number of Australian meat establishments remain suspended for minor labelling non-compliances.&nbsp; Despite Australia following China&rsquo;s processes for suspension removal, thoroughly addressing the non-compliances through the implementation of corrective actions and meeting China&rsquo;s strict reporting timeframes, these establishments remain suspended.
		&nbsp;


	Australia reminds China that as required in Annex C of the SPS Agreement Members must ensure that any procedures to check and ensure the fulfilment of SPS measures are undertaken and completed without undue delay, and to keep an applicant informed as it undertakes its review processes.
	&nbsp;
	Australia urges China to apply consistent criteria and transparent timeframes on a non-discriminatory basis for approval procedures, including the procedures for assessment and approval of commodity registrations, establishment listings and lifting restrictions on suspended establishments.
	&nbsp;
	At the last SPS Committee in March 2021 China undertook to forward our concerns to its competent authorities and we would welcome any feedback China can provide from those authorities. Since raising these concerns, we have seen no improvement to these measures and Australian exports continue to be unnecessarily impacted.
	&nbsp;
	As we have previously noted, Australia would welcome bilateral engagement with China to discuss and constructively work to resolve these concerns.


&nbsp;

	Thank you, Chair.


&nbsp;


	Canada and other Members continue to experience significant undue delays in China&rsquo;s approval procedures for the import of food products and of foreign establishments seeking to export to China.


&nbsp;


	Canada continues to wait for China to update over ten separate lists of approved Canadian products and facilities eligible to export to China, including:


&nbsp;


	Canadian companies exporting grains and oilseeds, which has not been updated since March 2019;


&nbsp;


	Canadian cherry and blueberries exporters, which has not been&nbsp; updated since 2020; and


&nbsp;


	Canadian fish and seafood facilities and approved species, which has not been updated since 2018.


&nbsp;


	There are also a number of Canadian meat and dairy establishments that are still waiting for approval from China to be added to the list of establishments eligible to export.


&nbsp;


	Some of these establishments submitted their applications to China in 2017 following audits conducted by China and have yet to receive a response.


&nbsp;


	The excessive delays, lack of transparency, and the failure to provide a rationale of approval procedures for foreign export establishments results in significant uncertainty and disruption for Canadian exports.


&nbsp;


	These are unjustified administrative burdens for Canadian industry.


&nbsp;


	Canadian establishments recommended by the Canadian Food Inspection Agency to export to China comply with Canada&#39;s food safety requirements in accordance with Canadian legislation such as the Safe Food for Canadians Act and Regulations and the Health of Animals Act and Regulations as well as China&rsquo;s import requirements.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada recalls that Annex C of the Agreement on the Application of Sanitary and Phytosanitary Measures states that:&nbsp;


&nbsp;

Members shall ensure&hellip; that [approval] procedures are undertaken and completed without undue delay and in no less favourable manner for imported products than for like domestic products.

&nbsp;


	Canada urges China to finalize and publish the many lists of Canadian products and establishments that continue to await registration to the Chinese market without any further undue delay.


&nbsp;


	Furthermore, Canada recalls that Annex C states that:


&nbsp;

Members shall ensure&hellip; that the standard processing period of each procedure is published or that the anticipated processing period is communicated to the applicant upon request...&nbsp;&nbsp; 

&nbsp;


	Canada has repeatedly requested information related to the eligibility and registration of Canadian establishments and of food and food products. China has not provided defined timelines.


&nbsp;


	Canada urges China to respect its obligations under the WTO SPS Agreement and provide timelines for the acceptance of all Canadian food and food products awaiting &lsquo;approval&rsquo; by China.


&nbsp;


	Canada also urges China to transmit the result of the approval procedures as soon as possible in a precise and complete manner.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	In accordance with Annex C of the SPS Agreement, China must communicate any deficiencies in applications in a precise and complete manner; process applications as far as practicable when requested; and provide information on the stage of the approval and an explanation of any delays.


&nbsp;


	Canada urges China to immediately provide the reason why Canadian products or establishments have not been approved and provide a clear explanation of the undue delays.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Moreover, Canada recalls that Annex C of the SPS Agreement states that:


&nbsp;

Members shall ensure&hellip; that information requirements are limited to what is necessary for appropriate control, inspection and approval procedures.

&nbsp;


	Canada urges China to limit information requirements to what is necessary and ensure that their approval procedures are transparent and predictable.


&nbsp;


	Canada considers some requirements requested by China to be restrictive and not required to undertake approval procedures necessary for China&rsquo;s appropriate level of sanitary or phytosanitary protection.


&nbsp;


	Over the years, Canada and China have implemented many bilateral arrangements that have successfully provided food safety assurances for consumers.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Canada is ready to continue supplying safe, high-quality products to Chinese consumers.


&nbsp;


	In conclusion, Canada calls on China to implement transparent and predictable approval procedures for foreign export establishments without undue delay to meet our common objective of avoiding unnecessary barriers to trade and ensuring trade is maintained based on science and risk.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	Thank you, Chair.


&nbsp;

Responsive/rebuttal intervention 


	&nbsp;Thank you for the opportunity to respond to several points raised by China, specifically regarding specific food safety issues and bilateral arrangements.


&nbsp;


	All Canadian federally licensed establishments adhere to rigorous, internationally accepted standards and food safety requirements including strong sanitation and hygiene requirements.&nbsp; As previously mentioned, Canada verifies that Canadian facilities comply with Canada&#39;s food safety requirements.


&nbsp;


	If Canada becomes aware of any potential food safety risks, appropriate actions are taken immediately to prevent contaminated foods from entering the domestic or international food supply. Such instances are managed appropriately, separate from issues of the approval of establishments and products at issue here.


&nbsp;


	&nbsp;Further, regarding bilateral arrangements between Canada and China, Canada has provided detailed information to China regarding the strong measures in place in Canada to ensure the safety of the Canadian food supply and food products exported from Canada.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


	China has conducted audits, both in person and virtually, of Canadian establishments and Canada has fulfilled any additional requests, whereas China has not responded in a timely manner to approve and publish the eligibility for exporting establishments, which is central to the concern Canada is raising here.&nbsp;

agricultural&nbsp;and seafood&nbsp;establishments and products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Since first raising these concerns over seven months ago,&nbsp;we have seen no improvement. Australian&nbsp;exports continue to be unnecessarily&nbsp;restricted&nbsp;and&nbsp;we&nbsp;have no&nbsp;clear sense China&rsquo;s processes or timelines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Australia is still waiting for China to honour its commitments in approving establishment registrations and updating&nbsp;administrative&nbsp;listing changes. Some&nbsp;establishments have been waiting over ten years for approval.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Australia has also faced significant delays in China accepting and publishing our product listing requests, and requests for renewal of registrations prior to export across a range of products. Chinese authorities have not provided clarity regarding the process, timelines for approval or additional information required for a number of products.&nbsp;

Australia has provided numerous examples of these issues at previous meetings of this Committee, including:&nbsp;



	Australian meat exporters are still waiting for China to&nbsp;list&nbsp;15 additional meat establishments&nbsp;outstanding since&nbsp;March 2017, and for China to update administrative listing changes and consider applications to reinstate previously suspended establishments.&nbsp;


	Infant formula exporters have been waiting two years for China to consider their comprehensive submissions for brand registration approval.&nbsp;


	Seafood exporters have been waiting over four years for China to consider their comprehensive submissions to list new Australian seafood species for export to China.&nbsp;


	Australian horticulture orchards, packhouses and treatment facilities are still waiting for China to consider their information and update its lists so that they can export.&nbsp;





	Australian hay export establishments are still waiting for China to consider their renewal requests submitted more than a year ago for licenses that expired in February 2021.&nbsp;



&nbsp;

Australia requests that China&nbsp;apply&nbsp;the same&nbsp;process for&nbsp;approval to all WTO Members, consistent with China&rsquo;s WTO obligations under Article 2.3 of the SPS Agreement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;

As required&nbsp;under&nbsp;Annex C of the SPS Agreement,&nbsp;Australia requests that China&nbsp;ensure that&nbsp;any&nbsp;procedures&nbsp;to check and ensure the fulfilment of SPS measures&nbsp;are undertaken&nbsp;and completed&nbsp;without undue delay,&nbsp;and&nbsp;that China keep Australia&nbsp;informed&nbsp;as it undertakes its review processes.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;also requests that China provide&nbsp;full&nbsp;details of its&nbsp;processes and&nbsp;procedures&nbsp;(including indicative timeframes&nbsp;and information requirements)&nbsp;for assessment and approval of&nbsp;products and&nbsp;establishments, updating administrative lists&nbsp;and lifting restrictions on suspended&nbsp;establishments.&nbsp;

As we have previously noted, Australia would welcome bilateral engagement with China to discuss&nbsp;these concerns&nbsp;and constructively work to&nbsp;resolve&nbsp;them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia recognises and&nbsp;respects&nbsp;China&rsquo;s right to&nbsp;take sanitary and phytosanitary measures&nbsp;necessary to protect human, animal or plant life or health, including&nbsp;inspection&nbsp;and&nbsp;import checks at the border, provided they are based on scientific principles and not maintained without sufficient scientific evidence.&nbsp;However,&nbsp;Australia has observed a pattern of&nbsp;increased inspection&nbsp;and testing measures implemented at the border by China.&nbsp;In many cases these measures have been initiated without prior warning or communication from China, do not appear to be based on scientific evidence and have been applied without regard to the risk associated with the product&nbsp;(e.g.&nbsp;testing 100 percent of wine consignments.)&nbsp;&nbsp;

Given the absence of&nbsp;any evidence that these measures have been based on scientific principles&nbsp;and&nbsp;the lack of&nbsp;communication from China about the measures,&nbsp;Australia&nbsp;can only&nbsp;conclude that these increased inspections implemented at the border by China have been applied arbitrarily and are heavy handed.&nbsp;These&nbsp;measures have&nbsp;led to delays&nbsp;and&nbsp;significantly constrained trade across a spread of agricultural commodities, including&nbsp;rock lobster, wine, wheat, barley, oats, canola, logs and table grapes. In&nbsp;some&nbsp;cases&nbsp;this has completely&nbsp;blocked&nbsp;Australian exports to China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

China&rsquo;s recent increases in inspection and testing&nbsp;of Australian agricultural products&nbsp;occurred without prior notice and&nbsp;were too sudden&nbsp;to enable&nbsp;exporters to make necessary holding arrangements particularly important for perishable goods.&nbsp;On&nbsp;several occasions, after having advised&nbsp;Australia of detected non-compliances, China&nbsp;did not respond&nbsp;to Australia&rsquo;s requests&nbsp;for&nbsp;full detail on&nbsp;China&rsquo;s methods and&nbsp;findings, such as&nbsp;the&nbsp;testing regimes&nbsp;and methodologies&nbsp;used by China to detect or determine the non-compliance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Australia has robust biosecurity and food safety&nbsp;systems&nbsp;in place&nbsp;and&nbsp;has&nbsp;sought to address&nbsp;China&rsquo;s concerns through the provision of investigation reports for all alleged non-compliances.&nbsp;Australia&nbsp;communicates&nbsp;with trading partners and works&nbsp;to&nbsp;constructively&nbsp;resolve issues, while minimising any unnecessary impact on trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;China has, however,&nbsp;been unwilling to engage with Australia on&nbsp;these investigation reports or&nbsp;proposed&nbsp;corrective&nbsp;approaches that would provide confidence that our exports&nbsp;do&nbsp;meet&nbsp;China&rsquo;s import&nbsp;requirements, without the need to unnecessarily restrict trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;Australia continues to treat these issues on their technical merits and has sought to address them bilaterally with relevant Chinese authorities. However, many of the technical submissions to China have gone unanswered and we are disappointed with China&rsquo;s lack of response to our efforts to re-instate trade.&nbsp;

Australia&nbsp;again&nbsp;requests that&nbsp;China:&nbsp;



	respond to Australia&rsquo;s requests for information on China&rsquo;s inspection and testing requirements; and&nbsp;&nbsp;


	provide Australia with details of its inspection and testing measures and&nbsp;explain&nbsp;to Australia&nbsp;its reasons for imposing&nbsp;each measure, and&nbsp;


	engage with Australia&nbsp;on&nbsp;our&nbsp;proposals,&nbsp;including our submissions and non-compliance investigation reports&nbsp;to address China&rsquo;s concerns and work constructively to reinstate trade.&nbsp;



Australia would welcome bilateral engagement on these matters by China, including exchanging information between regulators and working collaboratively to resolve problems or misunderstandings so that trade can&nbsp;promptly&nbsp;resume.&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de Panam&aacute; presenta ante los Miembros de la OMC su preocupaci&oacute;n comercial con relaci&oacute;n a las demoras indebidas para la autorizaci&oacute;n de de empresas de productos l&aacute;cteos por parte del Per&uacute;.

&nbsp;

El Anexo C del Acuerdo MSF, en su art&iacute;culo 8, p&aacute;rrafos 1.a) y 1.c) establece que los procedimientos para verificar el cumplimiento de una medida sanitaria que se inicien y ultimen sin demoras indebidas y sin exigir m&aacute;s informaci&oacute;n de la necesaria a efectos de los procedimientos de control, inspecci&oacute;n y aprobaci&oacute;n apropiados, los que se han visto vulnerados para Panam&aacute;.

&nbsp;

Panam&aacute; desea destacar que, a pesar de las notas enviadas a Per&uacute; solicitando los procedimientos y tramites requeridos que se deben cumplir para la aprobaci&oacute;n para exportar y las gestiones bilaterales sostenidas, Per&uacute; no ha comunicado el periodo de tramitaci&oacute;n previsto para la habilitaci&oacute;n de nuevas empresas; contraviniendo el art&iacute;culo 8 y el p&aacute;rrafo 1.b) del Anexo C del Acuerdo MSF, que indica que previa petici&oacute;n los Miembros deben comunicar el periodo previsto de tramitaci&oacute;n de un procedimiento de aprobaci&oacute;n.

&nbsp;

A la fecha, son tres industrias l&aacute;cteas paname&ntilde;as que desean exportar sus productos al Per&uacute; y que mantienen tr&aacute;mites pendientes sin respuesta, pese a que han sido cursadas notas de solicitud a las autoridades competentes de ese pa&iacute;s, que a&uacute;n no han sido respondidas con el consecuente impacto negativo para las exportaciones paname&ntilde;as hacia el mercado peruano.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

La delegaci&oacute;n de Panam&aacute; agradece a la delegaci&oacute;n de Per&uacute; por la atenci&oacute;n a esta preocupaci&oacute;n y reitera su disposici&oacute;n de mantener una comunicaci&oacute;n abierta entre autoridades.
